      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE OPENING OF TENDERS.</p>
        <p>Lively Discussion.</p>
        <p>So many contentious topics came up for discussion at the monthly meeting of the War. ington Town Council on Tuesday evening that the proceedings occupied over three hours. The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) presided, and there were also present :—The Deputy- Mayor (Alderman Forshaw), Aldermen A Bennett, W Bolton, T Burton, John Evans, James Evans, W Pierpoint and J Smethurst; Councillors 0 Archer, T If Ashcroft, W It Beillan, T Birth's, T Chantler, J G Craik, E Cneeingtoe, .1 Dickenson, .1 Halliivell. J Hawthorn, It Hewitt.% C Jackson Holmes, W II .1 Jenkins, T Lishford, J T Locker, Dr McKee, Pemberton, li Roberts, NV Rogers, J Sankey, .1 Skellsed, T S Steel, 0 Toole, J Wilkinson and NV J Wilson : Messrs 3 L Whittle (Town Clerk), 3 Fairhurst (Borough Treasurer), T Longdio (Borough Surveyor), F V I. Mathias (Electriol Engineer), J M Murray (Director of Education) and J Gray (Water Engineer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>LATE MR. GEDDES.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. GEDDES.</p>
        <p>At ft deputai</p>
        <p>present", IV'</p>
        <p>- ----- The MAYOR alluded to the late Mr John Gerldes,d.P.' who, he said, had been a highly respec..el re3ident of the borough for many years. It bed been his privilege to have been associated with him for same years in business matters, and he had always found him a keen, honourable and true man of bus nes, (ilear,hear. He had a most kindly manner to all with whom he came into contact ; and it wet in no spirit of pedantry that he (the Mayor) made use of the beautiful Latin epigram in regard to him that he possessed the an:triter in mode, the fortiter in re ; it applied to Mr. Geddes more than to any man be had ever met. (tier, heir) The Mayor, moved : "rhst thisCouocil receives with deep regret the announcement of the death of Mr. Geddes, a worthy and respected townsmen and a magis- trate of the borough, and desires to express with the widow and family the condolence of the Comma in their bereavement." Alderman Pi saeolxv, in seconding, said all who knew the late Mr. Geddes loved him. Alderman BENNErT supported and d)seribei Mr Geddcs as an ideal citizen. The resolution was carried in silence. DEFECTIVE CHILDREN. THE CHOICE OF EMPLOYMENT QUESTION. A WANTON AND WICKED %VASIL' OF THE RATEPAYERS' MONEY:' _ . .</p>
        <p>Councillor Ito-1-31;;;;;;o—essi the confirmation of the minutes of the Education Committee, which rcquired the approval of the Connell. lie allude/ to the pro- poinal to erect a school for defective children in Parr- street, awl said the comuittars had had the matter under consideration for a long time. These children were s source of trouhle and anxiety to the teaching staff, and were not receiving that particular and special attention which was necessary if the brat in them was to be brought out. (Hear, bear.) The backward scholars often had a considerable effect on the average education received by fellow-workers of the same class. The resolution only conarnittted the Council to the principle of providing a school : the plans and estimates would have to mime before the Education Authority. tb ith reference to the choice of employment question, Coun- cillor Roberta explained that the object was, es far as practicable, to assist the parents wheel position was such that they hal very little influence in the direction of obtaining suitable employment for their children. The proposals were provisional and did not commit the Council to anything except a principle. Since the matter was withdrawn at the last meeting of tire Town Council, the Board of Education had signified their Intention to contritute to the cost. Councillor Bitimits seconded. Councillor BEtttaN intimated that he should object to any expenditure on the scheme when it came before the Council for final approval. Councillor PExtBERToN said now was the time to oppose the scheme. Too many things had been got through in the way suggested by C 'engine r Roberts. labia opinion the scheme was entirely wrong awl he should vote against a single penny of the ratepayers' money being spent upon it. Toe Government had already provided a labour bureau, and no one need be concerned that the officials would die of over-work. It would be a wic ed, wanton waste of money to provide a new scheme at the expense of the Warrington rate- payers. The labour bureau could not make a single job, except for their own officials. end they appeared to have proved a failure. The finding of suitable situa- tions for children on leaving school was regulated by the demand and supply and could not be controlled by artificial meats. They had also MD consider the fact that the trade onions very wisely refused to allow their trades to be flooded with apprentices. Warrington was already being burdened mitt' a greater expense on education than it ought to bear. Why should Warring. ton have an education rate of Is. Scl, while the neigh. bouring County of Cheshire escaped with 1.01 ? Alderman JAMES EVANS said the whole question of employment wanted veatilating. Berme he would commit himself to the new scheme be wished to know upon what lines it would be run and what control the Council would exercise. Alderman BOLTON moved as amendment that the scheme be referred back. The metter should be taken in hand by the Legislature instead of saddling municipalities with fads and extravagance.. He also complained that the Town Council had not sufficient representation on the committee which would work the acbeme. Councillor LASAFORD seconded. Dr. McKEE said this was an absolutely aimless scheme and the Labour Bureau was an absolute failure. The Council ought to have fall particulars as to the cost and control before committing themselves to the principle. Councillor WILKINSON : I uoderstood Councillor Roberts to say it would cost the ratepayers nothing. Councillor PEMBERTON : You will not get much for uothine. Councillor ROBERTS expla•ned that since the last • meeting of the Education Committee a communication had been received from the Education Department, in which they stated that they were prepared to make a grant to the local authorities in aid of the Expenses of the scheme. Councillor PEMBERTON : They will give you about 3s. in the B. Councillor WILKINSON said that any man who stated that the labour bureau can a failure did not understand it. It was fulfilling a very important duty and although its effects were rerhaps not prominent locally, it had dcne much to bring men into touch with work in other towns. Alderman Bourn:if : It is not a failure for those who draw the salaries. Councillor ROBERTS : There is an absolute miscon- ception co the part of every speaker es to the real facts. This has nothing to do with the labcur bureau Alderman BOLTON : I did not say it bad. Councillor ROBERTS : That is exactly what you did Say. Alderman BOLTON : I never mentloced the labour bureau. Councillor ROBERTS I Then I must have had my ears filled with cotton wool. (Laughter.) Continuing, be said it had been stated that the bureau was a hideous failure, and that the juvenile employ merit scheme would also be a hideous failure. He was not present to defend the bureau—although he could if there was a suitable opportunity—but that was not the opportunity or place, lie said emphatically that the labour bureau throughout the country were performing signal services —(hear, hear)—in bringing employers and men together and helping to find out the men who did want work and those who would not work and who pestered employers. This scheme was not pins to be under the control of the labour bureau ; it wonld have nothing to do with it. There would he a special committee to be under the control of the Education Author ity,which was composed of people connected with the administration of educa- tion end which mould be largely helped by the head- masters and headmistresses of the schools who knew more about the scholars than anyone else. (Ilearhear). Couocillor PEMBERToN said the scheme provided for the co-operation of the labour bureau. At Ibis point there were ournertus interruptions, and the NIA OR remarked :—" Really these interruptions are intolerable. (Hear, hear.) I proiest against them and I won't allow them." (Hear, hear.) The amendment wss carried, I votirg for and 12 against it. The otl er minutes e coding the confirmation of the Council were approved.</p>
        <p>a 40 CUPS of perfect coffee 2 from 1 bottle of Symington's Coffee 2 Essence—its eco- 2 nomy appeals to the 122 thrifty housewife 11 as forcibly as its fragrance and strength appeal to g the connoisseur. Be sure you Et. Symington's. a nes Sytniwgtays k Co.. 2 Edinburkk &amp; London.</p>
        <p>MINGTONS</p>
        <p>Coffee Essence. 4, :;,a.caa.ia.gQl.73</p>
        <p>visa TO INDUSTRIAL SCIIOOLS. meeting of the Watch Committee a report of al on appointed to visit industrial schools was .d the institutions inspected being Kerriston .eft School, Eye, Suffolk, and an industrial ocher:: at Buxton Lames. The deputation (the Mayor end Alderman Tinnion) personally interviewed the Warrington boys at each school, and were quite satisfied with their appearance end the progress they were making. The thanks of the committee were passed to the deputation for their exhaustite and interesting report. THE POLICE AS "STRIKE BREAKERS." Ate meeting of the Watch Committee, the fallowing letter wee read from the Warrington Tradec and Labour Council: " lam instructed to ask you te place the following resolution, which was passed by the above Council, before the Watch Committee :--` That this Council protests against the locil police force being used as strike breakers, end further points out that we cannot safely reduce the force by about 40 men unless we are overstaffed, which means overrated.'—l remain, dear sir, yours respeotfully, Elowtx Pussros,secretary. STRIKERS TEIANK POLICE. The following letter from the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants (War. ington Branch) was read at a meeting of the Watch Committee : "Tot in Worship the Mayor. Dear ir,—At a mass meeting of railway servants, held in the Ca-operative Hall on Sunday, August '20.12, it was resolved that his Worship the Mayor be &amp;ski d to put it on record the keen apprecia- tion we feel towards the Chief Constable and all officers of tha Police Force for the kindly and courteous manner in which they have conducted their duties towards us during the recent dispute. I beg to remain, yours faithfully, J. TAYLOR." The minutes were confirmed on the motion of the</p>
        <p>amatofy</p>
        <p>111-ATotit seconded by the DEPUTY-NIAYOR. TTER MARK ET-STREE IMPROVEMENT. The Finance and Estate Committee have resolved that application be made to the Local Government Board for the borrowing of the sum of .£7,157 as lid, Wing the amount of the expenditive incurred to date in connection with the Buttermarket-street improvement. At a meeting of the General Porno.. Committer, it was resolved that the Chairman end Deputy-Chairman of the Street Improvement Committee and Councillor Bellian with the Town Clerk and B Snrvayor be appointed to wait upon Messrs. Greened!, Whitley and Co., Ltd., with regard to the purchase of their premises I in Upper Bank-street scheduled for the la idening of Buttermarket-street. The Maroit reported that Mr. Whitley told them that afternoon that if the Corporation took a Amy the portions of the premises they wanted what would be left would be a dead letter to the company. The premises consisted of a malt kiln which had been in regular use for 90 years. The firm would not rebuild because they were not in communication with any railway. The deputation new the reasonableness of the ideas that Mr. Whitley gave them. The Ganeral Purposes Committee had been under a misapprehension in regard to the matter. MR. WOODCOCK'S INCREASE OF SAL tRY. _ _ _</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Finance end Estate Committee, the following reallat len from the overseers was resd : " Resolved, Olt in recognition of the valuable services which Mr. W. H. Woodcock, the assistant overseer, laas renders] since his appointment in September, 1899, his salary for that office be incseated as and from Ist April last, to £2BO per annum, this being the salary already allocated to him in the Overseer's Accounts" ; also that his stiffly be increased from £2OO to £230 per annum as and from the hit April last. Cauncillor WILKINSON. moved the adoption of the minutes, sod referred to the pr 'posed increase in Mr. Woodcock's salary. He read a letter from the Clerk to the Assessment Committee stating that his committee had resolved unanimously to plAce on record their high apprelintion of the valuable and willing services rendered by Mr. Woodcock. They had added that it was the capable manner in whir's he had performed his duties that was in great measure responsible for the increase in the rateable value. Councillor Wilkinson said Mr. Woodcock was appointed in 1893 when the rateable value was £150,000, and it now stood at £280,000. When he was appointed assistant overseer in 1899 he received £5O per annum and it had gone on until he now had £2OO. One feature of Mr. Woodcock's work was that twelve years ago the voters list contained 10,679 names, and there were no loss than 732 claims and objections, while this year the ' names of 12,910 voters were on the list, and only 13 claims were made, net alone saved the Corporation about £4O in printing. Mr. Woodcock had many times placed property on the rate books months before it might have been done and had thus help.d the corpora- tion greatly. Councillor LOCKER seconded, and saia that they had in Mr. Woodcock a servant well worthy of the addition to his salary which was being proposed. Alderman BetiNerr supported the increase. Alderman Jamse EVANS bore testimony to Mr. Woodcock's work on the Assessment Committee. He regretted that the committee could not see their way to accept the recommendation of the overseers that his salary be increased to £2OO. The minutes were agreed to. FILTHY CONDITION OF THE STREETS.</p>
        <p>When the minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Com- mittee were before the CaUnCil Councillor JENKINS mentioned the filthy condition of Bessey-street near the Farmers Stores. The Harlan : The newspaper offices are round there. CouncillorJsvata:a : Yes, bat it is not only the newspapers. There is a lot of other waste papers and it should not be allowed to accumulate. Alderman SMETRURST SIB it WM unfortunate that so many roads met there. Councillor DICKENSON said there was the same nuisance in Nicholson-street. The MAYOR remarked that no doubt the deputy. chairman (Councillor Roberts) would bear the matter in mind. I HOUSES FLOODED DURING RAINFALL.</p>
        <p>Councillor ARCltart said that the last rainfall bad made the state of things in Church-street and the dis- trict appalling. When there was a downfall of rain the water in houses in Dutton-street and round about was a foot deep. Since the last rainfall there hod Wen a great amount of sickness. The MAYOR : The Corporation have no responsi- bility to provide storm overflows. Councillor Awcrisit remarked that there was a flood in the mildest of rainfalls. That state of things had been brought to the notice of the Paving and Sewerage Committee many times. Alderman Join; Eva-is said that the last 'storm entailed mu* hardship upon the district referred to. He knew for a fact that the houses were flooded, and the Council had no right to ulkw such conditions to ottain. Alderman BOLTON said it was not the case only in Church-street but also in Orford-lane and other low- 'Ong places. The Health Committee seemed to have many fads, and he hoped they would tackle this nuis- ance, which was a danger to health. Alderman JOHN EVANS said he was surprised that the people had not made a greater protest than they had done. His cellars had three feet of water in them at the last storm. Councillor PEMBERTON said he was glad to see that members were taking a great interest in the affairs of the town he woe never more pleased in his life. (Laughter.) With regard to Councillor Jenkins he must say that Bewsey-street was scavenged like the rest of the town. The reason there was no much paper about was because of the calibrae of the trade of the shops. The police could deal with it if they carried out the bylaws. The MAYOR: If it were thrown about wilfully they would do. Councillor PEMBERTON : Nobody ever does anything wilfully. (Laughter.) The M•YOR : Carelessly. Councillor PEMBERTON, continuing, said that the Paving and Sewerage Committee would go into the matter and report. With regard to the flooding of houses he reminded them that Alderman Tinnion a few months ego spoke of the conditions of rho sewers and said that between £150,000 and £200,000 would be required so that they could have anew sewerage system. The minutes were adopted. ASSISTANT GAS ENGINEER'S SALARY INCREASED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Gss Committee, it woe resolved that the salary of Mr. J. McNichol], assistant gas engineer, be increased from £2.:5 to £250 per annum, as and from the Ist prox. Alderman PIKRPOINT, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, spoke in high terms of Mr. NoNicholl's services to the Corporation and the Council approved of the increase. LABOURERS' WAGES ADVANCE.</p>
        <p>Ile Gas Committee, at a meeting on September 6th, tassed a resolution to the effect " that the wages of the labourers who are fit and willing to work in the Retort House when called opon,be increased by Id. per hoar." Alderman Pierpoint exolained that that would mean an incresse of Is. rid. pe, week. In answer to a member, Alderman Plituroncr stated that from CO to 40 labourers would be affected. The total wage would be 235. 10d. per week, lie explained the work in the Retort House was higher than that in the yard. Aldermen Bor:rox : This means an increase of from £0 to £5 per week. The minute was approved. OUR WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The Water Committee regoleed that a in. main be laid from Hatton-road to the two cottagm situate in Houghs-lane, Appletoc, and that the engineer to authorised to proceed forthwith with the laying of a 12in. main from Marsh House-lane, to the end of Scotland-road in Buttermarket-street, such main to be continued to Market Gate %%hen Buttermsrket-street has been widened. Alderman JOHN EVANS, teconded by Alderman BOLTON seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor CRAM hoped that while the water main was being laid the tramway lines would be attended to. The minutes were adopted. THE LETTING OF A CONTRACT BY AN OFFICIAL. ANIMATED SCENE.</p>
        <p>In proposing the minutes of the Town Hall, Perks and Baths Cc mmittee, Alderman Bolton said that they</p>
        <p>bad got the consent ef the Local Government Board to borrow £3,37 so that they could erect two battle, one for each an. They had now got a chance of going on with tbem. That visa one of the best things they had</p>
        <p>CIO; for the town for a considerable numbe", of ;ears, an by it the people would be able to develop their muscles mad become c'eaner. They would therefore</p>
        <p>benefit in health. Councillor DlumestsoN seconded the minutes. Councillor Lisette Otto referred to the following minute " That the tender of Messrs. W. Winstanley and Co. for the Wetting, end repair of the Rinsing, of two Greenhouses iu Bank Perk, be accepted." He asked the Chairman k Alderman liolton) when were the tenders opened and by whom ; did the committee authorise the opening of the tenders prior to the day of the committee meeting; who took it upon himself to accept the tender? Ile added that the work was commenced before the committee had met to consider the tenders. Alderman BOLTON said the tender was only for a small amount (CO 1:1s.) and when the surveyor received it the work was proceeded with. Councillor LASH FORD said the work Was almost com- pleted before the committee considered the tenders. Alderman BOLTON said the wcrk necessitated immediate attention gni no the Surveyor accepted the tender, which was the lowest. Councillor LASIIFORD askrd if they could °spat people to tender if they we-e not prrprrly consilered ? To this the Mayors replied that the lowest tender was accepted. COUREMIOr LAEHFORD denied that the lowest tendet. was accepted. The MAYOR reiterated that he had it en the best authority that the lowest tender was accepted. C tun dllor Lassie:SßO asked tt hat right one individual had to open tenders ? There was Emmething done that he was ashamed of when he was en the Sanitary Works Committre. Aldermsn JAMBS EVANS replied that the tender wad the los eat and tJouncillor Lashford in deference to the chair should withdraw what be had sold. COMMiIIOIII CHANTLER, Ca tic. ROG FRS and Alder- man SMETHURST els, mentioned that the tender was the lowest which had bten received. COORCiIIOr LASH rORD said he would withd-aw his allege; ion on that score. The MAYOR: You ere out of order, Councillor WILKINSON remarked that the roost merlons part was that the work was commenced before the committee considered the tenders. Councillor JEER tNi said the explanation given by Alderman Bolton was a common sense one as there were times when immediate action was necessary. Had the time crme when tt i .ey had no confidence n their officials ? Esper ienced men deserved a certaM degree of trust and what possible harm could there have been as the tender ass the !sweet and the roes; ion should be looked at from a common-sense standpoint. Councillor PEI' lIEKTON or jected to the print iple of the work heiug commerced before the tenders were submitted to the committee. They bad a standing order that tenders shnuld be sealed and opentd before the committee. The SlAvott mentioned hat it was not against the standing orders for an I Olcial to open a tender when no more than £2O was involved, as only contracts abovqe that amoent eere obliged lobe ndreriized. Councillor PEMBERTON said is bed not been done properly. Councillor WILKINSON : Prove it hen not teen done roperly, Councillor PEMBERTON.: It has_not._ .</p>
        <p>Councillor WILK InsON (to Councillor Pemberton) : 1 won't have this town blackened by you. Councillor PESIBLETON Ycu ere only one in the town. Councillor WILKINSON; November is coming round again. Councillor PEMBERTON : I don't CATO A hang for you or November either. (Laughter). Councillor WILKINSON : I am slob of it. Councillor BELLIAN : Councillor Wilkinson is out of order. Every member should be allowed to express his opinion. Nobody interferes with Councillor Wilkinson. Councillor WILKINSON; No, I have got sense.</p>
        <p>(Laughter). COUReiIIOT LASHFORD: So have other people. Councillor PEMBERTON : I don't mind at all. I am awfully pleased when people lcse their temper and say what they man. It is ref.eshing to me to hear their true sentiments. The tender is only £5 Lis. and I agree that the committee had no need to advertise. Put we have a standing order that tenders shall be sent in retled and opened before the commiteee. Any intelli- gentleman knows the reason for that. The MAYOR (ironically) : But 1 am not an intelligent</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBBRTON : Yes, you are. Aldermen BOLTON : That's a splendid testimonial for you. (Laughter). Councillor PRAIBERTON said that as the Mayor bad not had the business experience of some other members of the Council, he would not be" to "the dodges of certain trades. (Laughter.) \Vita regard to t're case under notice, the pornt was that the tenders were brought before the committee to be adjudicated upon when one had been accepted two days before and the work nearly completed. A principle was involved. If the committee had referred the matter to the Borough Surveyor for decision, it would have been a different thing. However, Mr. Longdin might have acted on somebody else's °rile s, and it was unfair to say they were attacking him. The manner in which the tenders bad been dealt with was an improper and dangerous., precedent to net up. The Maven: It was the lowest tender. Councillor PRAIMIRTON : That is quite true. Coun- cillor Lashford says that. The MAYOR : He did not admit it ts me when I told him. Councillor PEMBERTON : He got up to say so and was ruled out of order. The MAYOR: I gave him every charm I told him, on proper authority, that it was the lowest tender, but he did not withdraw, although he bad plenty of time. I would not have allowed him to withdraw afterwarda, even if I had known he was going to do so. Conncillor PEMBERTON : He was perhaps a little excited over it. The MAYOR : I think he treated the chair very imoroperly. Councillor BREMERTON : lam acre none of us wish to do that. Councillor SANKSY : What does Councillor !Ash- ford mean by saying that there was something in con- nection with the Sanitary Committee that he wan ashemed of? Councillor LASHFORD : It was not on the minutes, but I refer to the bag question some months back. Aldermen JOHN EVANS said Councillor Lashford had charged the committee with wrong-doing in the case of the bag contract. Certain tenders were sent in and before they were opened and considered, Councillor Lashford advocated the acceptance of the tender of one of his private friends, his reason being that the party in question had tendered for many years and had never had a contract. Councillor WILKINSON: That ins good idea. Councillor Lassrone explained that he did not receive a correct notification of the time of the meeting at which the tenders were considered. In this case the tender which he advocated was the lowest. Alderman Bor.-ros said the discussion had been a storm in a tea cup. It was very easy forms mistake to occur in the opening of envelopes,nal to say that every i little detail n an urgent matter of that kind should be carried out on red tape lines was not business. The minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>.1;t120. a a 1114 gala</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDOR. SHOULD THERE BE A CEVIENT TESTER LIVELY SCENE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDOR. SHOULD THERE BE A CEVIENT TESTER LIVELY SCENE.</p>
        <p>There was a lively discussion when the minutes of the General Purposes Committee were considered. Councillor LASHFORD referred to the following minutes en the private agenda of the Council : " Resolved, on the recommendation of Mr. Webster, and subject to his accepting the fall responsibility for the efficiency of his services, that Mr. U. H. Hodpon be appointed to carry out the cement testing and examination of other material in connection with the contract at a salary of £2 2s per week, the engagement to be terminable on one week's notice from either side. He sail he had never known a case like that anywhere before, Mr. Webster had been appointed to do the work and if the cement needed testing, he should do it. Councillor WILKINSON: YOU don't know what you are talking about. (Laughter.) Alderman SMETHCRST : Disgusting. Councillor LASHFORD said it was abominable to carry on business in such a manner. (Hear. hear.) The committee did not agree with the appointment and yet they brought it here to be confirmed by the Council. The MAYOR: The committee have agreed to it.</p>
        <p>.Alderman SMF.TMURST : Of MUM, they have. Councillor LASHFORD moved that the minute be not confirmed, and the motion was seconded simultaneously by Councillor PEMBERTON and Councillor 11.swwer.c. Councillor PEMBERTON supported the amendment and geld he had never known a man to be appointed specially for such a position. Besides Mr. Webster the Corporation bad to pay a Clerk of Works. The latter, be said, bad a splendid time on a job of that description and if Mr. Webster wanted ao extra man to watch the cement be ought to pay for it out of the very bandemne remuneration they had offered him. The council would make a great mistake if they confirmed the appoint- ments because they would be responsible and not the architect. 'rhe MAYOR, the TOWN CLERK, and several members assured the Council that Mr. IVebster would se held iesponsible, and Alderman BENNETT mentioned that the remuneration Mr. Webeter was receiving was .C 750 and if he bad to pay the cement tester out of that the job would not pay him. A long discuesion ensned on the qneetion and eventu- ally the lecommendation el the committee was approved by 11 votes to S. The above debate was very acrimonious and Alderman Pierpcint deprecated it, remarking that for 12 months St least be would not be able to speak or vote in that Council Chamber. He hoped that the debates would improve in the meantime. THE PILE DRIVING CEREMONY. ALDERMAN FORSHAW'S POSITION.</p>
        <p>---- Alderman BURTON wanted to know what stood in the way of Alderman Forshaw driving a pile at the War- rington Bridge on Thursday. Several members agreed that something ahoulel be done no that Alderman</p>
        <p>Forshaw could participate in the ceremony and Coun- cillor Pemberton said that although there were only 2 pile driving machines, one of them c iuld be moved in about 5 minutes and then a third pile could be driven. The TOWN CLARK' said that at a meeting of the Bridges Committee it was resolved " That Mr. Webster be requeated to arrange, if practicable, for the driving of a third pile, and that in the event of him being able to do no Alderman Forabaw be invited to drive, pile." Ile (the Town Clerk) wrote the same night to 51r. Webster to the effect that the committee were unanimous that three piles should be driven and reqcest- ing him to make the arrangements. Mr. Webster replied that it was impracticable, as there were only 'two pile drivers. The oast of a third pile driving machine (added Mr. Whittle in answer to a member) was estimated at from £35 to £lO. After exnressions of regret from several members that Alderman Feral tw could not drive a pile the latter said he did not blame any member of the Council. Ile did not think they had been discourteous but he did say : be thcu‘ht a third pile might have been driven.</p>
        <p>1. tiln the a 0 I OURTS- e 4 _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>MARKET THEFTS BY JUVENILES.</head>
        <p>MARKET THEFTS BY JUVENILES.</p>
        <p>A WARNING. Seven small boys were charged at a juvenile court on Thursday before Mr W Roberts and other magistrates, with stealing a quantity of biscuits, some time during the past six months, from a stsil in the market, the property of Robert Fitton. The Chief Constable said that the prosecutor resided its Man- chester and attended the Market, where he occupied a lock-up stall, on 'Wednesdays and Saturdays. lie had suffered loss to a considerable extent by these thefts and bad been put to great expense to try and prevent them. Re was loath to prosecute and had only done no in the boys' awn interests. Detective Sergeant Reddington, while making itquiries into a further theft of two tins of sardines by two of these boys, ascertained that they were associated with the thefts of the biscuits and he also obtained the names of the other defendants. He (tLe Chief C instable) thought that the fact of the boys acting together made the matter worse, as he did not think they were really bad in themselves. They did not seem to tiodentmd the seriousness of the offence. _ . .</p>
        <p>„..—. Mr. Fitton said that spirt from the thefts of the bisonits, greet loss bad been experienced throm,lt the damage to other biscuits which the boys had nat been able to obtain. . . . . . .• ..</p>
        <p>Two of the boys were further charged with stealing two tire cf sardines, the property of Messrs. Pegrams. The Chairman of the Bench said that, a serious offence bad been committed. the boys knew perfectly well that they had no right to take these things away. The magistrates,although they could nc t over-look the matter had taken a lenient view. If the defendants appeared there again they would be seriously dealt with. Each boy would iezeive three strokes of the birch-rod, while the two boys who wereconcerted in the sardine stealing would receive an additional stroke (soh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>ASSAULT ON A CONSTABLE. QUESTION OF RIGHT OF ENTRY.</head>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A CONSTABLE. QUESTION OF RIGHT OF ENTRY.</p>
        <p>The right of a constable to enter a dwelling-house was the chief feature of a Mae heard at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday before Messrs F A. Frost (presiding), C. E. Parker End Charles White, when Peter Hares, 61, Liverpool-road, Snnkey, was charged with assaulting P.C. Jones at Sankey, on September 301h.—Mr. Knowles preset teed on behalf of the County Police, and Mr. T. S. Steel, who represented the prisoner, put in • plea of not guilty. Mr. Knowles said that on the day in question the constable was called to the house of the prisoner at 9 a.m. End found him ins quarrelsome mood. He paid two further visits to the house, end upon the last I occasion a large crowd had gathered and toe prisoner's I wife was shouting "Murder" and " help " from out of I a bedroom av:ndow. In consequence of this and the remarks made by the crowd the constable felt it his duty to go into the house. The wife was shouting that her husband was on the stairs armed with an iron bar, and she implored the constable to save her and the children from being murdered. The constable obtained an entry by climbing through the kitchen window and then opened the door and admitted two witnesses, and the trio proceeded to go upstairs. As they were about to go upstairs the prisoner ran into the front bedroom and fastened the door. The constable went to the door and called upon the prisoner to be sensible and quiet. The prisoner then repeatedly thrtht the iron ha,, which was about 6ft, long, through the panel of the door with snob violence that it went into the wall upon the other side. The constable and a witness named David Benehill seized the bar and pulled it from the man. They then obtained entrance to the room to try to persuade the prisoner tobe quiet. The constable was nfl looking at the prisoner when the latter rushed at the constable and struck him on the jaw with his head, which partially psralysed the con- stable and before he could recover he was again struck. The witness Bonehill sod the constable then seized the prisoner and he was locked spun a charge of assault- ing the police while in the execution of his duty. In reply to Mr. Steel the constable after corroborat- ing the above stated that he had no warrant. Mr. Steel said that the fact of the constable having I no warrant, which the law strongly emphasised, went to show that the constable had no right to enter the home. Mr. Knowles : Where a felony is going to be com- mitted, and murder and wounding are felonies, and the Constable thinks something of that nature is going to happen, he has power to enter a house. Three witnesses having corroborated Mr. Knowles' first statement, the prisoner's wife stated that she "begged " of the constable many times to go into the house and save them. Mr. Steel quoted a case which stated that a constable had no right within the exerciee of his duty to enter another person's house without a warrant which is properly backed and without the slightest irregularity, otherwise there could be no charge. Thin constable never even wert near the people that were in danger Sod try to get them out but went straight for this man. He went to arrest the prisoner, if anything for a mis- demeanour, as there was no charge whatever against him. The constable had no right in the house end therefore the case was bound on legal grounds to be disc issed. Mr. Knowles said that it was th 3 duty of a constable to take precautions to prevent the man, by his removal, from committing a felony. The Chairman said that after carefnl consideration they had decided to convict cud would therefore fine She prisoner 10s. including costa with the alternative of 14 days' imprisonment. Mr. Steel stated that the case was not finished as he had not finished his evidence. He bad not intend( d that to he the finish of the case without they had decided upon a dismissal upon the legal point as to whether the constable was there on duty. As they had decided against him (Mr. Steel) on those grounds Ire intended to proceed with the case Continuing he said that evidence would be produced which would state that the prisoner when arrested was suffering from three wounds on the head which were bleeding profusely. Ile was taken all the way to the she Police Station in that condition before the wounds were attended to. He had ;also been in prison for four days and as the assault was not a serious one he ventured to hope that the ease might he dismissed upon payments of costs. fbe Bench adherred to their previous penalty. , Mr. Knowles asked for the advocate's and the , witnesses fees but these were refused. I Prisoner was then summoned by his wife for persistent cruelty, but pleaded not guilty. Alr.T. S. Steel defended. Complainant stated that it was a regular occurrence when the prisoner was in drink for him to ill-use her. About four weeks ago she had to look herself in a room to escape from him after he had ill-used her. On September 30th when he came from work he banded his wife 193. and when she asked him if he had not any more to give her prisoner knocked witness down and kicked her. In reply to Mr. Steel she denied being in the habit of having people in the house drinking. She did not pawn anything but a war medal and clasp which was her own property for it had been given to tier by the prisoner. SLe was compelled to pawn it to pay the rent- The Peach granted a separation order with is. a week allimony the wife to have the cost Aly of the seven children. A charge of assault by P.C. Jones against the prisoner Peter Hares was withdrawn on the application of Mr. Steel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Sein'etDAY.—Before— Messrs. J W Hayward (in the chair), T Pemberton end A J Jolley. Trim. OF BOOTS.—Albeit Edward Coke, of no I settled abode, was charged with stealing a pair of Mel boots, value Si 6d, from a shop door in Lovely-lane,l the property) of Messrs. Stubbs, pownbroker.—The Chief Constable said that about 10.5 the previous day an assistant employed at the above named shop, raw the prisoner acting in a suspicious mutter. lb o kept him under observation and noticed biro 'ohm eon- , tinually to where the boots were banging. Priaocerf took out. knife and was lathe act of cutting the boots down when somebody WI nt past. lie afterwards carve beck again end took the boots.—Prisoner who had nothing to say was sentenced to 14 days imprisonment. MONDA Y.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) /Mermen Bolton, Smethurst, James Evans, Forebear, Messrs S Wright, W Roberts, W Furness, II Thornton, G Pilling and T Locher. ALLEGED POLICE ASSAULT.—CASE DISMISSED.— Thomas Pridden, 11. Glilbert-streef, was charged On 1 remand with assaulting P.C. Liarrison.—The Chief IConstable said that a week the previous Sunday the prisoner was locked op by a Constable for f eing drunk and disorderly and while on the way to the Police Station it was alleged that the prisoner kicked the</p>
        <p>officer. There was an allegation against the officer that he struck the prisoner and broke a tooth.—P.C. Harrison said that on September 25th he had occasion to apprehend the prisoner and a man named Burgess for being drunk and disorderly in Egerton-street. Whilst on the way to the Police Station, at the top of Howley-lane the prisoners began to struggle violently. He put Pridden on the ground and blew his whistle for assistance. While on the ground Pridden deliberately kicked witness on the back of the hand and inflicted a nasty wound. A civilian rendered assistance until the arrival of another officer. Pridden's wife then tried to pull her husband away and witness had to push her away. P.C. Taylor and Harry Woolstencroft, of 138, l'adgate- lane, spoke as to going to the constable's assistance. The latter stated that Pridden, while lying on the floor, landed out with hie arms and legs—A number of witnesses were called who stated that the constable drew his staff but the constable denied this allegation. The Bench adjourned and upon their return the Chair- man said that the magistrates were of opinion that an assault had been committed but that it was accidental and the case was dismissed. The Bench commended the action of Woolstencroft in going to the constable's aseistance.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Councillors Henry Roberts (in the chair) and C Jackson Holmes. Wien ASSAULT.—WiIIiam Cosgrove, 26, General- street, was charged on remand with aulaulting his wife. Chief Inspector Williamson said that the case was adjourned from December last, the originvl charge against the defendant being one of ii filming grievous bodily harm upon his wife, by kicking her in the jaw, which was fractured. Since the adjournment the police had not received any complaints from the wife.—Mr. Steel, who appeared for the defendant, applied for hie discharge.—Mrs. Coagrove said that her husband had been very good since the adjouranivnt.—The Butch decided to hind the defendant over, in the sum of .610, for twelve months. A SMART PENALTY FOR A SERIOUS OF FENCE.—</p>
        <p>William &amp;rags, Heath berm, Thelwall, wee charged with beisg drunk in charge of a home and van in Knutsford-road. A constable said that about 2Ai p.m. the previous dry shilit on duty at Bridge Foot, he ricerved a complaint theta man in charge of a furniture van was drunk. He met the prisoner between Beatrice' Street and Grove-street. He was sitting on the van and %hen witness requested him to get down he was no drunk that he could hardly stand.—Prisoner stated that be was out very early in the morning and he got a glass of beer which hid opset him.—. One of 20a. was imposed.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDA V.—Belore Mr. Charles White (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Messrs. IV. T. Williams, .1. W. Heyward, and T. Locker. ARREARS or MAINTENANCF.—Benjaraia Baugh, fleck-road. Walsall, an ironworker, was summoned by his wife Alice Baugh, 21, Lilford-street, for Arrears ct maintenance amounting to .E 7 156.—Prisonar stated that be had not been able to pay any money through being out of work. The complainant stated that she had not seen her husband since the day the separation order was granted at that court.—The Bench ordered the prisoner either to pay the money or go to gaol for two months.</p>
        <p>MISCELLANZOUS.—One cue of drtink and disorderly-, one case of drunkenness, two cases of Oghtin g, and oan case of begging were also heard at this court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>"WHY IS MOTHER ALWAYS ILL</head>
        <p>"WHY IS MOTHER ALWAYS ILL</p>
        <p>" Every Picture Tells a Story."</p>
        <p>WORN OUT with the miserable pains of Kidney Disease— Backache, Headache, Dropsical Swellings, Dizziness, Heart Attacks, and twinges of Rheumatism-- WARRINGTON WOMAN.</p>
        <p>WORN OUT with the worries and cares of the children and the home—the constant stooping about, and the running up and down stairs from morn- ing till night. Is it any wonder that the kidneys break down? WORN OUT—So much to do, so little strength to do it. Every house- hold task made a burden by the miserable pains of kidney disease— Lumbago, pains in the loins and back, heart attacks, dizziness, nervous head- ache, rheumatism, gravel and urinary disorders— Those bearing-down pains, the swellings of dropsy, and that heavy, drowsy feeling which makes you feel so low and miserable. But don't grow disheartened. A neighbour—a Warrington woman—tells in the next column what a comfort Doan's Backache Kidney Pills have been to her. Doan's Pills are for the kidneys only—not for constipation, indigestion or liver troubles. They relieve the kidneys and bladder like laxative medicines relieve the bowels, and dispose of the kidney poisons which are the cause of so much suffering in men and women.</p>
        <p>Mrs. S. Howell, of 13, Albett Street, Hewsey Road, Warrington, says :—" I've been wonder- fully better in health since I used Doan's back- ache kidney pills. I took them for the kidneys, which had been causing me a great deal of misery for about nix months. "The trouble started with lumbago. The pains in my back and kidneys were frightful. The kidney excretions were disordered. There were, also bad headaches. I suffered terribly with these at times, and also with nervousness, dizziness, and a constant tired feeling. " I kept to my bed for four or five weeks, and then got up, but was still bad. 'then two months afterwards I was laid up again for three weeks. " When I first began using Doan's backacle kidney pills, I noticed that ease came after the first four or fire doses ; and after that I steadily improved, until I haulms quite well. (Sigsed) Selina Howell." IVea-ly four years later. Mrs. Howell said : " Doan's pills cured me acme time ago, and I am as well now as I have ever been in my life." 2/9 a box, six boxes ;of all Chemists and Stores, or from Foster-MeClellan Co., S. Well* St., Oxford St. London, W. Don't ash for backache and kidney pills—ash distinctly for DOAN'S Backache Kidney Pine, the same as Mrs Howell had. WRITE FOR ILLCSTRATED BOOKLET ON 'DISEASES OF THE MONEYS AND BLADDER," SENT POST FREE</p>
