      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>GOLF.</head>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON GOLF CLUB. WARRINGTON LADIES V. HATDOCK PARK L ADM. —Player at Warrington, 4ch Ootobar, 19 I. Miss Greensll 1 Mien Knowles Mrs Newcombe hr. Hepherd Mrs E A White _ . I</p>
        <p>...... ...... 1 bliss F Glover ... I Mrs Green</p>
        <p>Mrs Birt.les ... Alin Bainbridge.</p>
        <p>Mr. Carnegie and St. Ann s Organ.</p>
        <p>St. Anna Parish Magazine gays t—The month of September, 1011, sew many and most important advances in our Organ Renovation Scheme. • The Church Council met on Monday, September 4th, and drafted replies to the form of irquiry sent by Mr. Andrew Cernegie. These were duly sent with the hope that our appeal for a grant-in-aid might be favourably considered. Om Tuesday, September 12th, a letter was receired from Mr. Carnegie conveying the promise of aid up to £125 to be spent directly on the organ. We are only voicing the unanimous feelings of all connected with our pariah when we express our most grateful thanks to Mr. Carnegie for his kindly and opportune gift. We may add that the vote of thanks missed at the vestry meeting on Thursday, September 21s1, Las been forwardtd by the Vicar to the donor of the gift.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>SHAKESPEARE'S VILLAINS.</head>
        <p>SHAKESPEARE'S VILLAINS.</p>
        <p>DR LAW S LECTURE AT THE ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>"Shakespeare's Villains " was the interesting title of a paper read by Dr. Charles D. Law at the Old Academy on Monday evening. Dr. Law, it will be remembered, portrayed Macbeth in the recent celebra- tion of Shakespeare's birth and, therefore, he was well qualified to deal with his subject. Mr. Robert Clamed presided over a good attendance. Dr. Law said the wonderful gen'us of Shakespeare was illustrated thrr fighout the plays in no surer way than by the rare which he was able to charaetteise all shades of good and evil in man. NON here did we find a etereotyped creation, wire!) kept on appearing over and over ag tin throughout the plays, but every character had it. own distinctive features which imparted an individu 1 personally. In thia respect the Elizabethan Ilaywri;ht stoad pre-eminen.ly alone. In what order Sh be peare wrote the plays was con- jectural, but au horities were agreed that the dates of their composition might be dialled into four periods. The fi st extended between 1588 and 1596, the second from 1596 to 1602, the third from 1602 to 1607 and the fourth from 1608 to 1613 It was in the third p nod that Shakespeare j,ave the world the majority of his creations of bad men. That was suppased to be the time of the pa. es lire, when the brightness and vivacity of his youth had gone, when his soul was full of bitterness and when hie oUTLOOK ON LIFE GENERALLY WAS TINGED 51 an SADNESS. With the excaptlan of the historical characters in the three paste of Ilet.ry VI. and Riaeierd lll.uo bad men appeared in the plays of the first period. The only outstanding one of the second period was Shylock, Don John in "Much ado abut nothing" and Oliver in "As you like it" brig of little importance. But between 1602 and 1607 (the Gird period) we found Claudine in " Hamlet," Angelo in "Measure for measure," logo, Macbeth, and Edinund in King Lear. In the fourth ant last period, lachimo in "Cymbeline" alone stood out as a really bad man. Thnt Shakespeare did not find himself c nsp4le Ito introduce a villain lotto a tragedy was amply proved wh-,n Romeo and Joh, t was considered, and that a really had man could be brought by I. ito into a comedy, without appeal ing out of place, " Much ado about:notitiog "furnished a etriktng example. After dealing snub the villainy as exhibited by Don John and Oliver, Dr. 1,91•1 said that when Shakespeare wrote the " Merchant of Venice" if be had a moral object in his mind, it was probably to show the debasing effect which fall tarred the pose seine of wealth. _</p>
        <p>SHTLOCK, THE JEW. stood out clearly as a man whose heart and soul clung money and the acqui.ing of it. lie would bear insults, remain friendless and lose his daughter no long as he might. It had been claimed. that Sbylock'a revenge was based upon his religi n, but that WJe estrontly doubtful, as his mind dwelled more upon his ducats than on his belief. Like all bid man Shylock was a liar and a hypocrite. It can in the terrible scene with 'rubel that Shylock alsv.lutely reveal. d b ms-if, for tiers he showed that be sat without 1 de for his d ughter, that his ducats were to him hit Clod, that he was destitnte of pity, and that Ms revet go was that of a disappointed and m.oldene I beast. 0„ ly for a brief mon.ent did he let us are that the spark ol love had ever dimly illumintd his soul and th tt. was his reference to his dead wile Leah. tt hen S •yfrck tottered out of the Court dep ited of everythi g. Ids wealth, bin child anti hi, religion it w a an it., ful punishment, but he only got shot he dt a-rveti. He (the lecturer) confessed that he was quite u-adle t. discover in the character of the Vvnedan money- lender, a single redeeming trait, a-1 wl en It e audience was moved to tears on his behalf he con- sidered it as a great c nupliment to the a t r, hut a misconception of what Sitakesrare dam ded 84,1, ric should leave the court as biterly hated by the audience, as he was by the nth, r characters In the</p>
        <p>plea. (liner,bra'.) Referring to CLALIDIUs the murderer of II toilet's father, and the naurpar of that rightly be'onAei to Hamlet, the croon, end the title of king, Dr. lan said that he mesa bad roan in every particular. Ile mass sensualist and drunkard and it WM diffienit to underetand why the people sere satisfied to have him 83 their Islet, he predecor.sor having been so different to him in every respect and the rightful successor to the throne (Hamlet) being so popular. Ile w.s a smooth-tongued •illian end referred to the king he had lately sent to his doom as "our dear brother" and he was ever anxious to ingratiate himself with Hamlet, whom be bad every reason to be afraid of. Cladius was a nerveless, cowardly villian and he trembled lest his sin should find him Ott'. Had he possessed the courage of either Richard M. Macbeth or logo he wou'd not have dallied bog, but wonild have fould cute% and effec.ive means to rid himself of Hamlet whose very existence woes perpetual source of fear and danger to him. The happiness which he looked f trwtrd to enjoying after he had murdered Lis brother (Hamlet's father) married his widow, and mounted the throne, was but momentary and try as he would he could not drown his conscience in the wine bowl, nor obtain the much desired salve for it by prnyer. Had Claudius not made a confident of Laertes, had he kept his own c Anna, it is most likely that his object wou'd have been attained end that he would have continued King of Denmark. In including</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>M ACRETH</head>
        <p>M ACRETH</p>
        <p>In the gallery of SheltAty;;Tre'n. villains the lecturer recognised that criticism was at once invited. It wits quite possible to reg the Then., of Cowl .r in two perfectly correct and yet entirely dissimilsr tiers. He might be considered as a hi° yd.thirsty, cruel fiend in human form and an a most unfortunv'e victim of fate. Personally, he incline! to the litter view, conceding however, that in his or saniss.ion thee were elernenui of crime waiting to he developed by circurnstance and temptation. II id not AI iebe.h had the misfortune to meet the weird sisters on the blasted heath and to have married a most amti I ma woinsn, he p obsbly would have reached old ego with honour and have left hehiud him a name unsullied end a r puts ion clear. But it was decreed that lit shoold be the channel through which evil agencies could woes, end to hit 1110111de misfortunate he proved midleible metal in the bands of spirits of darkness. At the opening of the tragedy Macbeth was a hold and n ible warrior, e trustworthy general suds itiiot cl subject. lie stood high in the estimation of bis king Ynd both present and future honours were assure/ him. Until he met for the flr4 time the witchis ant Ikten Yri t their dish dile] prophecies no thought of tingly power had enteved his soul. Likely enough if there had been but one prophecy and that of filacheth's becoming king, he would never have thought of trying to mils° the unfolded future. It was the combination of circumstances which undid him, lie was ever under the sonkinnining Mut:lnce of his wife—and he knew it. For his wife Macbeth had a deep affection, and it was thi I which was a greet element in his downfall. lie (Dr. Lsw) thought that agleam of admiration must be kindled for Macbeth as when he met NI/soden he was a fearless aold:er, he was BO coward. Proceeding to comment on</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>RICHARD 111</head>
        <p>RICHARD 111</p>
        <p>Dr. Law said that Richard was unquationah'y an actor- villien. His histrionic abilities were Protean the question was : When was he really hims II?• 'hose probably were the reasons why the portrayal of the bunch-backed king hid always been the delight of tragedians, and the joy of audiences. He was the greatest reeks believe HIM. Shakespeare devoted his genius to depict. Shifting and changing as he did, actor that. he always was, Richard was consistent in only one thing—wicketiness. Still his wickedness never had the loathsome Machiavellian quality which ways predominant feature of Logo's villiany. Amongst all the bad people with whom he was associated, be they Yorkista or Lancastrian', Richard stood above them all, both in the extent of bin wickedness and in his intellectual superiority. In no other historical play had Shikespesre adhe-ed more closely to facts, and given his imngination of incident less scope than in Rit herd 111. The anachronisms and inaccuracies which abounded in moat of the other dramas based on history were almost entirely absent and Richard 111, might be accepted ass true record of the events which occurred at the closing of the Wars of the Rosen. Shakespeare was only remembering the recorded description of Richard, pls3sically and mentally, when he introduced the last representative of the House of Yora as the principal character of a tragedy. Quoting from the chronicles of Holinshead and Hell we learn that Richard was bons with teeth, be was itgly, and his left shoulder was higher than the right. Wickedneu, anger, envy, belonged to his nature, a quiet sharp wit to his mind. . . . Close end secret, a deep dissembler, lowly of countenance, he was at the same time impervious and arrogant at butt, disdainful eons in death, outwardly compsniable where he inwardly hated, not afreid to kiss whom bethought to kill; despitions and cruel, not for evil will alway, bat oftener for ambition and policy. If his ufety or ambition inter- fered, he spared neither friend nor foe." He was entirely alone; he was loved by none; and he could love none. Mentally. inflnitely superior to everyone else, physically so repulsive that the dogs balked at him, deprived of love for either men or woman, and possessed of a hurt that could never be stirred by pity or fear, what good could ever spring in such an organ- isation. To deal with the villains of Shakespeare and omit</p>
        <p>11(:11 With to play Ilamlet without the Prince of Denmark—a</p>
        <p>virtual impossibility. Ingo occupied a pinnacle of wickedness, which none other of the other bad men of Shakespeare in any apprecciable measure approached. He was the consummation of the author's power to depict vileness inhabiting a human form. The deeds of wrong of Macbeth, Claudius, Shylock and Richard, paled before those of Ingo. Macbeth was urged beyond hie better self, Claudius would willingly have been satisfied with one crime, Shylock was dominated by a love for gold, Richard's crimes were inspired by ambition. But lago was winked for the sake of wickedness itself. Though he affirmed that it mut jealousy that was his prompter it was not no. His war a mind " that butcheries." In bin days of childhood he no doubt obtained exquisite joy by witnessing the pain and suffering of dumb creatures. His was the nature which would gloat over the writhings of insects deprived of their limbs or wings, and yet alloeed to live, helpless kittens and blind puppies struggling for their little liven in a butt of water, sr the orphaned liadglings crying for the mother that had been pulled out of the nest and strangled. Dating all that wan good, worshipping at the shrine of evil, lago was • veritable disciple of Satan. Considering his comparative youth (he was 28 years of age) one might well wonder at the matured state of his villainy, if it were not remembered that he had developed his talent for devilry from hie earliest days. Possessed of many talents', and a most active brain; a good soldier, and a clear reader of character there was no rens .n why he should not have risen to a high position , in the army, but his inborn baseness overwhelmed all ' honest endeavour for advancement. lb) always ridiculed the idea of virtue, honour, conscience and repetation and w .uld not even spare his (ten wife in his vile machinations. Nothing was sacred to him. (Applies.) MLOSWALD LOFfIIOUSF, in proposing a hearty vote of thenks to Dr. Law, remarked that be was glad there had been no apologies for the vlllsies of Seakespeare. In the latter part of the Victorian ern people con- tinually ' sPologiaed for Shakespeare's villains as for all other villians including Judge Jeffries sod he thought it was a mistake. (hther, hear.) Mr Wit SllERal`lta seconded the re ition, which was supported b) Alderman Besalsrr and enthusiastically carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SCARCITY AND HIGHER PRICES.</head>
        <p>SCARCITY AND HIGHER PRICES.</p>
        <p>By Mr. C. B Parker.</p>
        <p>There appears to ho, almost all over the civilized world, a rise in the price of foodstuffs. This rise is being felt in some countries much more than in others. Any general advance in price is natural') least felt in a country which can freely dr rw upon the world's supply. Consequently the gereral advance is Islet in Free Trade territories. Those countries which by Tariff barriers restrict their supply to their own products are more affected by Causer, natural and ificial, which influence prices upward. Free Trade dres not prevent rises in values due to natural causes, but it mimimises theirs because countries with open ports can freely drow from all the world. On the other hand the restrictions of Protection tend to aocentuate any advance, the advance is in'ensified, and the conditions created make it easy for holders to force prier a upwarde. The recent advances in the different countries amply confirm the above. In France we find three is a sidespread agitation launched by the housewives in which cm flirts have taken plsce b rte.; el troops and rioters ; sh rps have been raided end attemprs made by the buyers to fix their 07/11 prices. According to the " Temps "of Angus' 31st" every prohibitive tariff ought to be gradually lowered so as to restore free composition an quick :is possible. 'rho Protection madness of Europe will fa'l if itself when it has provoked extreme consequences." Even the " Drily Express " speaking of the trouble in the North of France, says " prices must fall or there will be a revolution." The " Lantern° " of Sep ember 3rd declares" To end the present crisis there must be a soppression of the en-to ra duty, in the general ri,terest of the consumers who Irmo Che large majority of the lab, tiring classes it will be nr cessary to adop'</p>
        <p>.:, . . this solution." In Austria, Vi tura, we have an unrest which has taken a violent turn for the reducoon of prices of fordetuffs, aid particularly for t e free import of all frozen meat</p>
        <p>In Spain there is an agitation of a like character wh'ch has obliged the Government to abolish, at any rate for the time being, the special taxes on foodstuffs coming in•co towns. In the United States the' increased price in giving strength to Vie iocial upheaval which takes the form of an orslau.4lll en the trusts. But the per pie are beginning to nee that what gives scope and power for mischief to the trusts is the tariff, and the movement to tear up dm Protective system by its roots is gathering force. In Italy prices have been steadily rising for some years, and the British Consul at Venice in one of his reports says: "The cost of living in this part of Italy may be said, without exaggeration, to have doubled within the last ten years. There is ale is great increase in price of all atanufa?tured commodities, for the high protective duties have killed competition." In Germany the facts are much the same. Herr Blume, a German, says "Germany's food in now the dearest in Europe, being all found, 7 rr cent, dearer than French food, and 15 per cent. dearer than English food." A German paper says that " the part played here by the recent drought is not denied by the Uneolll- promi,ing Free Traders, btu the resolutions that are now being pas-ed, instead of reproducing Providence, declare that the Tariff system had made a natural serial&gt; privation, an intolerable one, and they. call not for a change in the weather, but a change is the Tariff System." . . . .</p>
        <p>There is an interesting feature in regard to the German position at 'he present time. The grain crops this year have been exceptionally good, and yet the price compared with the world's natural price is as high as ever. The Berlin " Tageblatt " compared the other day the prices of a ton of wheat as follows : Marks. Berlin 211.50 New York 153.80 Liverpool Paris The price of rye, the staple for the broad of the ye tple, las risen in spite of the god hal vt at. Thi,, paper speaks of rye as folio. a : - - " The oafs, is well known. It lies almost exclusiee'v in a tariff policy which drives cern abroad and dumps it on the foreigner. What is the good of our fine rye harvest this year, when, witlt the aid of export premiums and cheap export railway rates, the excellent German rye is " dumped " as pigs' feod 1.1 Denmark and Finland, while at home we suffer privaion ? To-day as a grain trader writes to us that in Germany, on the Ruesian frontier, rye costs 175 marks a ton, whilst across the frontier Russian traders are baying the same grain from German traders for 125 markt."</p>
        <p>Perhapa the above needs explanation. Suppose German importer buys Canadian red wheat, and pays duty on the same. He gets a receipt or certificate for the payment of this duty. He then exports an equivalent value in rye, produces his certificate for . . . .</p>
        <p>&amp;duty paid on the wheat:and gets a return on the whole less a small percentage. The (Erect of this is that in Germany, with its bountiful harve-t of rye, the consumer has got tio benefit. The price in Germany is maintained, the exports are increased, and the foreign instead of the home buyer gpts the advaiitage. The German exporter f rye hare increased threef. Id during the last five years, while at the saute time both wLeat and rye bread have gone up in price. If the price of rye an stated is 175 marks a ton, it pays the German merchant buying rye from the German producer to pay say 165 marks, to sell thin rye to a Danish cattle feeder at 125, because with the rebate he is as well or better r if. than it he sold it to his own countrymen at the higher price and got no rebate. Means bile the Danish farmer, if the rye is of good quality will in- vert it into bread, ii of second or third qu Oily it is cheap enough to be mod to feed his cattle and pigs. From Denmark we get our largest supply of bacon. We import from that country to the value of over £6,000,000 annually, about half of our supply from outside sources. Tbe benefits, therefore, that the Dane secures in cheap pig food through the folly of the German. is shared by us through our heir g able to secure such a large supply of bacon from Denmark. The Germans are beginning to see their folly and the absurditirs of their fiscal system. The comic aspects of l'rotoction would be more in evidence were it not for its eel ions side. The laughter of the comedy is bushed by the wrongs it inflicts upon those least able to bear them. Is it little wonder that the only party shich as a whole stand for free importation of food- stuffs. I, mean the Social Democrats (I am aware this is only one of their planks) should be gaining all along the line, and largely through those who are not Socialists voting with them on the food question ? If the German franchise were on the saa.e basis as our own the next election would result in making a clean sweep of Protection,. at any :rate .as it affects</p>
        <p>foodstuffs. rTbia would be the form.. "Tariff Reform' would take in Germany. hi a very signifieant fact . . .</p>
        <p>that the entrees of jthe Sociat Kern-ne' rats is net confined to the industrial centresl but they are</p>
        <p>winning successes in purely agricultural districts, because the small farmer is beginning to realise that the benefits he was suppoicd to get from the protec- tion of his produce is more than lost in the higher prices he has to pay for what he buys, and especially in Ids foodstuffs for his cattle, pigs, and poultry. lt is a significant fact that we in England are importing yearly an in2reasing quantity of wheat from Germany and yet whist in Germany is usually about two pounds a ton dearer than with us, and bread is always dearer. Is this not a proof of my statement that Pr teal in has somethicg comic about it ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>p i i CHILDREN'S CORNER, a Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. Ni-"\e-1/4-rws.,—,•,-..re•e•v</head>
        <p>p i i CHILDREN'S CORNER, a Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. Ni-"\e-1/4-rws.,—,•,-..re•e•v</p>
        <p>THE LEGEND 011 THE PINE. BY MARY H. PHILBRICK.</p>
        <p>It had been along, happy day, but now the children were tired and while they waited for the conveyance which was to take them home from the picnic grounds they begged Aunt Eunice for a story. . .</p>
        <p>" What shall it be ?"Aunt Eunice asked. " About a pine tree," answered Harold, looking up into the gently swaying tops. " Yes, about a pine tree," chorused the other children, wild were resting comfort- ably on the pine needles. Sr Aunt Eunice began: In the garden of the Fairy Queen stood a beautiful pine tree, a small tree with long, slender leaves always dancing and rustling in the sunshine. The little tree never attracted much attention, but she did not care, and only tried to help and protect the frail, tender flowers which grew at her feet. One day the Queen saw what a lovely pine it was, and then she came often and talked to it, but instead of being very happy at this, and trying to pass on to others some of the kindn3ss which the Queen gave to her, the little tree became so proud that she drew her branches away from the other trees, and held herself haughtily. Soon, while talking with the tree, the Queen told her a secret and of this the little pine boasted, the oak said he, for one, did not believe it, the maple rustled her dainty leaves, while the chestnut shook with derisive laughter, even the birds pre- tended not to believe it. The sparrow teased and teased the tree, so that to prove that she had the secret she told it to the chattering bird, and it was a secret no longer. The Queen lost her temper when she learned that the pine had told her secret, and in a passion she struck the tree With her ward and said sternly: "May you grow so tall that your head will be too high for any one to tell you a secret, no one shall be able to climb into your branches and talk to you as they do to the other trees." The leaves of the pine curled up like needles with grief and remorse as she grew rapidly taller and taller, but the Queen did not relent, and to this day, up in the top of the pine tree one may hear her sighing for the happy times that are gone. THIS WEEK'S STORY. BLOWING HER HORN.</p>
        <p>Teddy had never seen a cow, being a city boy. While on a visit to the country he walked out across the fields with his grand- father. There they saw a cow, and Teddy's curiosity was greatly excited. " What is that, grandfather? he asked, breathlessly. "Why, that's only a cow," was the reply. "And what are those things on her head ?" was the next question. "Those are her horns." The two walked on. Presently the cow mooed loud and long. Teddy was amazed. Looking back, he exclaimed: "Which horn did she blow, grandfather ?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>I TRUE FABLE.</head>
        <p>I TRUE FABLE.</p>
        <p>A frown went scowling down the sircet one pleasant morn in May, And Is ys, will you believe it? Everyone he met that day, Man, woman, lad and lassie—it in queer, but it is true— As soon as they eime near him, went off a-frowning, too!</p>
        <p>Next day a smile went beaming down that very self- same street, And every single person that it winsomely did meet, Man, woman, lad and lasdo —and it went for many a mile— Jogged homeward all a-lasppy, wearing each a pleasant smile! Now from this little fable it is very plain to see, There's no one but has influence, wh^ever he may be And if you are cross and crabbed you make others crabbed too, And if you smile on others, they will likewise smile cn you! —By ADELBERT F. CALDWELL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>INSURANCE BILL.</head>
        <p>THE INSURANCE BILL.</p>
        <p>Chancellor to " Stand or Fall " by it.</p>
        <p>Dr. Macnamara, speaking in London on Monday evening, said he had that afternoon received a letter from Mr. Lloyd George, who said : " You may tell your friends in London that the Government means to press the Insurance Bill through during this autumn session. There is no ides of abandoning any part of it. We shall stand or fell by it ens whole. The sug- gestions that we should at this stage divide the bent fits, carry a third of them on to the Statute Book this session, and postpone the rest, is a particularly fatuous one. No one who knows anything about the , bill or its history could ever have made such a proposal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CONSERVATIVE v. LIBERAL.</head>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE v. LIBERAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Begbie, discussing in the special issue this week of" Public Opinion " (which commences its second half-century), thus defines Conservatives and I Liberals, He says ; " On the public platform the Conservative politician ii,, Polite and fluttering to democracy as any Liberal, but In the depths of his heart and in the privacy of his own soul he is conscious of suspicion and is profonndly aware of danger. He does not believe thst the Voice of the People is the Voice of God. Rightly or wrongly beholds that it is perilous to put questions of great pith and moment to the hazard of the billot-box. Examine the thesis of Conservatism—a perfectly intelligible and a quite honourable thesis—and you will surely (Ind that its ground and foundation is distrust of democracy. " The Libersl, upon the other hand, Is distrustful of the expert. He believes that a vast mass of humanity, representative of all classes and interests, is more likely to come nearer to a right and truthful decision than an aristocratic committee or a body of profersors. The Liberal is the man who prefers to put his case before a Jury ; the Conservative is a man who would prefer to take the arbitrament of a Judge."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a very good attendance on Sundry pre- sided over by Mr. Boyle, of Widnes, who said he remembered tVycl, Re Church as a boy in the days of , the late Her. 0. S. Keeney, and spoke of the good he received from him sod also from the address's of Mrs. Reaney. lr. Miller, of Widi es, gave the address upon " She bath done what she could' lie said be wished it had bees an open meeting no that ha could speak to the women as well. Woman had bad her share in the evil Influences of the world, end she ought to take her shsre in the removing of evil. Ile spoke of the good of notable women, namely bliss Floreatce lti‘htingale, the mothers of Wesley and Spnrgeon and all good mothers who were influencing the lives of their childrt n Mr. McArthur sang " Thou art passing hesice," and " Nearer my Clod to Thee." Mr. Payton was -it the organ. Mr. McF.schren reed the lesson and Mr. Miller lad the prayer. There was a retiring collection to the funds of the society. A most hearty meeting closed by singing a I will guide thee with mine eye."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Ridiculing the Law of Libel.—A short time ago the writer of a serial story was mulcted in damages for unintended libel. She invented what the fondly imagined to be an imaginary name for one of her characters. when upon someone who bore the same name, came forward and made a claim fur defamation. According to the law, the novelist or journalistic writer could only matte sure of being perfectly safe by giving his characters numbers instead of natnes, nod that is a ndlculous state of things, tending to bring the law into contempt. The serial story %hien was ineernipeed by these legal proceedings, is it appears, to be published in book form, in which the author gives her own name, Prothero, to the character which cost her no dear. Other characters are named after distinguished writers who have teat their names for this purpose, including, It is understood, Mr. U. B. Show, Barry Pain, Pet. Ridge and George R. Sims. The effect is to ridicule the low of libel, but the uncertainty of the law is almost as great bs that of the turf, and it is by no means sure that the fiction wiiter innate in substitutine real names lot fictitious outs.</p>
        <p>Women Writem—Many of the publishers' autumn lists afford evidence of the increasing number of women writers. In some of these it will be found that more than half the novels being issued bear the names of authoresses. Not all of them will bring the writers perpetual fame but the fact that so many women engage in authorship is a sign of the times. Except in the case of a few brilliant exceptions which prove the rule, it used to be thought "unwomanly " for a lady to openly appeal to the readiog public. It was this feel- ing that induced so many to appear under a male psendmym, like the Brontii sisters. There have been " Georges" innumerable, including George Eliot, George Sand, George Egerton, George Fleming, and a hest of others. The growing tendency of male authors to write as women, is a more modern development, but an example may be found in Margaret Smith, which yeas the pet name of the American poet .1. J. Whittier, and at one time Mrs. Horace Manners covered the identity of Mr. A. C. Swenhurne. Women's Inferiority.—" The Women's Charter," as drawn up by Lady Malatreo, was the subject of di. - mission at a conference at Hall last week, presided over by Mrs. Russell Rea and Mrs. Gardner. the dominant note at the meeting wes the demand that in everything wives should be put on something like equal terms with their husbands including a legal right to a share of the family income. Women of the present day are not inclined to underrate their importance in the scheme of things, and another demand was for the reform of the marriage service. According to Mrs. Gardner, the Church is, and always has been, at pains to ineist upon the inferiority of the women to the man, and the marriage service having been drawn up by mem exulted man at the expense of women. All this has boon debated many times before, and there is a good deal to be said from the point of view that the inequalities of the ,aw as between men and women, in respect of marriage, divorce, and other matters, are out of place in these twentieth century timer. The Church, like the State, has always been governed without reference to women, and some of the irreligious spirit of the day, and the neglect of public worship, may, possible he due to the resentment which this distinction provokes. Just in Time—Accidents must happen occasionally in Church matters, as in all others, out the clergy and ministers concerned usually prove equal to the emerg- ency. There was, for example, a mishap at Preston, where two weavers who desired to be married had unfortunately forgotten to gi•e notice of their attend- ance, with the result that there was no clergyman present. Happily they had come in good time, but it MIS not uutil nearly five minutes to three that the curate WWI Men to pass the church and was at Once informed of abet had occurred. He immediately read so much of the service as is neces•ary to satisfy legal requirements, and finishing this part of the ceremony just before the clock struck, proceeded to read the remainder. There have been many similar occurrences, and other accidents are apt to occur, which call for presence of mind. Velveteen Populae—Rough cloths are to enjoy mach</p>
        <p>popularity this teetson. The old-fashioned reefer, ievived last year under the name of ratine, makes ideal yachting and travelling wraps, and has led to a return of the shaggy materials with curly serf 'ma, and to zibelines ot fairy appearance. Tomo latter should be avoided by women w hose proportions leucite generosity I in the matter of solid flesh, for they add considerably to the size of the wearer, whilst smooth surfaces have the opposite effect, lint there who cannot wear shaggy cloths may choose that universally flattering and becoming material—velvet—which is to be even more popular this sinter than last. The new colours are particulmly beautiful, notably the shades of red, and a green which is equally becoming to the elderly woman and the young girl. There is a difference in the fashion of velvet thin winter. It is seen more frequently in conjunction with cloth of a floe and supple make out only an expert in dress can successfully carry out this scheme. Velveteen will also be much more popular this winter than lust, and singular will be the woman who hos not one dress 'of it in her wardrobe. lbere ins certain richneta in the texture of this material which is becoming to every style of figure, and beauty. It shows up the hair and complexion tis no other material at the same prices does, and the more it is worn, the lean it shows ibe marks of wear, which is it. otly defect. The softer the make the better the weer, for it is quite a mistake to imagine that a little " body " improves the quality. Then the wider widths in which it is now made, greatly reduces the waste ii. cutting out, since it is possible to cut to advantage, and still get the pile right side up, which the narrow width velveteen quite precluded. Old World Modes.—To-days fashions are of all periods and epochs, for designers burrow from each and all, as the whim takes thera—apparrntly. There is method however in the rradness, and the results are eminently satisfactory, since they combine the picturesque of the older periods, with the practical of the modern. The seventies are recalled in the longer coat with the border of for, which by the way, should be of a soft, variety, fox for preference, and the high collar that accompanies it.. The vogue for lace takes us much further back, when women knew how to wear it. Iho fashions of 1830 for instance, were elaborate, but there was beauty in the flowing lines, and the generous sweeps and carves, and really lovely old lace, in II winces and panels, not to mention shawls, were shorn to their best advantage, when displayed to the trailing skirts of the days of the Empire. The Menu that has received such a warm welcome, comes to on from these distant days, and in fine filmy materials, how becoming it is ! Moreover it is a godsend in conjunction with a big satin rose, in transforming an evening dress. Its soft blurring folds hide, completely a shabby bodice, and with • little foliage added to the rose, which should be deftly tucked into the folds In front, a raw dress emerges. In Paris, the harem skirt has developed into a mere divided shirt, the division hidden by a panel— serge skirts hems the latter of surah, or velvet, and lace and fine cloth serves the purpose or ether materials. It is doubtful if the women of these Islands will adopt the new model, although it is mid that Pariaiennes and Americans, are weaiiog it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>LICENSING APPLICATIONS REFUSED.</head>
        <p>LICENSING APPLICATIONS REFUSED.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Tranafer Sessions, a Monday, Mr. J. W. Knowles applied on I shelf of the Burtonwood Brewery Company for permission to make structural alterations to the Qunen's Head Hotel, High.etreet --Mr. Knowles mid the object of the alterations wee simply to give the tenant better lining accommodation. An order- taking that the valuation of the house should not be altered for oonipenvation purposes by the addition would be given.—Mr. Hooch applied for permission to undertake certain 'dim:lora] alterations at the prr Tiff 4 of the Row and Crown lea, Cloircb-atreet.—"llie Bench refused both applications, the chairman (Mr. A. R. Norman) remarking that the Bench considered the town was even yet oven-licensed, and their policy was to reduce the number of licences. He would nt bn justified in encouraging owners to spend money on prem iaes at present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME. "The Gay Cordons."</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME. "The Gay Cordons."</p>
        <p>The habituCa of the Palace and Hippodrome have enjoyed a distinct novelty this week in the shape of a fuii-sized musical comedy. rccupying the whole of the evening in one show. "The (lay Gortions " have paid their first visit to Warrington, and en Monday they were heartily received by a large audience. As a musical comedy the " Gay Gordons" does not impress us very favourably, f,r like so many produmiot s of its class it consists of • medley of mu-ic, mirth and mimicry, which are loosely linked together and leave no lasting impression. But as light fare it suited the Palace audience admirably, and applause was not stinted throughout the evening. In Act I. a shooting party from Mblrose Castle smear and among them is pretty Peggy Quainton, the heitess of the millionaire American, Andrew Quainton, who, for the time being, is the owner of the shooting and the occupier of the Castle. Peggy has original thoughts on the subject of marriage. Her wealth has tempted many suitors and she has rejected them all ; she wants to be loved for herself, by some honest, handsome and brave man, who shall know nothing of her position and prospects in life. In • moment of wild inspiration and girlish irresponsibility the changes places with Victoria Siddons, the daughter of the proprietor of • peripatetic Punch and Judy show, and who acts as " help" to Widow Janet McLeod, the tenant of the cottage on the left. Presently appears Angell Gramme, her foster-son, and a dashing private in the Gordon.. Ile and Peggy are soon in love with one another and swear eternal fidelity. Complications arise when Angus mine oat to he the missing Earl of Sfeltrose, aid finds himself less in favour as a suitor. But all comes right in the end after the maiden hats masqueraded in her brother's uniform, and Angus in that of a fellow-private when he should be doing the honours at Meltrone Castle. The second Act on the terrace at Melrose is distinctly the better of the two and gives most of the principals more scope for acting worthy the name. Miss Katie Tate, is admirable as Peggy, and pereonates the light-hearted Awe; ican gill with any amount of spirit and @ancients. Her chief song " Humpty and Dumpty " was enthuaimatically encored, and merited the recall. As Angus Graerne, one could not expect a better performance than that of Mr. Edward Leith, whose ready wit, abounding energy end' go" recall Seymour Hicks, the originator of the pet. He wan the life and soul of the piece whether in the role of Private Greene, or the Earl of Sfeltrose. The companion part to that of Peggy was pleasaotly pourtrayed by Mies Marie &amp;tensed as Victoria Siddone. Her singing and dancing in the second act were an attractive feature. Mr. J. B. Gordon looked a typical "millionaire," though hardly Yankee enough to be the father of the very " Amur:jean" Peggy. The Nervy Nat Punch and Judy twig e were a never-ending puree of amuse' mint, the leading parts being well sustained by M . Frank At don, W. H. Desmond, and Mien Madge Seymour. .A strong company of soldiers and guests make an adequate chortle in the more musical items and the comedy is excellently stewed and in iunted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>BABY'S DBAYIII FROM SCALDS.</head>
        <p>BABY'S DBAYIII FROM SCALDS.</p>
        <p>Jrssie Reynolds, the eighteee months old child of William Charlts Reynolds,' tram driver, of I.Tt, !Ryes- street, died in the Pro•idence Iloepital, St. Helens, on Monday. from maid" received on Saturday when the child pulled a can of hot tea over, which bad beero got ready to "end to ber father, and veal badly scalded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>wARRINGrroN BAND SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>wARRINGrroN BAND SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>CROSFIELDS BAND WIN THE NATIONAL CHAMPIONSHIP</p>
        <p>THE PERFECTION SOAP</p>
        <p>The people of War, ington heard with delight and pride on Sunday that the blue ribbon of the brass band world—the championship of Great Britain and the Colonies had been won by the Perfection Soap Works Band at the twelfth annual great band festival held at the Cr3stal Palace on Saturd.y. Ever since the bard began t distinguish itself in the competitive field, the members have had one supreme object in view - to win the National Championship—and now at the fourth attempt, they have achieved their ambition. In 1907 they were awarded the fifth prize; in 1908 they came within an ace of victory, befog placed see aid, and in 1909 they were third. Last year some difficulty arose and they did not compete. At last sound training and perseverance have their reward and to the many honours which have fallen to the "good old own" is now added that of producing the premier brass band el the Kingdom. By its magnificent victory the band bec,mes the IcAder for twelve months of the national challenge trophy of the value of 1,000 guineas. A ceitilicate for Laming and a cash prize of Xl° accompany the trophy and in addition, each member of the band, including the bandmaster and secretary, will also be presented with the Cryst il Palace Championship bronze medal, with bar and clasp. Two extra prizes are presented by private firms —namely a complete set of new uniform value .1:50, for the band, and a bandmaster's black or white patent leather cross belt, with pouch, whistle chain awl badge, for the amateur bandmaster. The wincing of the championship also carried us ith it two valuable London engagements which were fulfil'ed on Sunday afternoon and evening. While hearty congratulations are due to all the members of the band, omelet honour must be accorded to Mr. W. hlailiwell, their talented conductor,who not only had five binds among the seven prize-winnera in the championship section, but had also trained one of the winners in the grand shield section. The cult of the brass band has made astonishing progress in recent years, a result for which the Festival is largely responsible. The railway com- panies, it may be mentioned, refused to allow any facilities for the first contoat as they had little faith in the enterptise ; now, however, the companies vie with each other in running excursions. Londoners were utterly astonished when they discovered what splendid bandsmen the north-country working men were. They found that the various fashionable bands had to yield priority f place, and in short the brass bands from the north were a revelation to the south- country public The first conductor woe Sir Arthur Sullivan, and he was very loath to occupy that posi- tion. When he heard the magnificent performances of the brass bands, however, lie was simply astonished, and at once became most enthusiasic. That occasion, by the way, was Ida last public appearance. Lancashire and Yorkshire provided nearly one-third of the 1113 competit rs, but the latter county had by far the larger Lumber, for 43 Yolkshire bands were present against. only 10 Lancashire bands. For the principal competition, however, Lancashire and York- shire supplied six competing bands each, while Cheshire added another, leaving only ten from other parts of the country. Cheshire sent the champions of last year —Foden's Motor Works—and Lancashire supplied no fewer than three previous champions, two of which had won the thousand-guinea trophy twice. The result of Saturday's rented was to add another champion to the Lancashire list in the Perfection Soap Works Band. Since its institution in 1900 the championship has been won by the following</p>
        <p>band; :- 1900. Denton Origins! 1901. Lee Mount 1902. Black Dike Mills 1903. Besses 0' Th' Barn 1904. Ilebburn Colliery 1905. Jewell Springs 1906-7. Wingates Temperance 1908. Irweli Springs 1909. Shaw 1910. Foden's Motor Works In the championship section on Saturday the following competed in the order given :—St. Hilda's Colliery, Cleckheaton Victoria, Owann Cae Ourwen, Batley Old, Lot n Red Cross, Birmingham City, Norland, Rushden Temperance, Dannemora, Kettering Town, Kettering Rifles, Spencer's Steel Works, Wingatsa Temperance, Skinningrove Silver, Perfection Soap Works, Goodshaw, Irwell Springs, Shaw, Foden Motor Works, Gossage's Soap Works, Hebden Bridge, South Moor Colliery, Marton Colliery. But for the depression in the cotton trade we should no doubt have seen more Lancashire bands at the Palace. Those which did appear did them- selves credit, and even when they were not among the prize winners they played remarkably well. The test piece was Meyerbeer's " Les Huguenots" arranged by Mr. Rimmer, who has in recent years !seen responsible for all the championship selections. The piece bristled with difficulties, and it is safe to say that there are not more than three or four bands in the country capable of rendering it perfectly, and certainly few bands outside those entered for the contest could even perform it passably. The music therefore provided a real test of merit such as is needed in a competition of this character. The concert ball where the bands played was crowded dui ing the greater part of the afternoon, num'ans of the audience being unable to find seats when some of the better- known bands were playing. The competition was exceedingly keen, but (says the " Manchester Guardian ") there can be 110 doubt that l'erfection and Foden's stood in a class by themselves on their Saturday's performances. Many competent judges were in doubt to which band the prize ought to go. The result of the contebt was as follows : I. Perfection Soap Works, Warrington 2. Foden's Motor Works,Cheshite (winners of the n I lady Telegraph " challenge cup) 3. Wingates Temperance, Lancashire 1. Shaw, Lancashire 5. Batley Old 6. Spencer Steel Works 7. I.uton Red Cross The massed band yerformance is the econiug was even better than usual. The bands performing the selectioes were Batley Old, Foden's Motor Works, Gossage'm Scan Works,lrwell Springs, Luton Red Cross, Pesfectien Soap Works, Shaw, and Speneen's Steel Works. Irwell Springs, Shaw, and Fi den's each rendered the selection with which they won the champion cup during the last three years. A concert was preceded by an organ recital by Mr. David Clegg who is bell known to Warrington audiences. The baud programme was introduced by a stirring !m- -kt-mance of the fanfares played at the Coronation of their Majesties the King and Queen, and concluded with a remarkably fine rendering of the " Bridal Hymn " and the finale to the overture of " William Tell." The announcement of the result of the coolest, which was wade at the close of the concert, evoked great</p>
        <p>, t i tAyt•, , • —. 4 _ . _ . . . . .. • • •Ci , jaao \ :-..,-.. 4? •-/ t,-. , :. Ai_ ~ •,. -ric. 7 ..z., ~ r ..:... , . Ai, , ...L. • " . • • .r , . A« , . . • - 4t Ako j't 1 - .L.: 44 r 4 - 0 .1,, 44 ----- ' ' • ,/A i ~...• , / ---- • "• 4 I . - ~.‘ , , 77 ( . ~_ , ~., 04,44 'iy ve e iir,i 'lllO ' ' • ;r-- s -'T" i , (~,,,,-, -'l'4. ' A ~ ,so 1 , . , . . . g, W / , .141148di ' +, 9 i ,to -,, _ , ..., . t...t/ - . ~, , / .„4,..,, ~..„. .. ........... ‘-.-_,.. .... „.. -,.....,..._, . ~... ...., ..w......„. ~, , . , , I _ 1 ------- _.,____,, -..-- --</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WORKS BAND, WINNERS OF THE THOUSAND GUINEA TROPHY.</head>
        <p>WORKS BAND, WINNERS OF THE THOUSAND GUINEA TROPHY.</p>
        <p>enthusiasm, the victory of the Perfection band being extremely popular. Musiciaus who have followed these contests from the beginning were loud in their praise of the bands appearing this year. ['here never has ben such good music producei, and it is quite evident that these cmnpetiti na have stimult,ol brass bands to a remarkable extent. . . . .</p>
        <p>On Sunday the champion band (Perfection) gave performances in the afternoon at the Paladium and to the evening at Tottenham Palace, and Fuden's performed at the Stratford Town klill tinier the auspices tf the Sunday League.</p>
        <p>gave them admission to the Championship section in the following year. 1907 PaW the band again very Euccessfal for firsts were scored at Shrewsbury, New Brighton, York,&amp;c., the record for the season being, 7 firsts, 2 seconds and 3 thirds In the Championship contest at the Palace the band gained its first award. This wan only a fifth but it was evidence that the Perfection band was on the way to national fame. The two following seasons were marked by farther progress and may be summarised than : —l9OB, 11 firsts, seconds, 3 thirds and 2 fourths and find to Irwell Springs in the National Championship ; 1919, 11 firsts, 3 seconds, 2 thirds,2 fourths and 3.d to Shaw in the championship. The number of contests entered in lit 10 was some- what lesseoed but the quality of the band's per- formances marked an advance on their previous achievements. Their record was unblemished by a single failure a prize was brought home at every venture. The record was Seven tioiti, I second, 2 t irds, 2 fourths. Compared with the other cele- brated bands in respect to first prozes won during the year the Perfection stood clearly ahead : Perfection Soap Wo,ks, 7. Foden's Motor Waggoo Worts, I. Wingates, 1:</p>
        <p>CludahaW, Black Dyke I</p>
        <p>, . Irwell Springe, The trophies of the year included :—Dsrwen Shield, Derwen Cup, Lord Masher's Cup (Bradford) and the White City Shield, but the greatest triumph of all was the annexing of a double first at the Welsh National Eisteddfod. JUDGES REMARKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. 13es-1;4;k-it-the R.-Wales co, test. "An artistic triumph in bra,s baud playing." Mr. R , Stead at Damen. "The finest performanee I have ever heard." Mr. J. 0. Hu ne at Shrewsbury. " Per- formance moat beautiful. Soloites play flora their souls, truly a magnificent rendering." I ieuteuaut Miller. " The my best rendering or</p>
        <p>Weber'e work I have yet experienced. " In 1006 the Mayor of Calais invited the band to [day at tie annual Marine Fete. This visit lasted nearly a week and the band were adjudged the most successful and were awarded the French President's prize —a beautiful Sevres vase. The record for the season just concluded is firsts; two seconds ; one thi-d ; one fcurth</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>VICTORS' HOME-COMING. CIVIC WELCOME.</head>
        <p>THE VICTORS' HOME-COMING. CIVIC WELCOME.</p>
        <p>It was unfortuante that the home-c 'ming of the victorious bond on Monday evening synchronised with a drench:ng dowel) ur of rain. Th,,, however, did not lessen the cordiality of the welcome extended to the champions who have done such signal hotionr to the town by their achievement. The train in which r they tt availed from Eusten was duo st Bank Quay at 8.30 an I long before that time a huge crowd had assembled outside the station. Torch bearers were in waiting to head a proxession krough the pt incipal streets. When the victors made their appearance cheers were raise! and congratulatory hand-shakes were pressed upon the men by over-joyed friends. As befitted the importance of the occasion, a civic welcome was extended by the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph), who wis accompanied by Alderman A. Bennett, Gmincillor H. Roberts, the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) the Tkputy-Town Clerk (Mr. A. T. Ballaway), the Assistant Town Clerk (Mr. T. H. Spinks), Mr. T. A. MoBse, Mr. B. S. Townroe Mr. E. A. Whits and others. . -</p>
        <p>Court:tiler 11. Ron Etas, as a director of the firm, expressed his satisfAction !hat the Mayor had come to welcome the band and caletl upon his worship to address them. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Maros said he was very grateful to Councillor Roberts for the privilege cif receiving the band and addresaing a few words of welcome to them on their victorious return from the national challenge trophy competition. As first citizen of this good old town, it was a pleasing duty to congratulate them upon their splendid achievement. He ineluded in his congratula- tions the firm of which they were all employes. He thought it was very creditable to them, as working- men, that they should have performed that great task, especially as they hid competed upon such high grounds of musical art. He (the Mayor) was a great lover of music and there was no doubt that real classical music had a most elevating and refining influ- ence. If workingmen had musical taste and cultivated it, they could enjoy life as well as the highest in the land. One of the most gratifying features m.f industrial life was that many large firms were increasingly solictons for the social welfare and intellectual progress of their employes. Music was undoubtedly one of the highest methods they could employ to improve their liver and make them happier. Warrington was net behind other towns in the direction he had mentioned and no firm could take a keener interest in the welfare of their workpeople than Messrs. Crosfield. (Applause.) His worship concluded by thanking the band, on behalf of the good old town which be represented in its social, every-day life, for the honour they had done it by gaining the challenge trophy. It was, he believed, a real championship --(hear, hear) —and he hoped they would retain it for some years. (Applause.) Councillor ROBERTS apolog'sed on behalf of the chairman and deputy-chairman of the firm for their unavoidable absence. They . would have.. been</p>
        <p>delighted to be present if it had been possible and they asked him to convey on their behalf, as well as for the rest of the directorate, their sincere congratula- tions on that well-earned victory. Councillor Roberts continued : You have made your directors proud of you. All the workpeople are proud of you and War- rington is proud of you. (Applause). In conclusion he did not think they ought to separate without thank- ing his Worship for offering them a civic welcome and be would ask them to express their feelings in the way they usually did on such occasions by giving three hearty cheers. Cheers having been given for the Mayor and Mayoress, the band struck up a march and, beaded by the torch bearers, the procession traversed Parker- street, Sankey-street, end Bridge-street to the Lion Hotel. On all hands the victors were greeted with enthusiasm. SUPPER AT THE LION HOTEL.</p>
        <p>After the procession the band was entertained to supper at the Lion Hotel. The proceedings which were of an informal, social character were presided over by Mr. T. A Moans who read the following , telegram from Mr. Hasler°, a director. " Please con- vey to the band my heartiest congratulations on a magnificent victory." (Applause.) As regards myself, continued Mr. Mosey, I think all you bands- men know that for eight years I have had one longing desire, and on Saturday you brought it to pass. (Applause.) Now I feel satisfied. I will not say any more, but you know what I feel about it. (Applause). During the evening songs were given in excellent style by Mr. Morris and Mr. Pownall. There wan a special appropriateness in Mr. Morris's song" Keep on pegging away " and the moral was not loot upon the bandsmen whose perseverance has at last met with such a splendid reward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>'THE BANQUISTORY.</head>
        <p>'THE BANQUISTORY.</p>
        <p>The band ;sli.i'Cli now holds the proud title of champion, is a comparatively young organisation, having been formed eleven yearn ago. ha formation was greatly encouraged by Mr. Arthur U. Crosfield, who purchased the first set of instruments. The first throe years of its life were spent in earliest organisa- tion and preparation, and it was not until 1903 that the band tiied its fortunes in open contest. This was at Runcorn, and so well had they profited by the instruction t f their conductor—Mr. Halliwell -that they succeeded in winning the firet prize, a cup and cash. A new set of instruments was purchased and the band applied itself with greater diligence , to competitive work. The Compstall contest in 1911 was entered, and out of 22 entries the Perfection band stood second. This : was no disgrace far the first prize winners were Slaithwaite—a band of first-rate reputatim. There were many minor successes during this year but it was not until 1905 that really first-class competition sas entered. In this year the Junior Cup WM wou at the Crystil Palace contest—a result that was more than satisfactory and weedy encouraging. 1900 found the band amongst the premier organisations, fighting hard for honours and its is-accesses during the year some 5 firsts, I seconds, and 2 thirds. These included the second card for the Grand Shield Competition at the Crystal Palace - a success which</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Slowly Wasting Away</head>
        <p>Slowly Wasting Away</p>
        <p>THE DEADLY PROGRESS OF AN/EMIA</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams Pink Pills renewed my Blood and built up my system</p>
        <p>".It is painful for. ma</p>
        <p>to tell you all that I .bare</p>
        <p>suffered through blood- lessness, but I will tell you all I can in order to Ile I p o her sufferers."</p>
        <p>Thus' Mrs.</p>
        <p>9, Stanfoul St., Southampton,</p>
        <p>her recital' of 'the acts of her illneAs, from which she was rescued</p>
        <p>only by Dr. Williams, Pink</p>
        <p>Continuing hEr con- vernation with the re- , porter of the Southampton Times Mrs. Mazure said :</p>
        <p>—"About two years ago I MRS. MAZUR&amp; realised that something was . . . - .</p>
        <p>wrong with my health. On occasions I had the utmost difficulty in getting about, for my back felt very weak and my limbs seemed useless to support me. Then I began to suffer from headaches ; also palpitation of the heart became 133 distressing that I could hardly manage to get upstairs. Every night I lay awake for hours with dreadful headaches and bathed in pei spiration.</p>
        <p>. _ IT WAS .V. 1.10.11.1 WITH INDWESTIoN,</p>
        <p>"My appetite disappeared, and I could hardly take any food without pains torturing my chest. A doctor told me that I was suffering from Anicmia with Acute Dyspepsia. He gave me medicine, and for a time, I gained some relief, but soon was as bad as ever again. ' I slowly wasted away, and had given up hope of being well again, when my sister persuaded me to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. Sbe had been cured of debility by the Pills : but my case was mach worse than hers. " After taking a few boxes of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills the headaches disappeared, and I enjoyed restful nights. Soon my appetite returned and I could take full meals without any ill-efftet after them. "As I continued the treatment, I regained weight and the colour came back to my cheeks. In time the palpitation ceased, and I no longer suffered from fainting fits. Dr. Williams Pink Pills renewed my blood and built up my system, and my health improved in every way right up to a complete cure."</p>
        <p>11ER S.I;TER AND BROTHER CUREI.w. TOO..</p>
        <p>Not Only is Mrs. Mazure thankful for her own recovery, and the benefit her sister derived from Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, but also for the cure of her brother in France, who, after an accident, lost a great amount of blood. Amemia supervened, and his condition became critical. Ile took Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and, states Mrs. Mazure, " he made a rapid recovery, and owes his good health entirely to the blood .;iven him by them." Sufferers should write for special booklet" Diseases of the Blood," post free, on request. Dr. Williams' Piek Pills are obtainable of dealera, or from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co , 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, t!a. thl. for one box, or 133. tbl. for nix, post free. Ask plainly for Dr. Williams' Pink Pills and never arteept substitutes.</p>
