      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>SIR EDWARD RUSSELL ON LIBERAL IDEALS</p>
        <p>The Welsh Demand for Disestablishment.</p>
        <p>MR A H. CROSFIELD ON OUR NAVAL SUPREMACY.</p>
        <p>The menilw rs of tie Warrington Parliamenttry I I lebating Society wore on Tuesday evening honmred with a vi-it from Sir Edward Russell, the distin- guished editor of the " Liverpool Dsily Post and Mercury," who took part in the debate on the Kr g's Speech introduced by the Liberal Ministry. 0 ring 1 Ito tie great interest taken in the proceedings, the society held its meeting in the Co-operative Hall instead of in the Cairo-street Church Parlour. There was an exceptionally large attendance on both sides of the House and the' strangers' gallery" was also well filled. Mr. A. H. Crosfield app ared as the newly- elected member for Kilmarnock and had a c3rdial reception on rising to address the House. Mr. Carol Smith, M.P., wrote that he was unable to attend, owing to another engagement. The King's Speech, which was published in onr last issue, forecasts measures dealing with industrial dis- putes, the readjustment of local rating, ehctoral reform and unemployment. If time permits, attention will also be directed to a Bill to provide insurance against sickness and invalidity, a Territorial Amsnd merit Bill, an Education Bill, having for its obi ct a thorough national system of education, and a 1111 to provide a greater measure of self-government to England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales. TUE PRESIDENT OF 71111 BOARD OF TRADE (Mr. M. Howarth) in moving the usual loyal address to the throne, did not follow the usual custom of discussing seriatim the measures mentioned in the King'. Speech, but made an official statement on the Government's method of handling the recent railway dispute. Dis- cussing the employment of the military during the strike, he said the Government haul been accused of allying themsolvea with the Conservative and aristo- cratic classes against the interests of the workers. They most emphatically denied that. (Hear, hear.) In time pest the railway magnates had regarded the trade unions as pests, and if that autocratic spirit represented the way in which the railway companios or the Con- servative pa:ty were approaching the difficulty, a peaceful solution would be impossible. If the Con- servatives intended to maintain that attitude, they 'would find the Liberal party ranged on the side of Labour. The employment of the military had roused the ire of the Labour party. Could they hesitate for one , moment bet seen the interests of 100:000 or 200,000 men which could be better looked after in calmer moments when the strike was over,and the sight of over 40,000,00 drifting towards starvation, with fool rising to fanine prices ? There could be only one answer, and the Government said " Sufficient trains must be run to supply the normal food requirements of the country and we are prepared to use every force at our disposal to accomplish that object." The Government also sent sissistance to municipalities who were notable to cops with the disorder which way engineered more by the hoolisan classes than by the legitimate strikers. If the activities of the Government had ceased at repres- sive mg asuree, they would perhaps have been worthy of criticism, tite., thanks chiefly to the tact and wisdom of the Chancellor of the Eschequer,there existed to-dry s real live Commission whose report would serve as a basis</p>
        <p>plae!l</p>
        <p>for legal in in the near future. The Home must n be deceivel by.the apparent tram-0114y of the situation at the tre.re it time. Vast Rll.llBel of the workers were in a ft, brag state of unrest, and disoonle it, due to R multitude of cartres, such as unemployment, the q 103. Om of a minimum. wage, the housing. problem,</p>
        <p>--Six</p>
        <p>iotemeeranee, and perhaps moat of all, the %mid ques- tion. It was the settlement of those questione,towards which Liberal legidation was tending, that would 'meliorate the misery which so many of the workers suffered. The Government had now the knowledge that their schemes of social reform would eventually bemme law. They had no longer the sword of Damocleo—in the shape of the Home of Lords—bangiag over their bolds. (Applause.) The PRESIDENT OF TUE BOARD OF EDUCATION (Mr. G. Davey Smith), in seconding, said the great unrest in the social and industrial world did a great deal to create the war fever. The member for W. Birmingham (Alderman Bennett) claimed that the Tory party had • monopoly of brains and general intelligence. (Laughter.) The Government hoped to bring in an Education Bill which would be so equitable, just and fair all-round that there would be no active or passive resistance from any sect in the community. Having only the limited intelligence with which Alderman Bennett credited them —(laughter)—it might seem a big order to devise • scheme to meet the requirements or all, but they hoped their efforts in that direction would be successful. The LEADER OF THE OPPOSITION (Mr. W. IL Stansfield) said the mover and seconder of the address had excited his admiration by the wary mintier in which they had walked to avoid rousing the suscepti- hiliti'a of the various sections of the heterogeneous conglomeration known as the Liberal party. (Laughter and a VOICE: " Whet about your own party? ") The President of the Board of Trade had offered a kind of excuse for the Government's are ion in connection with the recent railway strike but they moot bear in mind the old French proverb " lle who excuses himself accoses himself." When the Conservatives some months ago were strongly advocating the building of Dreads. lights to compete with a foreign power the Chancellor of the Exchequer taunted them with not being able to sleep in their beds. Their action had, however, been fully justified by events and he doubted whether France would have emerged from the Morocco trouble with honour and peace if our navy had not been up to the fell strength which the Conservatives , demanded. They had even converted the first Lord of the Admiralty (Mr. McKenna) who had recently reproved the " little navy " and " perice at any prices' party. In the king's speech, the Government offered a curious billmf-fare. The chief cook had had too many assistants who aspired to be dictators —particularly the Irish and Socialist cooks—and he had been compelled in desperation to serve up a "resurrection pie." (Laughter). Members would recognize with a certain amount of distaste the ingredients they had dined of before. (Laughter). Mr. Stansfield instanced several measures in the King's Speech which he contended had already been passed and said the only conclusion be could arrive at was that, like most Radical measures, they had been found to be unworkable. (Laughter). The Conservatives agreed that electoral reform was needed—in Ireland, for instance, which was considerably over-represented. lie fancied Wales would be found in the same category (Laughter). With regard to education, we had already got a thorough national system which was working well, although there was at present a tendency to crush out the Church schools. What the Government really meant to introduce, however, was a Bill to crush out religious teaching, but they were Dot honest enough to proclaim the fact. Toe movement, be contended, was inspired by great animus against the Church's teaching. (" Question.") There was one serious omission from the King's Speech— Welsh Disestablishmea was not mentioned, although the question was occupying the attention of the whole country. That proposal also, he submitted, was due to hatred of the Church by a certain section. The reason why it was not included in the present King's Speech was that the Government did not want the people of England to know too much about it. The motive of Disestablishment was animus against religion—(No) - or at any rate against religious Leeching as propounded by anybody but the Free Church Council. Thinking men were coming to the conclusion that in view of the great arrest and the disgraceful scenes which had recently been witnessed, the present was the worst time to diminish religious education or disestablish the Weigh Church. Dealing with the proposal of Borne Rule all round, Mr. Stansfield contended that self- government meant eeparation—(cries of diseent from the Nationalists)—and if Great Biitain was to have three separate Parliaments, why should not Ireland, where there was a certain amount of antipathy between different sectione of the populace, have two ? Surely Ulster was as important as Wales. (Laughter). The PRIME MINISTER (Mr. E. Wynne Jones), said the object of the Tory party in these days seemed to be to foment religious prejudice and bigotry, soil the Government thought they had sufficient of that at the present time without introducing a Wallets Disestablish- meat Bill. The best way to bind the Empire together and extend its influence was to trust the people. Self' government had succeeded in South Africa, with far more bitter passion. (as Sir A. Conan Doyle pointed out) than ever existed in Ireland, and it would be a far greater success in Ireland. By suggesting two Parliaments for Ireland the Leader of the Opposition implied that the people of Ulster were in some way different from the tents of the other parts of Ireland. If plural voting were abolished, he (the speaker) contended that even in Ulster there wi ski lie a majority for home Rule. (Applause.) In the present House of Commons Ulster was represented by seventeen Unionists and fifteen Home Rulers. There were three swats in Ulster which would almost certainly go to the Nationalists if it were not for the ploral vote. With regard to national defence, the Liberals believed in. strong navy. When they came into ollioe they found at least twenty war- ships were useless and had to be scrapped—after twenty years of Tory government.. So much for the interest of the Tory party in the Navy. (Laughter). They shouted for Dreadnoughts, but when Mr. Lloyd- George provided for them in his Budget they objected to meeting the bill. As to the Leader of the Opposition's charge of disunion amongst the Liberals, there were plenty of divided sections in the Tory party. There were the "last ditchers," the " die hard.," those who believed in the referendum and</p>
        <p>those who would have Tariff Reform at any price and would not wait—but they had got to wait a long time yet.. (Laughter end applause.) The Liberals were as much end even more intereeted in the Empire than the Opposi 60,1 COUM possibly be. (Laughter.) They believed that the army end navy should be no strong that there would be no fear of aggression from any part. The Prime Minister went on to urge the necessity for a readjustment of local rating. Ihe gentlemen opposite knew how their friends hod for many years rhifte I taxation from the I tnd on to the shoulders of the people. That woe what titey called broadening the basis of twilit., ihnu.tht-tr ) The Cl tvernment were detmrmivel that the bu ritm should be placed on the shoulders hest able to hear it and that industry ehould be freer than it was to-day (Applause). They were going to take back for the com- munity in the way of land tale, that which het caged to the community and leave sacred to the individual that which Itelcmged to the individual. In dealing with the poor rate, thee would enure Lb it tan c inc 3 n wh,c wee responsible Ulr most of the pauperis u in the country bore the greaten bu,d tn. (Applansl). With regard to electoral reform, he meld at see why the man who had tsetity houses shonld be entitled to more votes than the man who had only one. (Rear, hear). It should also be as any to get on thn burgess roll las it wog to get on the rent bo ik (Lighter and appl ruse.) The Prime Minister c tackled wito a roferenoe to the enthusiasm for social reform which wan the great chancteristic of the prt sent day sof said the churches would have to tore an interest. In Poen' qoeetions if they wished to retain their 101 l lence over the people. (Applauaa.) Silt EDWARD RUSSELL'S SPEECiI.</p>
        <p>Sir Ec.—;;"triiirtit; -- ----------------------- trust reusptioo, said Parliament had always been a piati m of his— perhaps the strangest passion he bad Mil in the course of his life. It happsned that he was a mum 'Sr of the very first PAT litimAntary Dehating Socisty that existed in this country. Ile remold:wed its formation and the E 1033 of the eminent publio men who founded it had proved to be sound—namely, that it was a wnolesoine, healthy, instructive, cultivating and civilizing tbiog t 3 induce the ordinary mu of business, the ordinary oit non of the day, to occupy himself in public allure sod t do e, so far as he °mil 1, tinier the form of Parliament whioh in the course of his ory had estab- lished itself is the best p tic .1 m too I. lie was glad to find that they in W•rrington bad exemplified that theory to the foil. Toe Prime Minister, in his eloquent peroration oa social reform, had summarised whit hod become the new, the informing, and the inspiring principle of the Liberal putty, at any rate for some time 40 c,310. They must recognise that history moved—that it had its phases and its periods and that as those periods passed and phases succeeded each other, thme who were in charge of the Liberal interests of mankind had to devote themselves to special tasks. " I for one" c in. tinned Sir Edward' should be the very list person to advozate, to approve, or to rest content with any policy</p>
        <p>- - - --. • ..... ~ ..- ' ' ' - '.'-' i 1,,- „...› ~,, 7:4 ~.5...,,,..1,::,.2i10.,,,,:,_. , ~,,..," I , ' . -,- ..,.. , ..,. -:,... ' ›. , - - _ ;i * .4o' i ,</p>
