      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SIR EDWARD RUSSELL</head>
        <p>SIR EDWARD RUSSELL</p>
        <p>which merely seemed to be intent upon providing for physical and material needs. But, on the other hand, those who are in the poorer sections of society are very apt to sly—end with justice —that it is all very well for persons who are better oft to speak kr them in this way; but</p>
        <p>TREY KNOW !lOW Tll6 MON PINCIIISB from day to day, how they are stinted and deprivod sometimes of necessaries, always of luxuries and, until lately, ton large extent of education and every ci vili ring Whence—and deprived of those entirely by the lack of means. The Liberal party came to a point at which it , had made very greet exertions for those higher reforms which were not exactly of a materiel kind. If the Liberal party at the present day, if Liueral opinions throughout the world have any means whatever of relieving the physical nee Is which are, in many cases, a scandal, they owe that pwar to the fact that they have first devoted themselves to constitutional reforms, to the establishment of freedom, to the emancipation of the people, and to placing the potentiality of progress in the hands of those who need the benefits of progress. (Applause). So when we are sometimes rebuked or ridiculed for having a duly in view the material interests of the people, I for one am satisfied that for some time to c that should be a very prominent branch of Liberal effort. These are still great grievances of another kind to address. I, for oae, udVe ueld itlr many years that</p>
        <p>THE- CASE OF IRELAND</p>
        <p>is one of these, and that true national life cannot be expected from Ireland while, it is doprived of solf- government and while it is expected to conform itself to the ideas of other subjects of the British Crown, who are without syrup sthy for their owa cherished and even loved pecularities. 1 any there are still reforms to be brought about, improvements to be made and a higher condition to be reached on that loftier pisne which is not essentially attached to material conditions though we shall (Ind that one result of self-government in Ireland will bet, awry on that great improvement in , material conditions which has already begun there. The case of Wales is similar. It is very easy to dispute the propriety and the justice of an</p>
        <p>iNf AiCIPtTION OF RELIGION IN WALES, but you have to deal in Wales with a sensitive, a thin king and a self-educated race—a race which has prided itself for at leost 200 years on the continuous education of its best impulses and Its holiest powers in commotion with the Nonconformist denominations to which it has belonged. I have hod experience, and I have no doubt msny in this House have had the experience of learning from Welshmen of the humblest claases in their own country how they regord them matters, how they hold the sentiment of nationality, how they are conscious that the sentiment of netionllity has become a senti • ment of the family and of the cottage—a sentiment of the whole life of the people. We owe it to the Welsh to acknowledge that to an extent superior to a very great proportion of the English race, Welsh nationality has consisted in intellectual and moral elevation. The tVeleh, whether they are right or wrong, say that the existence of an Established Church in this c matry, to which only a minority of the people belong, and which by its very existence is,. rebuke and though not exvctly an insult, a lowering in repute of the general religious life of the country, is to them intolerable. Whether we enter into that or not, the Liberal party says it is required by Liberal principles that in such a solid and united com- munity as With», Ruch a sentiment should not be allowed to go unredressed. That is the principle upon which the Welsh Disestablishment Bill is going to be introduced. A noble lord spoke yesterday—a very much and deservedly respected nobleman, who I venture to say, scarcely ever in his public appearances is worthy of ,himsel. Lord Halifax said there were only two reasons for a Welsh Disestablishment Bill. One wee Mitt"d of the Church and the other was a desire to get votes. I say each a statement is utterly nnworthy and I think I may also say that it Lord Halifax had bem a member of a Parliamentary Debating Society all his life he would have had a better feeling for his opponents' sentiments, a better apprehension of their point of view. ' It is utterly untrue that even the Welsh people bate the Church. It is utterly untrue thot the Liberal pity hes introduced any such measure as this for the obtaining of votes. The whole statement is a figment, but what is note figment is that the conscience of the liberal party dictates such a measure and that that measure in one of the obvious and first duties of the Liberal party." (Applause.) Continuing, fir Edward mid some of the remarks of the Leader of the Opposition with reference to the Davy were open to very strong criticism. Ile (Sir Edward) denied altogether that there was any distinc- tion between the two parties as to the necessity of maintaining the navy. When he read ?Ir. Molienne's speech he thought it eulidently obvious and trite, although it weal very proper thing for him to say at Ike moment. Let them recognise that NEITIIE.R PARTY WISHED THL, iOCIPTRY TO SE DETEATE.I, .</p>
        <p>or swished the Empire to auffer or decay, and that all of them believed that the best way in which we should assert our power and maintain our supremacy was by means of the navy. There would 'ISIS'S be, and ought to be, discussions of an expert and timecard character. 'But let them understand, as mso respecting sack other, that thole were questions which could be discussed. lie was sure the Ministry ot that House would always cherish the maintenance of the Navy as a greet object which it would faithfully, resolutely, and, to the beet of its ability, carry out. There were subjects of the greatest satiety and difficulty—painful subjecte—which were now uppermost in the minds of all politicians. There was an soxious state of things at borne, and they had also to regard a sudden outbreek of the war spirit and of hostilities in Europe, producing, as all such eveuto did, a</p>
        <p>srwse OF i STRLVE DISAPPOINTMENT AND DOEST as to whether the progreas of the world would lox achieves,' or whether, at any rate, the exietence and the practice of war would not be, for many decades, if not centuriee, an obstacle to the progress of mankind. Let the Liberal party take he,rt in the knowledge that progress was inevitable and that, enlightenment must go on. That was a faith which they could not abandon, arid la them consider iris, whet elements of progress there wore oven in tire present noxious state of things. One thing in the question of war was that they were able to present—though it might he for the moment fruitless —a recipe which would pit an end to such evils if it were only adopted. The liugirn Convention was at flirt ti Reeled and the late Sir henry Caraphell-Blonsrinsn was characterized am a sick'y sentimentalist for encouraging the idea. But it had grown and developed, and it had sunk Into the mini, of people sad into the miuds of People,</p>
        <p>I ad subtedly there was a feeling at the back of most men's minds in all the countries of Earope that if only the flawed frailty of rushing into war on provocations could be overcome, they would bare in the Hague system • ea.isfantory and perrosnent device for settling international d Roe-sees, just as every country had in it, law courts and jadici try R means of Bottling differ- enCeS between indi•ido.de. 11. urged the n not to ,ive tip the i let. It tot often hid happwsel dist nation were suddenly plunged iot s tie war fever by events which appealed to their sense of right and wrong or or acquisition. Let them not suppose that that was going on for ever. It was • s sand propMcy lb it some morn- ing the world will woke to the fact of the</p>
        <p>urTg ABWRDITY AS WELL AS THR ABOMINABLE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>CRURLTY 0? WAR</head>
        <p>CRURLTY 0? WAR</p>
        <p>" It is shim -I" a k bard" becaute might proves nothing and wrong has just as great an advan- tage as right can have in the use of martial means. As to the rm. look at home, I confess it is difficult to over-</p>
        <p>comes feeling of snot depression, but even in this we see an advance. Principles are discussed between men who ho,, both sides of the question. It is true that when thsy hove born discussed, there come up ugly facto—slide.' things, just like the outbreak of war heiwoeo !Lily and Turkey, which for the moment seem to thro • to the winds all the good that hos been done. I invite not only the Liberal potty, but the waole of this Eloise, to have faith, outage and hope in this motter also. I) p sod up l- it. we shall flad that sv`iile on the one limit the wants of the people, the necessities of the p rot and the con• tingencies of humble life press s that it is not astonishing that sometimes they nosy lead to sots of revolt—yet, in spite of this, a gratis! an Igor Woo of ideas, a gradual enlightenment of all &amp;awes will bring as to better state of mind in w doh reason will be heard on both sides and an enlightoned administration of public affairs will be molded at once to preserve order and to do the right." (Applause).</p>
