      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>MR. cao3riELD NAVY.</head>
        <p>MR. cao3riELD NAVY.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. If. CROSVILI.II (Kilmarnock), in prolamin • vote of thanks to Sir Edward Russell, said that It W 44 the first time It, had represented a So stiab constituency and he W 45 a frail ho dare not attempt the Northern Gselic. (Loughtar.) 113 hid nitha I Oa honour of addressing th it House before and be believed it was customary in the case of 'the first sin mth, set and maidenly trarogres3ion on deb ate "to have a little preparation in which to polish off one's perfervid and glowing peroration before firing it off. (Laughter.) He had not had that advantage on (he present °elision, as he was unaware that he would he o:pentad to make hie maiden speech. (laughter.) lie would, however, like to refer to same very p iintod and emphain remarks ihat fell from the L-oader of the Oppwsition on the ganglion of notional defence, ',ammo he (tir. Crosfleld) 1•44 afraid that the unhappy Mate of unrest that existed through, tot Europe might he made a pretext for demands In the direction of</p>
        <p>ISK,ESSIVIC MILT fA Rlsm</p>
        <p>-.„.. of wh'ch they hod Mara s noething in Warrington bairn now, and whinh he wee bound to ray his friends on that aide of the If ions were resolutely determined to combat, (Hear, hear.) S.r E hoard Russell had 'waken the mind of the (mammas majority of the Liberal party, as well as of the Oppntition, when he sail that all responsible Ministers in this oruantry were determined to maintain what Mr. Asrpaith had called the unchallenged and unchallengeable supremacy of the navy. The Liberal party in the House of Commons took a pride in determining that each man in it should think and judge for himself in these questions, and ha freely admitted that whenthey wan the naval espenditore mount up from .C 31,000,000 to .0 11,009,000 in a very few years there was grave consideration and searching of hearts. He had had many opportunities of damming with the First Lord of the Admiralty, as well as with their distinguished Minister for War, what seers the absolute necessitiee of the country. It was perfectly ridiculous to go talking helm, constituencies, as too many people did, as if we were living ins state of unpreparedness, dangerously esposed to any attack from the first nation that chose to try to moved rue. This country was NEVEK IN A HEIFER STATE OF DEFENCE - .</p>
        <p>than it Was to-day. Our preponderance of naval power was overwhelming, and it was a striking fact that so Radical and no democratic:, a 0 avernreent as we had had in recent years, had deliberately sanctioned such large expenditure on national defame. lie admitted the,. had been same difference of opinion, bat a. far he was concerned be never gave a vote or said a word that would hamper responsible Ministers from undertaking the expenditure decided npqn. there was not a man in the party who hated that wasteful, unproductive expenditure more time he had dons, but having regard to the p wition we had to face in Europe he thought the expenditure bad been justified. Ant yet he did not, hope one whit the lees that we should live, before many years were over, to see the realization of what had been no eloquently fore- shadowed by Sir Edward Russell. He was certain that not only amongst the so called governing classes, bat amount the democracies of Europe, the idea, to which Sir Edward had given expression were rapidly spread- ing. It was significant that when much was beard a few weeks ago about France and Germany going gaily Into wer, there was a meeting in Berlin, addressed by Herr Rebel and attended by hundreds of thousands of men, protesting against the criminal fatuity of war between civilized white peoples. Having pointed with satisfaction to the arbitration treaty with the United.. Staten which leas</p>
        <p>than a decode ago would have been regarded as a dream impossible of fulfilment, Mr. Cr. 'ld said the Government was entitled to the confidence of Parliament first because it was encouraging and giving practical effect to progress in the direction of arbitra- tion, and, secondly, locate° it wag maintaining to the full extent every rational means of defence that we should require for the secutty of the country and the Empire. (Applause.) In proposing the vote of thanks to Sir Edward Russell, Mr. Crosfield referred to him as a 111,{11 full of years and honour., who was distinguished as a man of letters, a politician and a steteam in. In these days when a section of the Press was inclined to encourage a fragmentsry, fissiparous habit of mind and when a great deal of flashy and trashy reeding sets offered to the man in the street, it was not too easy for a journalist to maintain that high level of literature which we had been proud to regard as one of the attri- butes of the British Press. Their respect for Sir Edward was deepened because he had always msnifeeted in his journalistic work the same fine tone which had characterised his address tbatesening. Ills political work bad been relieved by his love of the drama- - though whether he (Mr.Crosfield) was correct in men- tioning that by way of antithesis to politico, he did not know ; Perhaps Sir Edward would tell them there was no greater drama than was to be witnessed in the groat political orena at the present day. 'They were also familiar with Sit Edward's critical faculty in the realm of musics On all grounds they were proud to welcome a man of such varied attainments and accomplishment, and they thanked him for the truly eloquent, instructive and inspiring address he had delivered. (Applause.) The Rev. E. C. N. CAnterroot (C., Truro) se :ended the vote of thanks. lie said the workingmen who recently went on strike bad diaeovere I what the Conservative' had been tilling th.':a—thatch.-np commodities must cheap and perhaps awe 'tad labour. Cheap goods, wherever they came from, were certain to have ths effect of cheapening labour and lowering wages. Referring to the subject of land valuation in the King's Speeoh, he mid that the members would probably remember a challenge which he threw oat on the subject of site valuation 11,1t118 time ago. Site valuation had never been properly explained on the Liberals. le and when it, might have been explained by Mr. Lloyd. George the only thing he did sires to go up and down the country blackening the character of the Loris. Tee speaker went on to refer to the constitutional ques- tion, and expressed surprise that no reference was made to them is the King's Speech. lie contended that the King's surrender to the pressure of the Government left no security to the people for the observance of the laws upon which their long-inherited liberties depended end the only guarantee WhiCli WWI contained in the Coronation oath. Meanwhile the people were ender an absolute government. Mr. Asqoith and Mr. Lloyd George could do as they liked. The King had no check,, tine Lords could do nothing —( Liberal applatme)--and the people need not be consultel. When the Government had got • veto on the Courts of Law the subjection of the people would he complete. Things could not continue as they were. We paid for a real Sovereign and as soon as people began to find out that the Sovereign WWI only a figure head they would begin to talk of the expense. The Government made a pretence of' going" for the Howe of Lords at their last election, but their Idea of achieving that object was to get permission from the King to create more Peers. Now the cat WWI out of We leg. They seemed to have been arranging all along for the King's humiliation and a snare lo deprive the people of their liberties. The vote was silpported by Mr. Block - (N. Donegal) • and heartily passed.</p>
      </div>
