      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>°Or)" WILL-- 7u. FILtTIC</head>
        <p>°Or)" WILL-- 7u. FILtTIC</p>
        <p>NOTES BY " MUST."</p>
        <p>The Frawley case is in anything lint a satisfactory icaition, notwithstanding that on 'ffiesday the Union tecided that he NV. Warrington's man. After a three tours conference, attended by both Colonial and Narrington representatives, the Committee came to be decision that Frawley, being a member of the Narrington Club at the time the rule was passed and ant into practice by the Northern Union in November, ,009, is not eligible to play for the New S.,uth Wales League or any other club without the sanction of the Narrington Club. In the circumstances it is mpossible for Frawley to tike part in any of the games .1 the present tour of the Australians.</p>
        <p>Bat though Frawley is Warrington's player be has 1 t signed on for the club this Reason, and therein a wide gulf to be crossed before he dons the Warring- ton jersey once more. The Australasian managers 'bowed considerable temper on Tuesday, but on Wednesday. Frawley bad an amicable interview with the Warrington Committee and exp'ained that he was ander an agreement with the Colonials which would have to be the subject of negi tiition. The Warring- ton committee arranged a special meeting with the Colonial inanagers Lir the following evening, bat they never turned up, so that the situation remains some- what strained. The feeling of the Warrington officials sto leave the re-opening of negotiations with the Colonials, if the latter were wise they would try to secure the player :or the test matches, and let him sign on for Warrington for the rest of the season.</p>
        <p>- - -- Owing to injuries to Carter ad Creevey, the Warrington team to go to Leeds has had tube altered. Cuswo th c,mes in again at centre, and Daintith will work the *crams for Brooks. Team full-back ; Bradshaw, Tailor, Unsworth, and Woodburn, thierquarters ; Daintith and Brooks, half-back- ; Johnston, Thomas, Shugars, Cheaters, Btockleburst and Arnold, f, rwards.</p>
        <p>-- The "A " am to play at Wilderspool will contain Papalmra at full-back, and Waywell at threequarter. The Maori has been doing some training in London, and it is hoped he will b 3 in better form than on his first appearance. Team : —Papakura ; Waywell, Dickenson, Critchley and Povey, threeparters ; Kenny and Jordan, ball-backs; Naylor, Skelhorn, Newton (Widnes) Harmer, Trauter and Bradshaw, forwards.</p>
        <p>Warrington's display against Swinton was anything but convincing. The forwards scrummaged very feebly and tv,re badly beaten hr possesiion, while the combination am ,ng the backs was scrappy. Luckily, Swinton were a shade inferior to Warrington and had to retire defeated by 13 points to 8. On the day's p'ay', however, an impartial observer would decide that two poor teams were in opposition. It was the worst game Warrington have played this season.</p>
        <p>What struck me as being the fault with the front rank was a lack of honest scrummaging. Take the composition of the six. Thomas and Johnston have a reputation for " winging"; Arnold and Brocklehurst are lightweights, and we have only Shugars and Cheaters who can be expected to do much in the pack remedy is an obvious one, and I honed a more solid front tank would be selected for duty against the burly Leeds six to-lay.</p>
        <p>An early injury to Creevey's knee pc.bably prevented Warrington increasing their score, for on several occasions he was unable t 3 do much more than pass the ball, though his try in the second half was the result of a wonderful swerve at top speed. Broke made several dashing effotts, once without any support though a try was waiting. The secret of the Colonials SLICCOFS is quick passing and nod backing-op. Warrington frequently fail to support the man with the ball, and a certain score has been thrown away in eve!), match this season.</p>
        <p>A word of praise is the due cf all three debutante who made their first appearance for the senior team at Wilderepo I. Ari.o'd and Brocaleburst followed op in a way reminiscent of Mason, and their tackling was of the downright order. At centre, Carter played a most promising game, particularly on the attack, and be is well worth persevering with. Taylor showed some of his old form, Bradshaw was much more dashing and resolute, but Woodburn was practicall neglected. Since his first match the Coventiy player has had few passes which have been the slighest use to him. Our halves and centres seem to have lost the att of feeding the left wing, since Jack Fish retired from the scene of his triumphs.</p>
        <p>Swinton struck me as only a poor side, the forwards being better than the backs. Dai Davies was a "frost," in my opinion. Be did nothing bat snap up the ball and heave it to his partner. He was never guilty of running ten yards with it all afternoon. He ought to be put out,ide half and made to work. Most of the Manchester papers referied to his brilliant game. Well ! Well !</p>
        <p>The League table up to dates as follows: For. Against. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. 0. T. P. &amp;neg. 'Wigan IHalifsx Hull K. K 5 4 1 0... 16 11 65... 3 3 15 8060 Rt. Helens 5 4 1 0... 10 18 74... 3 6 24 8060 HudderafTld 6 4 1 1... 16 27 113... 7 9 41 75.00 Broughton 4 3 1 0... 7 2 20... 5 5 25 75.00 'Widnes Wakefield T 5 3 1 1... 8 18 70... 6 5 27 7060 Oldham 6 4 2 0... 12 21 96... 14 19 85 6666 Warrington 6 4 2 0... 7 11 47... 9 12 54 6666 Hamlet 5 3 2 0... 11 15 67... 6 4 24 6060 Rochdale H. 5 3 2 0... 9 12 54... 4 12 41 60.00 Dewsbury 5 3 2 0... 7 9 41... 7 10 41 6060 Keighley York 4 2 2 0... 7 5 29... 9 12 54 5000 Batley Salford 5 2 3 0... 4 8 32... 10 12 56 0000 Hull ...... 3 40... 5 28 114... 812 52 4785 Leeds 8 2 4 0... 7 18 62... 15 21 93 3333 Barrow %acorn Coventry Leigh E 1 4 0... 9 9 45... 10 14 62 2000 Swinton 5 1 4 0... 4 7 29... 11 11 55 2060 Bramley Ebbw Vale 6 0 5 1... 7 7 35... 17 26 112 833 Bradford 6O 6 0... 3 5 21... 20 91 133 0060 On Monday, the Warrington crowd will have the honcur a a County match—the first—in its midst. Lancashire will oppose Cumberland at Wilderspool, nod much satisfaction is felt at the recognition of Brook's fine form as scram half. On this game depend his chances of figuring in the Out matches against the Colonials.</p>
        <p>The teams for Monday will be : Lancashire.—Sharrock (Wigan) ; Miller (Wigan), Wild (Broughton Rangers), Jenkins (Wigin), Reid (Widnes) ; Brooks (Warring* ,n), Thomas (Wigan) ; I(amsdale (Wigan), Winstonley (Wigan), Avery Oldham), S Aspey (Widnes), Logan (Barrow), Briers (St. Ileince). Reserve: Ruddick (Broughton Rangers.)</p>
        <p>Cumberland :—Davidson (Broughton Rangers and Aspatris); Bouch (Broughton Rangers and Aspatria), Loma (Oldham and Maryport), Kitchen (Hudders- field and Wath Brow), Trelaar ',Barrow and Millom) ; W Dixon (Oldham and Ilaryport), Price (Barrow and llom) ; Ferguson (Oldham and Brookland Rovers) Owens (Oldham and Seaton), J L and R Clampitt (Broughton Rangers and Millom), Clark (Huddersfield and Bunkland Rovers), and Ward (Leeds and Egremont).</p>
        <p>The Anaralasian side play their first international to-day, against Wales at Ebbw Vale. The Welshmen have one of the strongest sides in the Unicn and shi.uld fully extend the visitors.</p>
        <p>The League games for to-day are : Batley v. Halifax. 'Wakefield v. Bradford. Keighley v. Brantley. Broughton v. Oldham. •Salfori v. Coventry. Huddersfield v. Dewsbury. Hull v. Hull K.R. Barrow T. Swinton. York v. Hunslet. ...Leede v. Warrington. Leigh Runcorn; . Rochdale v. Wigan. ' ligaridnes v. St. H.leç -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Results of Saturday's Matches.</p>
        <p>NORTELERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>O.T. P. G. T. P. Hamlet Brumley .. ...... .... 0 1 3 Widnes Hull • 3 4 18 Bradford North 0 0 0 .y Hull Kingston It 3 521 York Halifax 2 2 10 Haddonfield 2 2 10 Coventry 2 210 Keigbiey Warrington 2 3 13 Swinton Wigan Leigh 01,10 am 3 6 21 Rochdale H.... 0 2 6 Runcorn Ebbw Vale 0 1 3 Wakefield Trinity 0 1 3 YORKSHIRE COMBINATION. Leeds A 1 6 20 Purston W.H... 0 2 6 Batley A. — 21 Dewsbury A.... 0 0 0 Huddersfield A 2 2 13 Halifax A 0 3 9</p>
        <p>De'robin</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE OOMBINArioN. Leigh A 1 0 2 St. Helens A... 0 0 0 Rochdale HA 9 Oldham A 0 1 3 Wigan A. .—. 6 Salford A 0 0 0 Swinton A. — 12 Warring.° A 0 1 3 Widnes A 0 0 0 Pemberton ...... 0 0 01 1</p>
        <p>AUSTRALIANS TOUR. Australians At,alhaa</p>
        <p>Warrington Beat Swinton. The Swintoa mon paid their annual •isit to Warring- ton on Saturday, and were represented by a strang team. Warrington turned out as selected, but the team was to some extent experimental as Carter (centre) Brocklehurst and Arnold (forwards) were all promoted hoes the "A " team and given a trial in senior company. Beaatiful weather prevailed and there WAS a crowd of 6,000. Teams IVarrington—J H. Tilley, back; B Brad.haw, I Taylor, S Carter and W Woodburn, three-quarter backs ; E Braokes and M. Creevey, half hicks ; DI Johnston' F Shugars, J W Cheaters, A Brocklehurst,J Arnold and Thomas, towards. Swinton—it Johnson, back : J Parker, A Valentine NI Evans and A Morris, three.rpitrter racks; D Davirsv and J Wharton, bolt backs ; Blows, J Bailey, D B Davies, T Graven, W Preston and C Randall, for sards. Referee, Mr B Eanion. Wigan. Tonch judges, Messrs E Williama, Widnes and T Foulkes, St. Helens.</p>
        <p>Swinton had the advantage eta crow; breeze so were soon operating in the Warrington half. Fine punting by Tilley drove them back to midfield where the War- rington halves tried to open the game out. A series of clean exchanges between Brooks, Creevey, Carter and Woodburn made bead cay, but the visitors tackled well. Valentine was prominent with a neat run and punt into the Warrington router. but Johnston led the way back again. Morris made a line effort for Swioton but was well tackled by Bradshaw. Woodburn got away on the left only to be held at the corner. The same player tried a penalty shot at goal and was very near the mark. Se iaton got back to centre, and Davies passed out to his centres. Taylor was handy, and snapping up the ball ran half the length of the field before passing to Bradshaw to score a good try at the corner. Thomas fei.ed at goal, and Warriugton crassel over leading by a try to nil. On renaming the locals male capital use of the wind and were aeon hot on the attack in the Swinton qnarter. Early on a floe round of planing saw Carter serve Bradshaw excrellently and the wing man rounded the defence to score behind the posts, Tnomas kicking a goal. War:ington kept no, the attack and again Woodburn was unlucky to be stopped at the corner. Brooks broke away in tine style but was not supported, and a chance went begging. Davies led the Swinton men back, and lively work was seen in the homo quarter. The Blues got the ball out and it was worked across to Morris, vho scored in good position and added a goal. Warrington at once took up the running again, and Creevey receiving from Brooks completely bluffed the Swinton centres and can over unopposed, no gnat resulting. Swinton again rallied, and Brooks dropping a catch near the line give Parker tbe chance to race up and dribble over at the corner. Warrington had the best of matters to the end and Thomas increased the score by dropping a penalty goal from the quarter line. Final : Warrington, 2 goats, 3 tries (13 paints); Swinton, 1 goal, 2 tries (8 points). ST. HELENS SURPRISED AT HO3IE.</p>
        <p>St. Helens entertained Leigh before a good "gate." St Helens started against, the wind and sun, and, within a minute of the kick-off, sfolyneux kicked to the line, where Brown and Tom Johnson failed to gather, and Prescott forced his way over for 'cumin to improve. , Immediately upon resuming a similar state of affairs was enacted at the St. Helens' end, Davies crossing for Leigh, through a mull by Toole. Gulley improved. Continuing aggressive tactics, Lowe forced his way over for Leigh, Ganley failing ; and later St. Helena were penalised close in for an informality, and Gtoley placed One goal. Half-time 000re:—Leigb2 goals and 2 tries (10 points), St. Helens 1 goal and 1 try (5 points). Resuming, St Helens at once got dangerous, and from a passing bout by their backs Turtill dashed over at the corner with an unimproved try. Hanley dribbled half the length of the field, and eluding 'Fortin scored, but Bolewski failed to augment. Play was kaenly con- tested,and Trenwith ac wed for St. Helens near to the poste, Turtill failing. The last ten minutes was all in favour or St Helens who strove desperately for the lead, but they were held at bay by the grand defence of Leigh. Result :—Leigh 2 goals and 3 tries (13 points), St Helens 1 goal and 3 tries (Ii points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>WIDNES DROP A POINT AT BRAMLEY.</head>
        <p>WIDNES DROP A POINT AT BRAMLEY.</p>
        <p>The game at Bramley, where Widnes were the visitors, proved interesting throughout. The attend-</p>
        <p>once waa about 1,000. - At the outset Bramley, with the sun and a strong wind in their rear, had to act on the defensive, but Orton twice tackled Reid. A free kick enabled the Widnes forwards to dash for the home line, and Johnson was nearly over. The Bramley forw ads. with Stead, I Samos, and McManus prominent, rushed to the other end, but Jones clearel away the danger. Half-time arrived without any score. After some mid-field play Orton received from Hope and kicked to Jones. Stead, following up smartly, charged :knee's kick 15 -yards from the line, and ran over with a capital try. Walker failed at goal. Clever passing by O'Gara, Reid, and Lally enabled Widnea to deliver a strong attack on the Bramley line, but a Fen iity bat the advantage. Bramley were Boon dangerous again and Tinton crossed, but owing ta a knock on there was no score. The visitors worked their way back by short quick passes, and from a scrummage Noon ran and passed to G. Aspey, who scored the equalising try. Taylor could not add the extra poiats. Result :—Bramley, I try (3 points), Widnes, I try (3 points). RUNCORN v. BARROW A DRAWN GAME. The sweeping changes made by Ruuoorn in their back division proved of some advantage, for, on their ground they showed a distinct improvement against Barrow on Saturday. From the kick-off they went for the Barrow line in strong fashion, and it needed some determina- tion to keep them out. The first scare fell to the visitors. Treloar gaining a smart try after a good bout of passing by the home backs,Ssvage failing to take his pass, and giving Treloar his opportunity. The only other score of the initial half was a splendid goal by Logan from half-way. In the second half play was evilly divided, ant at times became very vigorous. Logan landed another fine goal, and Padtm-y got over with a crowd of opponents, and wall awarded a try for the home team, Richardson landing a splendid goal. Goal-kicking was the feature of this match, Richardson drawing level with an even still finer goal, following a scrummage for infringement. The fear goals obtained during the match were all from difficult positions. Bath sides strove bard to obtain the lead, and the game became exciting in its closing asses. Result :—Runoora 2 goals and 1 try (7 paints), Barrow 2 goals and 1 try (7 , points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A'S " FIRST DEFF.AT.—Tbe Warring- ton " A " team was defeated tot the first time this season at Swinton on Saturday. Early on, Dawson of the home team dropped a goal, and following scored a try. A rally by Warrington resulted in Dickenson making an opening for Skelhorn, who socred at the corner, Daintlth failing at gal. After this. Fletcher, the Warrington full back, had to retire injured, and Howard and Edsforth scored tries for Swinton. Final: Swinton "A," 12 points; Warrington A," 3 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RYL 4.NDS REC., V. MANDLEBURGS.</head>
        <p>RYL 4.NDS REC., V. MANDLEBURGS.</p>
        <p>The above teams were opposed at Manchester on Saturday, when the Recreation obtained a run-away victory of 16 points to dil. They ware superior to the borne team in all departments, the combination of the , backs being of the best quility. and there win being a I most desereiog feet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>For all Diseases of CHEST, LUNGS AND THROAT there is no medicine to equal VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</head>
        <p>For all Diseases of CHEST, LUNGS AND THROAT there is no medicine to equal VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Undoubtedly the most successful method of caring all diseases of the chest, throat and lungs is the Veoo's Lightning Cough Cure method. Having now stood a 14 yearn test, and awarded the Grand Prix and Gold Nfedal for purity and efficacy at the recent Paris Health Exhibition, Veno's Lightning C nigh Cure is suitable for the youngest child, it will not upset the weakest stomach, and is now the standard re uedy for coughs, colds, tronchitis, asthma, nasal catarrh, whooping cough. blood and pbelgru spilt ing, and difficult breath- ing. Price 91d., 1, le and 2,9 of all chemists.</p>
