      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>ORFORD ST. JOHN'S v. LEIGH VILLAS.</p>
        <p>--• The above match was played at Orford Avenue last Saturday. Leigh Villas won the toss and played with the wind the first half. After a few minutes play on both aides Leigh Villas broke away through a misunder- standing by the " John's "full and half backs Leigh Villas scored a lucky goal. He-starting the "John's at once settled down and played hard against the a ind. Several times the" John's " were all over their oppon- ents but the wind spoiled their shot. However, by Smalley securing the bill the " John's " opened the score through Tickle who scored with a lightening shot which gave Shakeshaft no pass;ble chacc3 whatever. The second half opened in grand style and after a few minutes' play Tickle gave the" John's " the lead with s firm shot. Toe " John's " broke away again through Sudell who sent well up to Smalley who centred finely only to Sod Tickle waiting, the letter doing the bat trick by scoring his third successive goal. The "John s' again scored through Smalley, and aeon 4-1. For the "John's" Tickle, ideachen, Whittaker and Smalley, were the pick, and for the Villas Monkn, Evans, Harrison and Shakeshaft were the beat.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S TEAM. Goal. T Littlefair, kacks, J Morton, A Meachen (capt) ; ball backs, J Smith, F Whittaker. 'l' Soden : forwards, T Scarisbrick, W Stretford, T Tickle, F Ashton, kV Smalley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>ALTRINCEIASI AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>ALTRINCEIASI AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>2No DIVISION. PADGATE UNITED V. LATCHFORD RECREATION. A very interesting match in the above competition took place loot Saturday on the Padgate (Jolted Ground, when the visitors elms without having this season sustained defeat and the home team had not been beaten on their own ground. For a time the play was very even and confined to midfield, when the home forwards went down in a body and scored 18 minutes from the start. A high overhead kick which landed into the goal area tested the goalkeeper to the best of his abilities, and he saved his charge in splendid style on I occasions, but had eventually to succumb to the repea'ed charges of the home forwards a second time. After this second reverse the visitors played up in determined fashion an: following a free kick close in the home goal they were awarded a penalty from which Ditch- field scored. Encouraged by this succeas the visitors renewed their efforts, and found the home defence weak in clearances, which were followed up, and the visiting forwards were able to make the score two goals each when half-time was call-d. After the interval the home defence seemed to fall away in their tactics and allowed the visiting forwards to get through and EC Jre. Then the home right wing took up an opening and raced away, and the inside man shored with a rattling shot and put the teams level again. Time was drawing near, and the excitement was keen amongst spectators and players alike. Ditchfleld, of the Latchford Loam. came under the banner of the referee, ant had to go off for misconduct. This cooled the temperament of the games ILtle, and the visitors defended grandly, and when the whistle blew play was in midfield, the result being three goals each. So each team managed to keep up their respective records, still leaving the Rees at the head of the competition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>WORKS ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>SECOND ROUND.-ANNUAL HANDICAP CONCLUDED.</p>
        <p>The second round of the annual handicap was con- I eluded on Saturday at the Horse and Jockey, Winwick- ' road. The dettils are Appended J Dunne, Rllands J Broaihurkt, Rylands l5 H Eyes, Rylands ..... ... 2 T O'Brien, Castle W Caldwell, Guardian It Barnett, P and K C Taylor, Rylands l5 P Hall, V and 14 7 A Ford, Lookers l5 W Bradshaw, Wh'cross 13 T Maskill, Rylands C Lunt, Fenne11-street .1 Ashton, Firth S Oldy, Ryland, A 'Morning, Whirecross 15 T Taylor, Rylands ... 11 J Fillingbam,Rylands 3 %Velsby, Gremings OF Rice, Corporation l5 E Clare, Longford...... 13 3 Barton, Ropery l5 0 Knosales,Corporat'n 9 %V Stokes, Crostlelds' W Akhbrooke,C.L..o l5 II Dagnall,jun, Corp'en 5 J Armitage, P and K .1 Litherland. Walker's l5 C North, Cockbedge... 11 W Readies, MAU:hers H Langley, Rylands wo It Dagnall,Richmonds'abs It Brob Au, Crosfields J Stell, P and K V Shaw, Corporation W Potter, FRU' W Langley, Rylands l5 3 Roltco, Rylands l4 C Perry, Ropers, l5 II Raiford, Ropery ll T Andrews, Orford wo B Regan, Fletchers ...abs A Field, Castle l5 John Warburton,Rlands 3 C Jordan, CL C l5 A Eakersley,Cockhedge 6 J Lewis, Corporation Jai Hewitt, P&amp; K A Gleans, I? and K T Wells, Walker) T Lowe, Rylands E Blackledge, Rylands... 15 A Brit, P and K 9 R Savory. Itylands l5 J Roberts, Orford lO I Dutton, Ropery w.o R Daniels, Whitecross abs G Warburton, Rylanis 15 3 Clare, Greenings l4 T Crozier, C.L.O l5 J Singleton, Rylands 7 .1. Appleton, W'cross W. %Voraley, Longford l5 John H Dutton, P &amp; K 12 A Ellison, Monks It Holbrook, P&amp; K J Whitfield, L and N-W 15 R Woods, Cable 7 D Jones, Winwick......... 15 0 Clive, Ropery ll T Sankey, Lockers l5 T Wynne,Fennel-street 9 B Jennings, Crosfields W Simister, Longford T Caddick, P and K l5 J Lister, Rylands 5 TQu min, Firth IS II Williams, Rylands 9 W Lawton, Crosfields l5 Walulh'il'nd,Winwick 11 J Thomvon, P and K l5 J Isherwood, Fletchers 13 3 Ho-yarth, Naylors</p>
