      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RELIGIOUS e-.TOC/AL</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS e-.TOC/AL</p>
        <p>. , .' • '</p>
        <p>NEW MINISTER AT COLBORNE STREET.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesley Guild. The opening concert of the kLuvsey -road Wesley Guild took plane on Monday evening. rho meetitg was held in the Lecture Hall, and the Rev W A Beckett presided over a large attendance. The evening was enjoyably spent, and during the intervals refresh- meats were handed round. The following are the items rendered :—Chairman's remarks ; part song, " Zephyr t king thy repose," choir; solo' Pilgrim of Love," Mr Maskell ; recitatinn, Mr J Nurse; put song, I' Guardian Angels," ladies choir; monologne, " Let'. Pretend," Miss Jones ; port song," Sweet and Low," choir ; quirtette, "There was an old woman," Misses Wardle and Norse. and Megan; Maskell and Dickenson; .t,osoodnizji on',itiecib' so ,sv" gnu! i pen;phiss crntdusco the choir and Misses Wardle and Plinston were responsible for the piano accompaniments. - -</p>
        <p>1„.....„,.... : \ Igyk ) \ i _____.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>TEIE REV. 11. OGDEN,</head>
        <p>TEIE REV. 11. OGDEN,</p>
        <p>- Who commences his ministry at the Holborne-street Baptist Church ts-morrow was born at Todmorden in December 1577. 11a had the misfortune to lose his father when he was sixteen years of age at which time he followed his daily employment is an Engineering works. Becoming connected with the school and church at Wellington-road he was baptized by tha Rev. T. Cotes and joined the church at the age of sixteen where his devotion and abilities as a teacher and church worker soon attracted the attention of the pastor. Although at this time he was working overtime at the works he began to study for the ministry and under the tuition of his pastor and the Rev. H.S. Staines proved an apt pupil. When twenty-two years of age he entered Regint Pnrk Baptist College, London, where he studied for three years. At the end of that time he accepted a hearty invitation to become the pastor of the Gladwick Baptist Church, Oldham, at which place he bas laboured ustil now. During the first two years of his ministry he attended classes at the Manchester Baptist College under Principal Marshall. During his pastorate the church was chastel of debt and a fund for a new school established. The various organisations connected with the church have always been closely watched and attended personally by him. On March 27th, 1906 he married ths daughter of Mr. D. Sutcliffn, Town Clerk of Todmorden, but unfortunately lost her by death in July 1910. During his eight years ministry le has been connected with many religious and civil associations a few of which he has served officially. He Ins been secretary to the Pree Church ministers fraternal, President of the Oldham and District Christian Endeavour Union and in for this year President of the Oldham Temperance Council. Coming with him ss his companion is his sister Miss Ogden who also has proved an efficient worker especially in the secool where she was the teacher of a class of young women. Mr. Ogden has preached at Golhome-street on many occasions during the past few years and since the pastorate has been vacint was the only one the members considered. Tney therefore extended to him a hearty call which he accepted, and takes tip his duties to-morrow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Insurance of the Parish Church</head>
        <p>Insurance of the Parish Church</p>
        <p>The current issue of the Parish Church Magazine mentions that the church is now fully insured against all damage by fire for over £30,000. The following are the items : NAVE:—Building, £17,600; Psin`ed windows, £1,900; Furniture and fittings, £2,000 : monuments, LIAO ; screen in nave, £800; organ, £1,250 ; plate, £5OO ; screen in chapel, £6OO ; bells alio clock, £640 ; total, £26,690. BIIANCICL :—Boilding, £3,000 ; painted windows, £1,400; total, £4,4:0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>St. Georges Mission</head>
        <p>St. Georges Mission</p>
        <p>The services at St. George's for the last six weeks since the departure of the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpatb (says the Pariah Church Magazine) have been most efficiently and acceptably conducted by Mr. H. J. Westbrook, who undertook similar duties at St. Clement's during the vacancy. We are indeed most grateful to Mr. West- brook, as we hear are all the officers and congregation at the mission. The Rev. .1. H. Buckmaster was ordained on Sunday, September 24th, at blossley Hill and is now in charge of the mission. He will find hard work we know, but he will also find we are assured good friends and kind helpers who will be only too so illing to sssist him in his duties. An earn(st of their welcome was seen in the gathering arranged for Wednesday last which was spontaneously proposed and onanimously carried out without any external recommendation or a oggestion. The Late Me. F G. Taylor.</p>
        <p>The news of the death of our late Church officer (says the Parish Church Magazine) came as a thunderbolt and caused wide and deep sorrow. In the town, in the parish, in his home he will he greatly missed, for Mr. Taylor was an excellent man of tininess, warmly attached to the Church rod devoted to his home. Thu circumstances of his death are very painful, for he was in the prime of life, of a vigorous constitution and possessed great abilities. He would certainly have made his mark in the town, and would have furthered in every way in his power the interests of the Church. The funeral was attended by many prominent townsmen and, though quiet, was marked by the deepest respect and reverence. The hearts of all go cot to his father in his arid bereavement. They are well aware what each was to the other. A better son there cculd not have been, and we know how his affection was appreciated and requited. May God support our respected friend in this boar of need and bless this sudden and ulna. panted ending of what promised to be such a bright and prosperous career to the most serious consideration of on all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Late Mr. John Geddes.</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. John Geddes.</p>
        <p>We regret to say (says St. Paul's "Parish Magazine') that we are becoming poorer in more ways than one, every year. We have just recently lost by death one of the oldest and most respected and regular worshippers in the person of Mr. John Geddes. What an example his life is to all of us, so quiet and unassuming, so regular in his attendance at the means of grace; unfortunately we are obliged to mention that such as he ara few and far between, the Caurch becomes the poorer in that sense by such a loss. In another sense his pecuniary assistance was of immenee importance to us; this also makes U 3 the poorer, and unfortunately, at we look around us, we wonder who will take his place. His body was laid to rest in the quiet country church- yard at Waverton on Tuesday, September 230 ; many of his old and valued friends were present to show their respect for such a life. The serv!ce was fully choral and conducted by the Vicar of Wayerion, the Reverend Willoughby Jones; the Rev. H. V. Pigot and your Vicar had the mournful privilege of committing his body to the ground, in a sure and certain hope of the Resurrection. So closes the earthly life of one of Warrington's very best.</p>
        <p>Bishop of Liverpool and Harvest Festivals.l Why not all on One Date? The Bishop of Liverpool ([Jr. Chavesse, sac the preacher at the harvest thanksgiving service at Emmaeuel Church„Southport,on Suuday. Ile remarked that that day in nine Southport churches thanksgiving service, were bolo, held. It was a good and wise move to flu a certain day for the holdiug of harvest thankn. giving in a locality. There wan • great danger at present of their harvest thanksgiving service, losing their real object of an act of worship sod thenkagiving to God and becoming mere spectacles, and of their Christian people wsodering roost church to church to look at the various decorations, listening to the various enthems, and listening or not listening to the ipreachers, lie trusted the move made in Southport would be wisely followed throughout the diocese of Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Hill Cliffe Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Hill Cliffe Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>• On Sunday the Hey. Georo Curtis (the newly' appointed minister), commenced his ministry at this historical church. There were good congregations both morning sod evening, and euthusinsin amongst the , people—all desit ing a season of great prosperity for the future of the church. The choir rendered efficient aid to the services during the day by their sweet singing. Mr. Curtis took for his sahjsct in the morning " Com- pleteness in Christ," addressinq. his topic to Church members, his text being Col. it. to, showing that our salvation WWI complete in that there was a complete "atonement," complete forgiveness," and cmnplete " righteousness" in Him. Our life, toll, woo complete in its a tteefulness," enjoyments," " provision " and " triumph.," and lastly our hope was cmnplete. God's Ito&amp; was unlike man's. Ile worked to a finish and His work was perfect. In tha evening Mr. Curtia preached from 2 rimothy, i., 12 " I know in whom I have believed," ltc. His main thoughts were that. Paul believed something. This was contrary to the order of the day. It. was now fashion. able to doubt. Paul's belief was " worthy." Not things merely but a personal Saviour," I kr.ow whom." It was "Intelligent" he said, "I boom " and it was "fined "for be said "I bare believed." It was the fixed tree that dug its roots deep into the soil that flourished. Then Paul committed something! His deposit was lodged in the hands of the hest trustee. This trustee was able to keep. No element of weaknees was to be found in anything God did. And finally Paul effected something. He bnd in view " that day," the day of triumph over all adversity nod earth trials when be should receive the crown of life. 'rue Communion Service brought to a close a very happy d3y, a goodly number partaking of the ordinance. " Christianity a Dangerous Thing." Rev. H. Williamson at Bewsey-road.</p>
        <p>Preaching at Bouncy-road Wesleyan Clinch last Sunday morning, the Rev. Hugh Williamson, ILA, said that oue of the stock arguments against Christianity was that it was a dangerous thing. If that statement were some piece of imagination, he said, it would matter but little, but the trouble was that it was a fact. Christianity said in the first place, think for yourself. and in the second place, act for yourself. Those were both dangerous things to do. They were not to take their opinions from anybody else, and no if their neigh- bour were of the opinion that the earth was round and they were quite sure that it WAS flat, they should not go about ecnoing the opinion of their nelghboue, but stick to their own. And if it should be that the Creeds the Church, and the parson held certain views of our Lord Jesus Chiist, and if they did not commend themselves to their best judgment, they should not go about saying they believed them. They should hold their own views, thinking for them- selves, and standing on their own intellectitil feet. It was nt the method of Christ to shove the fact of His Messishship continually into the face of His disciples—rather did He want them to discover the fact for themselves. It was possible that they might be unable to resolve their doubts and end at last ins perpetual state of unbelief, perhaps' scepticism or agnosticism,for there Was no gturanteethat they would come out sn the Ch,istian side. Mr. Williamson went on to say that ages ago Christian men realised that it sans dangerous to tell men that they oh mild think for themselves because men were so stupid. And so it came to pass that instead of creeds being an iustruct ion and guide to thought they became but a parcel of the truth and marked off on all kinds of notice boards, " Trespassers will be prosecuted" and the rest of it—a man was to keep within a little area or else very serious penslties were imposed upon him. They also said that they must not allow people to act for them- selves and no in Apostolic times Christianity was watered down into a sort of Judaism and a kind of law which enveloped and directed men's lives. But Christisnity watered down had proved to be more dangerous than the pure thing. Men began to wonder whether the creeds held all the truth. Some things did not seem to them quite satisfactory and yet all tbe time and under the pressure of external authority they profeesei to find in those things the most complete expression of truth that God had vouchsafed to man. And no all the time they were living a hypocritical life. They became wishy-washy and humbugs and he preferred a race of honest agnostics to those. Jesus came to them not simply with an invitation but with a challenge, which they seemed to ' forget in the midst of soft and silly sentimentalism and their life of ease. Christ did not say "Come unto Me because it is nice," but " Come onto Me and I will show yon how to live always on the edge of danger " —not stoma unto Me and I gill show you how to get on well with everybody, but maybe how to get kicked out of your own house maybe how to lose every penny you have got." Christ did not say "come unto Me and I mill solve every difficulty," but " Yoe must think out things for yourself." Christ said that they must tare grit, spirit and courage to be of service to Him, and if they did not have those there was no place for them in His Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>BRICK-STREET MISSION. The harvest thanksgiving services in connection with the above were held on Sunday when the church looked at it best, decked with fruit and, vegetables, to the taste of the ladies of the congregation. The preacher both morning and evening was Mr. G. Hunter,of Leigh, the church being well-filled a': both services.—On Monday evening the products of the harvest were dis- tributed, followed by a concert provided by the follow- ing artistes: Misses Jarman and Howell, and Messrs Attiwell, Fiddis and Coultas ani the Rewsey Glee Party. Mr. W. Wright occupied the chair. HOLY TRINITY CEICRCEL - .</p>
        <p>The harvest. festival was coatjtmed on San day when the preacher was the Rev. J. k[organ Price, formerly curate at Holy Trinity, who received a heir ty welcome from his many Warrington friends. The musical portions of the services were very creditably rendered Iby the choir. At the morning service the Te Deum was sung to Sullivan's beautiful setting in D,and the anthem "Thou visitsst the earth' (Calcott), and the Eyrie and Credo (Roland Smart In Elist) were features of a bright thanksgiving service. In the evening the Magnificat and uric Dimittis (Goss in Ai and the anthem "Ye shall dwell in the land " (Stainer) were impressively rendered, the solos in the anthem being sung by Messrs P Smith and A V Hodgson. The musical arrangement of the services and the singing of the choir bore testi. mony to the grout pains taken by Mr J A ih'estcott, the choirmaster. Mr W B Randall was at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>_ ST. JOHNS CHCItCH.</head>
        <p>_ ST. JOHNS CHCItCH.</p>
        <p>—•- -- Specral harvest thanksgiving services were held at St. I John's Preab)terian Church on Sunday last,the preacher being the ltev. William Reid. At the cowling service the church was filled to overflowing. Mr. Reid preached from John xii. 24," Excepts corn of wheat fall into the ground and die it abideth by itself, but if it die it bringeth forth much fruit." Mr. Reid said there were many ways by which they mi•ht approach the harvest subject. The occasion on which the words of their test were spoken was that of the Passover Feast in Jeru- salem. Certain Greeks laid to the d actples "We would see Jesus." Jesus was informed and saw as the request the first fruits of the sovereignty He wee going to exercise in the Gentile world. Jesus prrlt 'coded the law that the approach to life was through death, law which was the very antithesis to that proclaimed by Greek philosophy, which held that the beat in life was to be obtained through the culture of the beat in self. But it was no new law that Christ declared—they met with it in sacred writ from the earliest times. They saw it In the mysterious Trinity, giving of itself • of its best, giving Jeans Christ to cite for the redemp- tion of the world. They could not comprehend it but on • bumbler level they could understand it.</p>
