      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <p>The farmer scattered [leaned into the earth, and if he grain of wheat remained alive it died in the saving ef to life, but if it lost ita form end diel, then out of that death life sprang up an hundred-fold. The path- way to life invariably mil inevitably lead through death. That was the Word of the Lord, rind reigned supreme throughout the ages, history and experiencs bearing it out day after day and year after veer. "Except a grain of wheat fell into the ground sod die it ahideth by itself." God acknowledged thst law, and His servants down the ages had acknowledged the truth lof the same. It remained for them to toe the 11).0 line, as God showed them the opportunity. There was no other way. The choir grue • creditable rendering of the anthem " Oh, worship the Lord," u 'der the leader- !Minot Mr. A. 0. Leressy,Mra James Snsilharn being the organiaL The church was beautifully desorated for the services by the ladies of the congregation. Ths raised platform-pulpit, which sprcidlly leads itself to a tasteful display of h fruita and tl isms, looked very pretty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>GOLBORNE-STREET BAPTIST CHURCH.</head>
        <p>GOLBORNE-STREET BAPTIST CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services in connection with the above Wane of worship were held on Sunday. Tba church had been tastefully tin:towed by the ladies of the c tngteg ition with as abtandancl of fruit, flowers and corn. the Rev. J. T. Schofield (a former pastor of the church, now of Taunton), was the preacher both morning and eveninr, large congregations astembling both services. In the morning the rev, gentleman took his text from 2 Kings, iv., 42-41, and In the evening Genesis, tail., 11. A service of song entitled "Tee Mills of God S' was contributed by the choir in the afternoon. Mr. Ben Few occupied the chair while the connective readings were given by Mr. E. Wynne Jones. "the choir uader the able conductorship of Mr. F. Atkinson and Mr. Barnet gave an elf.tottve rendering of the various items. Mr. S. kickers presided at the organ. The collections, which were in ail of the Cuurch Fulda were very satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The services to celebrAte the safe ingathering of the harvest were commenced at the above place of worship on Wednesday evening, when a large congregation assembled. The ch,irch presented a pretty sight the ladies of the congregation hiving used considerable taste with the abundance of fruit, flowers and ones at. . _</p>
        <p>their disp teal. Tha Vioar, the Rev. W. Bracecsmp assisted by the Revs. E. K. Peters and J. H. Banks con- ducted the service. Toe prtacher was the Rev. K. B. Blackeney, M..A , of South', trt, who took his tent from Gal. vi., 7-8. The choir rendered the anthem "Consider the lilies of the field' (.1 L Ilat'on) in a most praise- worthy manner. Mr. Wilkes presided at the organ. The collection was in aid of the Assistant Clergy fund for which £5O is required. Th• services will be cos- tinued to-morrow (Sanday).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>EHHANUEL (RRFORMED) CHURCH.</head>
        <p>EHHANUEL (RRFORMED) CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Special thsnksgi %lag services for harvest were held last Sunday, splendid nongnigations being present at all three services. The Curate-in-charge, the Rev. W. T. Jaye, preached appropriate sermons, taking for his subject in the morning "The Former, and he Latter Rain" basing his remarks on Jeremiah v., 21 Is the evening his subject was" The principle wheat " the tent being Isai h xxviii., 25. In the after- nuts Mr. Jaye spoke to the children on " Harvest Lessons." The choir rendered the anthem, "Thrust in thy sickle," "I seal lift up mine eyes"" While tie earth remaineth " I will sing of Thy mercy," their efforts being highly appreciated. Mr. W. J. Jowett presided ably at the organ. The congregstional singing was also most hearty, and it wis felt at the close of the day that there had been imbed a time of reel heartfelt praise, and Cumkagiving, not only for harvest mercies, but for God's goodness to the church. Mention must be male of the gifts of (nit, ad fl )wers presented by the children at tho afternoon service which were aft srwards di 'too '.ed amongst the oink.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>WARRINGTON PARISH CEICRCH</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CEICRCH</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services were continued on Sunday. In the morning there was a large congregation at matins and choral celebratioo of the lioly Communion, the preacher being the Rev. Canon Stevenson, principal of the Warrington Training College, who took for his text the word; " Say not ye, there are yet four months and O. cometh harvest ? Behold I say unto you, lift up your eyes and look on the fields; for they are white already to harvest." He pointed oot that often when the outlook from the Christi. standpoint appeared most hopeless a rich harvest was waiting to be garnered, if only the neoeasar effort were put forth in • spirit of faith. Another lemon to be derived front the text was the potentiality for good in every human life and ho urged his hearers tv matte the best 093 of the gifts with which tcl had en lowed them. . . . . _</p>
        <p>The n;unio of the Holy Cominuoiom was Erre io E with the except ilo of the S teethe act 0101 which were Bung to the late. Sir .John wir's settiogs. Th 3 . -</p>
        <p>church was crowded to. Evensong, wit 'a the Us,. CAOOO Wainwright, Vicir of St. John's, Alt ',wham, prencSed. He drew stole ilteresting analogies between the . . . . .</p>
        <p>- ultural and the epirausl world and u,gsd the lemons Of reverent ill humility in th 3 presence of the wonders of nature and of calm trustfulness j tO sd, as contrasted with ovsr-ansisty for the future. Toe ch tir gave • beaut ful randy ing of the anthem "Thou •i .i test the earth "(Calk*, the quattatte beine sung by Muter J. Sankey. Messrs. Wi ililra Hamlett, J H Draper, and J B Poole. Mr F M Dsrby, Mos. BAC, preen led at the organ nih his usual taste and skill. The collections throughout the festival, whizh wsre in ail of the clergy fund, amounted to £l5.</p>
      </div>
