      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SA rCRDAY. OCTOBER 1, 1911</p>
        <p>During the week there THE CHURCHES have been signs that AND SOCIAL the Churches are not UNREST. unmindful that the</p>
        <p>prevalent social unrest calls for some action on their part, as well as on the part of the State. The meetings of the Church Congress at Stoke, of the Baptist Union at Brighton, and of the Scottish Con- gregational Union at Perth have all shown a good deal of concern about the condition of the people, and they are beginning to realise that if they are to retain their hold upon the masses and to extend their influence they cannot ignore the material side of the people's welfare. They are beginning to be anxious as to why the working classes are so entirely indifferent to the work of the Churches. and their anxiety is to be welcomed on public as well as on religious grounds. So far, the Churches in this country have not incurred the active hostility of the workers, as they have abroad, the reason no doubt being the greater atmosphere of free- dom that prevails here. Many, indeed,of our Labour Leaders are lay preachers in some denomination, and, although the working classes do not attend church in such num- bers as the Churches themselves would desire, they are still not without affection for the basis on which the Churches are built. There is inherent in the mind of our democ- racy a deep religious feeling, as anybody who has worked among the toiling masses knows quite well, and the BISHOP OF LONDON was more than justified in his remarkable sermon at Stoke in rejoicing that, though the Labour movements in this country are not actively religious, they are far from hostile and are in the main still led by religious men. But the Churches themselves are beginning to realise that this state of things will not last if they allow themselves to become organisations catering only for the spiritual welfare of the comfortable classes, and it is the expression of this awakening at this week's meetings that is so gratifying. The growth of the limited liability company and the consequent growth of the factory system, to the gradual elimination of the private employer, has made a good deal of difference to the relations of employer and employed. The personal interest in the welfare of his workpeople shown by the old-time private employer has not been transferred to the managing director, whose sole interest in too many cases is the making of dividends for his shareholders When the workers see big dividends announced they know out of whom those dividends have been earned in the main, and one of the reasons operating in producing the recent railway strike was undoubtedly the chortling of more than one railway chairman at the recent half-yearly meetings regarding the increased prosperity of their companies. Someone has said, with a touch of cynicism, that 4 per cent, is a Christian dividend, that 8 per cent. is more doubtful, while 16 per cent, is a work of supererogation, and, therefore, partaking of the nature of sin. No one expects the Churches to consider the details of wages and that sort of thing, but they can exercise great influence regarding conditions of labour. Once the masses get the idea that the Churches are not concerned with their material and worldly welfare at a time when that welfare is suffering, then the Church will lose its influence on the people entirely. They are already suspicious that the Churches care more for the wealthy sub- scriber than they do for those who have to toil for their living, and it is for Church leaders to prove in a time of great unrest that this is really a misrepresentation of their attitude. Happily, the number of , social workers in all the Churches is increas- ing, and the conditions under which this poor live are impressing .them with the need</p>
        <p>i for them to advocate a higher standard of housing and of comfort, after which a higher standard of conduct will assuredly follow.</p>
        <p>Warrington was "THE BRIDGE OF always a proud Su E. borough. To-day it is even prouder, for have not the first piles been driven of the magnifi- cent structure which has been designed to form an adequate link between Lancashire and Cheshire at the famous ford over the Mersey in the heart of the town? For " years and years, and years and years" a new bridge has been talked of, longed .or, and pnjected in all manner of ways, but at last it has almost reached the stage of concrete reality. Anyhow one can picture it in the mind's eye with a comfortable amount of certainty, and it says much for the persistence and enthusiasm of men like the Mayor, Alderman Smethurst, Alderman Bolton—and indeed all the members of the Council—that the many initial difficulties of the scheme have been at last overcome. No one to-day questions the need of a new bridge, but until Mr. Webster's structure spans the muddy stream we shall hardly be able to realize the extent of the boon conferred, or the seriousness of tno disabilities under which the people of two counties, and our own big borough, have so long laboured. We hope that the work inaugurated on Thursday will progress rapidly and evenly to full fruition in the allotted time. There is scope for acquiring distinction in many ways and we are sure that neither architect nor contractors will lose a single opportunity of making Warrington Bridge an object of admiration for countless generations to come!</p>
        <p>The luncheon at the MR. ROBERT Town Hall was, for PIERPOINT'S many reasons, destinc- " SWAN SONG." tive. Apart from the historic importance of -the occasion from a local point of view, there were among the guests three gentlemen who have, or have had, the honour of representing the borough in Warrington in Parliament, and all three made speeches of note. The most striking oration was certainly that of the ex-Conservative Member, Mr. Robert Pierpoint, whose remarks were strongly tinged with deep personal feeling, arising out of his impending severance from the town "under circumstances which were not of his creating." We have taken the liberty of previously alluding to these circumstances as a matter of public interset, and seeing that Mr. Pierpoint has openly alluded to a situation that is not without bitterness, we may be pardoned for once again touching on the subject. Our regret at the approaching departure of Mr. Pierpoint hardly needs to us expressed here, for the feeling is prevalent throughout the community in the midst of which he was born, and in the service of which he has devoted the best years of his life. In the main, though by no means exclusively, Mr. Pierpoint's service was political. He did much for many worthy causes, but for the Conservative party in Warrington he rendered services which should have ensured him the fullest and most generous recognition. Mr. Pierpoint's crime was that he twice failed to beat the Liberal candidate for the borough. The fact that he had three times succeeded in previous years was forgotten, and the gentlemanly party "dropped the pilot" with as little compunction as a famous emperor once discarded the services of an even more famous statesman. Mr. Pierpoint was not, and has not yet been—so far as the public of Warrington knows—even thanked for the long years of loyal service to Conservatism in Warrington, and Mrs. Pierpoint, too, is to be allowed to leave without a " thank-you " for the inception of that energetic but misguided body, the Women's Tariff Reform League—now glorying in a new president. Little wonder Mr. and Mis. Pierpoint are leaving Warrington, and little wonder the wrench is painful. Mr. Pier- point did not recapitulate such grievances in his speech on Thursday—he has too much of -the dignity of an Englishman and a gentle- man—but they were present in the minds of many men who listened to the" swan song" of the late Tory member. His final words: -' the ways from Warrington to London are ehort and easy, and the ways are always open," bore a significance which was not lost eight of. It will not be with the concurrence of the majority of Warrington people if Mr. Pierpoint mAkes his severance complete and unbroken. Times may change and—a utres lemps, autreS mannieres. !</p>
        <p>The Warrington THE PARLIAMENT Parliamentary Debat- AT HOME. jug Society has entered upon a new session 'under the most encouraging circumstances and, with a Liberal Ministry in power, there is certain to be abundant material for interest- ing and profitable debates. Sir Edward Russell, who honoured the Society with a visit on Tuesday, interested all sections of the House by a concise and forceful exposition of the task which confronts the Liberal party to-day, and his strong plea for the fuller recognition of the sentiment of nationality created a great impression. Mr. A. H. Crosfield had the unusual experi- ence of delivering a second " maiden " speech in Parliament, for although he has addressed the august assembly at Westminster he had never before caught the Speaker's eye in the local House of Commons. He devoted him- self to the question of national defence and showed the fatuity of the arguments of those who would have us believe that England is in a state of unpreparedness to cope with any emergency. On the contrary, our forces were never in a better condition, and notwith- standing all the frantic efforts of the scare- mongers to alarm him, John Bull can still afford to sleep contentedly at nights, with a fully justified sense of security.</p>
        <p>The opening of the THE AUTUMN winter season sees no CAMPAIGN, relaxation of educa- tional work on the part of the Progressive Labour League, and the fine demonstration at the Cc-operative Hall on Thursday evening was a credit to its promoters and an earnest of the deter- mination of local progressives to retrieve Warrington's good name at the next election. The cheery, good-natured Tom Wing is always assured of a hearty good welcome to Warrington. His explanation of the Insurance Bill was a model of lucidity and must have re-assured any of his audience who were inclined to waver in their support of that great measure. Mr. A. H. Crosfield delivered a comprehensive speech of striking directness and force and showed the absurdity of Mr. Harold Smith's assertion that Free Trade is responsible for the labour unrest in England. We have only to turn to the Continent for a refutation of this preposterous claim. Mr. Crosfield also tackled the con- scriptionist' on their own ground and had no difficulty in showing that there is nothing ii=ilaumLyeserit national position to justify thisi elation of the liberty of the</p>
      </div>
