      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>COLOURS FOR THE PARISH CHURCH.</head>
        <p>COLOURS FOR THE PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>OLD COLOURS OF THE 2nd DATTALTI</p>
        <p>- - With impressive and dignified ceremonial, the historic ceremony of handing over the colours of the [Second Battalion of the South Rule ashire Regiment to, the Rector and churchwarden* for preservation in the, Warrington Pariah Church took placs last evening. The old and tattered relics, precious for their old associations and their record of a magnificent toll of famous victories, were borne to the church in a processi in representative of the military and civic life of the town. Eirorting the colours was a detachment of torch bearers whose blazing torchea gave a somewhat wierd touch to the scene. The procession, which was marshalled at the Drill Hall, was headed by the Band of the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment followed by a large master of Territorials, under the command of Lieutenant-Colonel J. D. Fairclougli. The Army Service Corps was in charge of Cap'ain Picton. The band of the fith-40th Regimen'sf districts and the trope stationed at Orford Barracks, preceded</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett is not, by any means the only crusader against black smoke Lancashire is doing more than "speaking out boldly" on the subject of the smoke nuisance, for we are informed by the hon. secretary of the Smoke Abatement League of Greßt Britain—of which the headquarters are in Mancheste.—that an important con- ference on the subject is to be held towards the end of this November. It is expected that a large number of experts will be brought together on this occasion, and that some practical programme will be drawn up by which the nuisance may be abated. A strong league, embracing the whole factory area of the North of England, with a branch office in each great town, would do much towards consolidating public opinion that some immediate legislation should be passed that would lessen the smoke evil, and it is hoped that this autumn's conference will result in this expansion.</p>
        <p>Photo, N SOUTH LANCASHIRE REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>-- the Mayor, who was accompanied by Major Goren, Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Sir Gilbert Greenell, Bart., C.V.0., aol civic officials. Surrounded by the guard of honour the old regimental colours were held aloft for the last time they would ever be seen in a military prrc ssion. On arrival at the old church, the main body of troops having taken their places, the colour party was met at the church door by the choir and clergy eho walked before them up the centre ' aisle, while the hymn "Onward Christian soldiers" was snag. The coLurs were carriel to the chancel steps by Lieutenants Shaw and Clements and handed to the Rector, who, after accepting them, delivered a short address in which he referred to the great deeds of the regi- ment. rho National Anthem was then sung aid a short form of service was gone through concluding with the hymn" Now thank we all our God." Huge crowds watched the passing of the procession to and from the Parish Church. NOTICE OF REMOVAL.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>The Baby Show. Entries for the second annual baby show v ill remain open till Tuesday next. Nursing Association. The annul meeting of the Warrington District Nursing Association will be held at the Town Hall on Monday evening under the presidency of the Mayor. Palace and Hippodrome. Next week Fred Giniirtt's Company in Dick Turpin's Ride to York " jay a welcome return visit to the Palace. Cavallotti Bros, described as they - premier all-round instrumentalists, and Stewart and ' Morgan in a vocil speciality, together with a host of other well-known artistes, ale also announced to i appear. VAC CUM CLEANERS free the home from dust. Price 2to 7 guineas. Try one on hire.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge st, Warrington.—Advt. Ex-King Manuel at Warrington. The en-king of Portugil accompanied by his mother I and a nun crone suite, arrived at Bank Quay station on Saturday a Iternom, shortly after three o'clock. The , ex-kirg who was on hie way to Lowther Castle on a I visit to Lord and Lady Lonsdale, remained for abut five minutes in the lotion awaiting his train, but as ibis visit had been kept a secret by the railway officials only a comparatively small number of people were on the platform. The Bible Centenary. The centenary of the Warrington Auxiliary of the British and Foreign Bible Siciety is drawing near. On October 22ad there will be the Centenary Bible Sunday. and no October 2ffth a mass meeting in Parr Hall. The first rehears-II of the grand united choir will be held on Wednesday next at 8 p.m. in Cairo- street schoolro:ini. Members of all churches and chapels are cordially invited. Ccemsair, Rodgers oelebrated knives and forts, —Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge at., Warr ington.—Advt. The Calico Ball. Our readers, of both sexes, are reminded that the Liberal Women's grand fancy calico ball tikes place in Parr Hall on Wednesday next, when&amp; large com- pany is expected to assemble. Prizes nil be given for the best and most original costumes, which have not to cot more than 5r., so that there is plen'y of scope 14 originality. The general pubic will be admitted to the galleries at a small charge, while tickets for the dance cast f.s. Gd. each. ANTHRACITE STOVES, Eoomonickl, clean, cheerful. Price frain 3716.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge at. Warrington.—Advt. Liberal Women's Conference. The Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Women's Liberal Associations will hold its annual Council meeting in the Parr Hall, Warrington. on Wednesday next. Mrs. Steward Brown will preside and open the proceedings with an address on current politics. At the morning session (11 a.m. to 1 p m.) the subjects under discussion will he: Disearablishment of the State Church in Wales, Wit. Ellis Griffiths and Mrs. James Bliss ; Government Insurance Bill, Miss Cicely Corbett and Lady A. Howarth. For the afternoon meeting (2.30 to 5 p.m) the programme is : Home Rule, Miss Patricia Hoey League) and Miss Crosfield ; The Local Government Qualification Bill, Mies Bertha Mason and Mrs. Ellis ; Women's Suffrage, Mrs. Stewart Brown and Mre. Barton. Ex-Naval and Military Civil Service Associa-</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above society was held , at the " Blue Lion,' Market-street, on Thursday. The President (Mr. E. Warburton) being unavoidably absent, Mr. C. Holt occupied the chair. The ordinary business was soon disposed of, thanks to the energetic secretary, Mr. W. Cummings, and then the members sat down t, a " tot-pot " provided by Mr. Lake, to which full justice was done. This was followed by a musical programme, contributed by the following artistes :—Messrs. Dukes, Henshaw, McCunlough, Sergeant-Drummer Clarke, and Warrington's favourite entertainer, Mr. Sam Auckland, with concertina solos and humorous songs. A very enjoyable evening was brought to a close by singing the National Anthem. " °SRAM " AND " TANTALVNI "LAMPS—Agent- J. S. GARLA.ND, 51, to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Workingmen's Infirmary Committee —Annual Concert. The Infirmary concert announced for this evening promises to be of a high order. Mr. Down's band will make its twenty-third appearance at these concerts, mid will render interesting orchestral items. Madame Rathbone (soprano) will sing "The Moorish Maid," and "Chinese love song" ; Madame Sanders " In the Chimney Corner," and" The songs my mother sang" ; Mt. Horace Minks (tenor), who created such a great impression at the concert last year, will render " Farewell in the desert" and "Come into the garden Maud." The bass, Mr. William Coleman, of Man- chester Cathedral, will be respons:ble for "Stone cracker John," and the "Corporal's Ditty." Mr. Sinks and Mr. Coleman will sing the popular duet , "Watchman ! what of the night" The Orpheus Quartet Party will render glees, and Mr. Howard , lanes willsastain the humorous rrtion of the concert , Mr. W. jf. Payton will be the accompanist. The concert Sol commence in Parr Hall at 7.30 prompt.</p>
        <p>• , • • • •Nk. . ' •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LNOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>LNOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Scene: A Warrington infant school. Time, Bible lesson—teacher is talking about Heaven. First dot: " Please teacher, my dada's gone to Heaven." Second dot : "That's. nowt! My dada's gone to Black- pool for a week." The above is vouched for as authentic by a local schoolmaster. . * * * Mr. Harold Smith knows as much about soft soap as Mr. A. H. Crosfield does about Perfection. On Thursday he remarked that he had never met such charming and kindly people as the people of Warrington. On Friday, "he had never come across an Infirmary better managed than the Warring- ton Infirmary." Though born in Cheshire, the Member must have had more than a nodding acquaintance with the blarney- stone I</p>
        <p>* * Lord Roberts, who is one of those who do not look their age, completed his seventy- ninth year on Saturday last. His great opportunities came to him rather more slowly than to his contemporaries. Lord Wolseley, who is his junior by about nine months, and Lord Kitchener were both raised to the peerage before they were fifty. Lord Roberts had to wait till he was sixty. He had previously been made a baronet, a grade which the two viscounts skipped.</p>
        <p>'" • - The pile driving was accomplished with considerable eclat on Thursday, when all was fair and bright. If the truth were ' known, however, there had been considerable heart-burning behind the scenes. Alderman Forshaw was mollified by the courtesy of the Mayor, who insisted on his Deputy tak- ing a leading part in the ceremonies, but another Alderman—the father of the Council —refused to attend the luncheon because he had been omitted from the list of post- prandial orators. • • Twenty-four shillings a week as a minimum wage for Corporation employees will make the mouth water of many an honest toiler in Warrington who has not the extreme felicity of being on the municipal pay-roll. Councillor Halliwell was the prime instigator of the step which the Council took—somewhat in the dark—on Tuesday evening, and no doubt his constituents and the workers generally, will be proportion- ately grateful. One feels satisfaction that Warrington is the pioneer of a living wage, but the way of the pioneer is often hard and always expensive. How many of the enthusiastic fourteen who supported the resolution stopped to reflect that the increase would involve an annual expenditure of £1,200 equal to another IVid. on the rates! • •</p>
        <p>Surely the reductio ad absurdum of tariffs is found in a German treaty with Switzer- land which contains a clause which deserves to remain famous. The treaty signed with Switzerland on November 12th, 1904 admits into Germany at a reduced tariff the bovine beast of the region of the high plateau of the in uotains belonging to the great spotted race or to the brown race which has been reared at an altitude of at least 300 mitres above the level of the sea, and has made each year sojourn of one month at the minimum at an altitude of at least 800 metres above the level of the eel. So careful had the German negotiators to be in order to exclude the French bovine beasts. It seems hard on the cow that has been reared in the pleasant valleys of Switzerland that it must pay the higher duty The picture of Mr. Balfour pursuing his policy of retaliation and superintending negotiations of this sort is (says the " West- minster Gazette ") one which no doubt every Tariff Reformer would delight in.</p>
        <p>Warrington Cricket and Bow Hag Club. After the closing main of the season on Saturday, a presentation in the f.mpa of a silver rose bowl was made to Mr. It T. Fairclough by Mr. W. Sikook, the president, the recipient basing occupied the position of captain of the bowling club for many years, and having been a member for nearly bait a century. FIRE GRATES. If you want a new grate our large and varied stook will interest ytu.—./. S. 0 •RLAND, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>AUTUMN CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>THE AUTUMN CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE'S BIG MEETING.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES BY MR. A H. GROSFIELD AND MR. TOM WING.</p>
        <p>A Reply to Mr. di</p>
        <p>The Co-operative Hall was filled to over- flowing with a splendidly enthusiastic audience on Thursday, when the Warrington Pro- gressive Labour League held a demonstration. Ifr. Tom Wing, ex-M.P. for Grimsby, was the principal fpeaker, and his racy and forceful exposition of current politics and his lucid defence of.the Insurance Bill met with general acceptance. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, who was received with great enthusiasm delivered an ' important speech, in whirls he replied to recent assertions of Mr. Harold Smith , M.P., with reference to Tariff Reform and the recent strikes, and also dealt with the question of compulsory military training. Mr. George Walker presided, and there were on the plat- form Messrs. Tom Wing, A. H. Crosfield, Councillor Locker, J. Morton, J. Jarman, P. O'Brien, S. Wright, M. Llewellyn, and Harry New. THE INSURANCE BILL. . .</p>
        <p>The chairman said that one thing that seemed to be troubling working men was the effect the Insurance B ill would Lave upon the Friendly ftocieties. Warrington was favoured with a good inany friendly ,ocleties compoeed of men who had been encouraged by .thrift to join them, and he only hoped that belore they made up their minds about the Insurance Bill they would understand the principles of it. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>'BILL WAS IN GREAT DANGER</head>
        <p>'BILL WAS IN GREAT DANGER</p>
        <p>In danger from tlwise who nought to destroy it from political and selfish motiyes, the latter wanting to get more than they were legitimately entitled to. If Friendly Society members upset the Bill, I very grave responsibility would rest upon them. for a few millions would then prevent between thirteen and fourteen millions from receiving the benefits of the Bill. (Applause.) Mr. WING'S RACY SPEECH. Tom Wing received a cordial welcome. He sag/ that since they last met much had happened. Grimsby had changed its mind—laughter;---so had Wigton—(laughter), and FO outsiders such as Mr. Crosfield and he, sympathised with each ether. (Laughter.) But there was nome gleam of natl.. . .</p>
        <p>don ihai those who had replaced Them were</p>
        <p>NOT IN A POSITION TO DO ANY BABY. (Laughter end applause.) Both might play on the big drum, but they would not destroy anybody's rest. (Langhter4 both were in the same host of Tariff Reform—a very leaky sort of vessel for any man to be found in. It always annie,ed him to hear folks talking about Tariff Reform as if it were a new thing, whereas it was the oldest thing</p>
        <p>TOIL WIN</p>
        <p>in polities--Noah knew something about it. (Laughter.) The Tariff Reformer was always wrong, he was always backing the wrong horse, and was always a day behind. TARIFF " DEFORM " WAS THE MOST SELFISH DOCTRINE •</p>
        <p>.... that had ever been propagated amongst the general Misses of the people. (Hear' hear.) Because the man who advocated it in the commercial sense generally stood to gain by it, whilst arguing that it was for the people's sakes. He wan not going to dispute the earnestness of a very large number or Tariff "Reformers," but he wanted to ask a question, and until they got an answer they need . not go any further. If Tariff "Reform" were a good thing—and, though it was not true, they were told tkat every other country under the sun had it—then every other nation should be doing better ' than ourselves. If they were not doing better than we, it was no use our adopting it. Would Tariff "Reformers" tell us of any nation at any time in the world's history which got exports into as many foreign countries as did Free Trade Britain? When that country was found out—it had not been found out yet—there would be some basis for the Tariff " Reform" argument, but SO long as these little islands had ships on every ocean, and its manufactures were found in every clime, then we were justified by the results to still</p>
        <p>ADHERE TO TER GREAT PRINCIPLES OF FREE TRADE.</p>
        <p>(Loud applause.) Warrington was no mean portion of the British Empire, and we were largely indebted to the trade that was done between us and other portions of the world for much of our prosperity. And vet he read that a certain chairman was not yet altogether satisfied either with Free Trade or with the Budget. Without the Budget, continued Mr. Wing, we could not hate had the Insurance Bill, for through the Budget we had got the State's contribution. But the Budget did not propose to undertake any portion of the payment which ought to be made outside of the State. (Hear, hear.) Having lucidly explained the details of the Insurance Bill, Mr. Wing referred to the provision of £1,500.000 to fight consumption. He said that to-day there were over 500,000 people suffering from this dread disease, and within twelve months 75,000 of these would have passed away, involving all the sadness, gloom, and despair, which inevitably followed. Dr. Addison, M.P. for Hoxton, and Professor of Pathology in London University, had said that in twenty-five years. if the Bill were passed in its entirety, we could abolish consumption. (Loud applause.) Consumption was now recognised as curable, and instead of only the wealthy being able to go to sanatoria. everyone who suffered would be able to go, and many would, no doubt, be restored to health and vigour. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>PT STAMPING OTT JONSUMPTION</p>
        <p>. would mean increased efficiency, it would raise us in the scale of nations, and it would have a reflex action on the life of the people. BY receiving money in times of sickness the physical condition of the people would be improved, and the physical condition of the people was of more importance than Dreadnoughts—(applaisse)—because the coming struggle was going to be based upon three things— &amp; sound physique, efficiency in work, and character. (Applause.) We could surround the country with Dreadnoughts, but if we did not have those three things we could not keep our trade. (Applause.) The Insurance Bill against invalidity was, he said. the most comprehensive attack that tad been made upon disease. After fifteen and a half years ex- perience of the Bill the Government would have a balance of £8,000,000, and with this they would be able to drop the age for old age pensions from seventy years to sixtv-Ate years. (Loud applause.) Every man under the scheme would be able to receive the full fruitage of his thrift in past years. (Applause.) At the conclusion of his speech Mr. Wing answered a number of questions put by members of the audience regarding the Insurance Bill. MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECH. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, who, on rising to address the meeting, was greeted with loud cheers, began his speech with a brief refer- ence to the Insurance Bill, which, he said, was an important part of that practical social reform which the Radicals and Progressives of Warrington had been talking about for a long time past, and to which the Government had put its hand. It was not bringing us the millennnium, but he for one could not exttggerate the depth of the importance which he attached to the subject and even the pathos of certain aspects of it. " I was in this room only two nialna ago," °rationed Mr. Crosfield. " and I had the privilege, quite like old times, of once more addressing the Speaker. (Laughter and applause). 1 thought it rather a happy augury. I spoke of its reminding one of old times, but I did not mean old times that were dead and gone for ever, and I hope I am not over-sanguine in viewing the position in that light'?</p>
        <p>• • 711' 0.0- 44 • • • _ - R.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>[aro'</head>
        <p>[aro'</p>
        <p>Id Smith, M.P.</p>
        <p>,Appluuse.) Mr. Crosfield next turned bis</p>
        <p>attention to</p>
        <p>LOCAL POLITICAL CONTROVERSIES. He noticed that their member, Mr. Harold</p>
        <p>Smith, had been on the war-path—(laughter) —and he (the speaker) would like to draw I attention not only to some things he had said, but to others which he had left unsaid. (Hear, hear.) " I notice," Mr. Crosfield, proceeded, "that Mr. Smith has once more evaded a challenge that I have pressed home a good many times in a very Practical way. I have challenged him—and the Tories in Warrington—either to cease displaying bills in their Conservative Club about ioreigners paying the taxes, and Tariff Reform giving increased employment and helping to raise wages, or to tell us in plain English just how this is to come about. (Applause.) Well, they go on making the same loose statements, but there is no proof. In his recent speeches Mr. Smith entirely evades the challenge I have thrown out, and, on the other hand, he says that I_ have called Tariff Reform a</p>
        <p>pernicious fraud without attempting to prove my attack upon it. Well, all I can say is that that really is not correct. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Smith cannot have followed our local Press as closely as I should have ex- pected a man in his position to do, or he would have known that I have time after time, and from platform after platform, put questions to him as regards both our foreign trade and our home trace. I have give.. him illustrations drawn from the old works at Bank Quay, and from my own experience in the soap trade in those active commercial days before I went to Westminster. I have told him of the astonishing way in which old, England, paying not fancy wages, if vouL like, but still a great deal higher wages than foreign soapmakers pay—(hear, hear)—is able to compete against and beat the foreigner. Not only can we wipe the floor with German competitors, but we can cross the Atlantic and with freights twice what they have to pay from America, and in spite of the enormous advantages which the Ameri- can manufacturer has in that marvellous country of the supply of raw materials on the spot, we are able to compete so success- fully that he gets much the worst of it in the long run. (Applause.) I have SHOWN MR. SMITH _ _ . .</p>
        <p>how this low cost of production which we get under Free Trade and which is so much more effective than all the niggling devices by which Protectionist countries seek to overcome their disadvantages—how that affects employ- ment in the export trade in our bigger indus- tries, which account for so much more labour than the soap trade. Then we have put another sample problem to bun. It is admilted that the home market, after all, is still the great market for the British manufacturer, taking perhaps half a dozen times as much of the produce of this commercial country as the foreign markets do. Well, we have challenged him on the home market. I have asked Mr. Smith to explain how, if our millions at home are the chief customers that our manufacturers have to look to, we are going to give increased employment if we introduce this reign of dear- , ness and scarcity and diminished capacity of our people to buy. It does not mean more employment; it means less employment. (Applause) Mr. Smith accuses me of saying that Tariff Reform is a pernicious fraud without proving what I say. I can only reply that what I have just re-stated for his benefit has been stated many a time before, but they have always evaded it, and I shall not be very surprised if they evade it again. (Applause.) As for the ' pernicious ' part of the expression, if it were only the introduction of the sort of vile system that they know under the name of " graft" in America,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>HATEFUL .CORRUPTION</head>
        <p>THE HATEFUL .CORRUPTION</p>
        <p>that pervades the politics of so many Pro- tectionist countries I for one should consider that, in its direct and indirect effects on the whole of our public life in England,. a pretty serious sacrifice, thoroughly justifying the epithet which I applied to it." (Applause.) Mr. Crosfield next touched upon Mr. Smith's declaration that the railway strike was the direct result of the Free Trade conditions which prevailed in this country. His exact words as reported were: "There were hundreds of men in Warrington who were out on strike because under the present Free ' Trade Government they could not get a living wage." An ounce of fact, continued Mr. Crosfield, was worth a ton of argument. It was, therefore, significant that, according to the Board of Trade Labour Statistics, in the year 1905 there were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>IN THE UNITED STATES 2,186 STRIKES,</head>
        <p>IN THE UNITED STATES 2,186 STRIKES,</p>
        <p>against 338 in this country: The number of workmen involved in the United States was 302,000, against 93,000 in this country. In 1909, the last year for which figures were available, the strikes in Protectionist Germany numbered 1,652, and in Free Trade Britain 350. (Applause.) He did not quote those figures to minimise the gravity of the problems with which we were confronted at home, hut it was simply nonsense to pretend that, with all our difficulties, we were not a great deal better off in the matter of strikes and the whole question of wages than they were in European protected countries. (Hear, hear.) He (Mr. Crosficld) was truly thankful that the principle of the minimum wage, which the Government embodied in the Trade Boards Bill on a small scale, was going to have a very wide extension. lie was sure that all the unrest in the labour world had hurried on the time and helped to strengthen the demand for the extension of that principle. Personally' he thought it was an awful pity that the whole of the capitalists did not voluntarily recognise that, with the increased cost of living, LABOUR WAS NOT GETTING THAT SHARE of the fruits of industry which ought to have gone to it. (Loud applause.) Whatever might have been obtained through the strikes, he greatly regretted that the British sense of justice and fair play did not come into opera- tion a little sooner. (Hear, hear.) He also believed that the strike had brought UR a great deal nearer to the time when this country would decide that, after all, it Was better for the State to nationalise the rail- ways. While they could not contemplate without dismay the colossal waste and the misery entailed by strikes, they could not sacrifice the right of the workman to strike in the last resort—at all events, in industries outside those in State employment. Nor could they demand that the employers should give up the right to lock-out in the last event. But of all the propositions he had seen during this sad and difficult time, nothing struck him as so well calculated to minimise the evil of strikes as ROPOSAL BY Sis MACARA,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>ADMIRABLE PROPOSAL BY SIR CHARLES</head>
        <p>THE ADMIRABLE PROPOSAL BY SIR CHARLES</p>
        <p>which he was thankful to believe the Govern- ment intended to take action upon. (Applause.) "Mr. Smith advises me," con- tinued Mr. Crosfield, " to try and regain the confidence of my fellow-townsmen, w bleb I have lost. (Laughter.) It would he imper- tinent of me to attempt any estimate as to what extent I may enjoy your confidence, but I would like to say that whenever the time comes for me to submit myself to your opinion, and I have to ask for a certificate of con- fidence, I will certainly appeal to my fellow- townsmen—(loud applause)—to those who, after all, know something of opinion in this town, and not to a gentleman who knew so little about it that I observe that during tb ' recent troublous times he opened his news- paper in the morning not without some appre- hension that there might have been a develop- ment of hooliganism in Warrington. (Laughter.) I am mighty glad that Warring- ton in that respect has compared well with some recent episodes in the House of Commons. in which certain members of the so-called • die-hard' or • die-in-the-last-ditch' party enjoyed a not too enviable notoriety.</p>
        <p>(Laughter.) There is an old saying that people who live in glass houses should not throw stones." (Laughter.) Returning to Mr. Smith's insinuation that Free Trade was responsible for the recent</p>
        <p>strikes and also for 'sweating, Mr. Crosfield said there had been a</p>
        <p>WORLD-WIDE ADVANCE IN PRICES of many articles, owing to shortage of supply That, however, had operated to a much worse extent in protected countries than in Free Trade England. Quite recently the " Daily Mail " stated that " world-wide labour an-</p>
        <p>rest prevails, strikes and riots and threats of strikes being reported yesterday from France, the United States, Austria, Germany Bel- gium, Spain, and Portugal "—all protected countries. The poverty and misery in souse of the minor protectionist countries were tragic, dreadful, and shocking, but what of the conditions in the most prosperous, pro- gressive, and capable of all the great Con- tinental nations—the protected country of i Germany? The following descAption of the STATE OF THINGS IN GERMANY was given in the issue of September 24th of the " Vorwaarts " newspaper : " Comrades, we are living in sorrowful</p>
        <p>times, and even graver times—are approach- iug. The increase in the cost of all the necessaries of life has caused the distress among the working classes to rise to a height that is in tolerable, and drives largo masses of the population to despair. This Agrarian and Industrial Protectionists who profit by the misery of the masses close their ears to the cries of those who are ex-</p>
        <p>ploited, while the Government, which is merely the Protection Executive, does not trouble to devise effective remedies." The same paper in its leader spoke of the immense increase in the cost of living, and demanded that the frontier should be opened and the Protection barriers against foreign muntries should be pulled down. (Applause.) Was it not the very acme of political in- sincerity to pretend that we were going to cure our own troubles by adopting a policy under which such miseries 28 he had de- scribed were possible? He bad never tried to stimulate race prejudice against any country, nor had he lent himself to any anti- German cry. He was only too glad that we should copy that industrious and enterpris- ing people whenever we could usefully take a leaf out of their book—such as in town-plan- ning and technical education—but we were not going to follow their example in regard to Protection—or conscription either. (Loud applause.) Mr. Wing might not know that they had heard more in Warrington about the introduction of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>COMPULSORY MILITARY SERVICE</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY MILITARY SERVICE</p>
        <p>than in most towns in England. What case was there for forcing conscription on an un- willing people? We had got a navy with what the Prime Minister called an unchal- lenged and unchallengeable command of the waters. He knew from authoritative quar- ters that never in the whole history of the navy were the 130,000 men employed in con- nection with it in finer form that at present. The personnel of our navy was the object of the unfeigned envy of all Continental powers that had to see to self-defence. We had got an army, and was there any other country that could do what we did under our volun- tary system, and provide that great over-sea army for Imperial purposes—an army full of keenness and enterprise—as also were our Territorials—(applause)—in spite of occa- sional discouragement which might be im- proved upon? We had 270000 Territorials—a larger number than Lord Haldane con- sidered the necessary minimum of strength in peace times, and five-sixths of the total establishment of the force. When they asked the experts what was conceivable in regard to invasion, they told them that the largest number of men they could conceive slipping into this country—if they were such fools as to come—was 70,000. The Admiralty, how- ever, said that estimate was a great deal too high. But supposing an enemy of 70,000 did come and we had 270,000 slightly trained Territorials and the Regulars as well? He almost apologised for discussing such a ques- tion. Did not everybody in the room know that the invaders would be simply eaten up? (Applause.) Bismarck once said, " I think I could get some of my soldiers into Britain, if I wanted, but I'm 'hanged if I know how I would get them out again." (Laughter and . applause.) He would not criticise the • patriotic motives; be appreciated the zeal of those who provided us with first one play and then another to warn us in advance of the ; fearful miseries we would undergo under in- vasion. What he did question was the judg- ment and the capacity to discriminate be- tween fact and illusion of those who year ; after year in this district so MIS-SPENT ALL THAT. ENERGY</p>
        <p>and fine public spirit on such a question. It was a little preposterous that we should bear so much of scares that were intended to make old ladies so uneasy that they were afraid to go to sleep at night, lest they should awaken to the melodious strains of a German hand in the back garden. (Laughter.) When pressed a little hard, the advocates of con- scription would turn round and say, "Perhaps we are safe, after all. But what 'a good thing, physically and morally." That would not do. Military drill was not the best kind of physical training, and if they wanted to advance the physique and well-being of the people, at about one-fourth or one-tenth the cost of compulsory training, they could do it. a great deal better by giving some of our slum population in their younger years a chance of a decent holiday in the country in the summer time. (Applause.) With the experience of conscription on the Continent staring its in the face, the advocates of the system were not going to persuade us that it was going to improve the morale of the English people. On this aspect of the question Mr. Crosfield gave the evidence of a distinguished medical man as to the brutal and callous methods which obtained in German hospitals, and also quoted his remark that " this gospel of Monti and iron sometimes degenerates into blood and money." If a nation founded itself on an appeal to force, they sometimes found that fraud was dogging its footsteps. Conscription did not raise the national morale, but they got a certain hardening of nature running all through the people. We did not want that in England. We wanted to preserve our feelings of • common humanity, of consideration for other people, and to avoid the hardening of lifc as between man and man, and between class and class. (bond applause.) THE WAGES OF CORPORATION WORKMEN. Mr. Harry New proposed • vote of thanks to the chairman and the speakers. He said that that meeting was the opening of a vigorous campaign which would be carried on in Warrington on behalf of democracy during the winter. Mr. New referred to Mr. Harold Kmith's statement that the indirect Cause of the labour unrest was the Apeech delivered by Mr. Lloyd George at Limehouse. Fancy • snan saying that, commented the speaker, who had just come from the House of Common. after joining in hooliganism which would not do credit to • gutted' (Ataolause.) We were now on an upward grade in Warrington, and God blesn those who voted for an increase in the W•geß of able-bodied labourers under the ('orporation. lApplause.) 'those were the sort of politicians they wanted- men who were doing something for humanity. It. was all very well to preach Imperiali.m, but what they wanted were men who would work un- obtrusively, and vote for wages which would bring increased comforts and leas anxiety to the home. He hoped that their action would not be 104 sight of on November 1. (Applause.) Mr. M. Llewellyn seconded the motion. and it was carried with acclammation. In replying to the vote of thank., Mr. Wing asked if he might convey a message of encourage- ment to Mr. Lloyd George in pressing forward the insurance Bill. The audience at once cried oat "Yes!" and Mr. Wing promised to make known their opinion. The meeting concluded with hearty cheers for the Liberal Government, Mr. erotifield, and Mr. Wing.</p>
        <p>The F R Benson Shakespearean Company at Liverpool. On Monday to at the F. It. Itenam C..inpitny will commence a fortnight's season at the Shako-veers Thea.re Livernool, timing which time they will play eight different Shakespasrean plays and " The Piper," which made such • big euccesa in Liverpool last Autumn. This is the sum gent mtmpany that the Benson Company has ever travelled and includes that well-known actress, Ellen O'Malley in all tits principal parts that Mrs Boom wed to play.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WORKING MAN'S BEST</head>
        <p>THE WORKING MAN'S BEST</p>
        <p>BEVERAGE.</p>
        <p>It is not the name that makes the article good, but the article which makes the name good. If Vi-Crcoa had not been as good as it is—as good as it rossibly can be—a man or woman might have bought it once or twice (or even thrice, just to make sure that his or her judgment was not at fault;, but would people drink it twice a day, year in and year I out, as so many thousands actually do. Certainly not! The truth is, there is not a more, invigorating, sustaining, nerve-soothing or body-build- ing food beverage on the market. lien and women in every walk of life testify to the great benefit, both mentally and bodily, which they have derived from Vi-Cocoa, but none more loudly or persistently than that great army of workers who toil herd and long in our mills, docks, warehouses, factories, foundries, and shipyards, and those who are constantly exposed to the inclemency of the weather—such as policemen, postmen, carriers, night watchmen, etc. If you've not tried VI-Cocoa before, try it now. Give Vi-Cecoa a trial. Never mind what you've been drinking at breakfast or supper in the past, start drinking Vi-Cocoa to-day—ycu'll feel the benefit of it at once. Do nct ask your gr, car for " Cocoa "—ask for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Wawa</head>
        <p>Wawa</p>
        <p>—lt makes all the difference. Every grocer sells Vi-Cccoa in Gd. packets and 9d. and Is. Gd. tins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WARRINGTON BAND UF HOPE UNION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BAND UF HOPE UNION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Coencil was held in Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge street, which is the new head- quarters of the Union, on Thursday evening. Mrs. James Dodds, president of the Union occupied the chair. The hen secretary, Mr F Gandy presented the 13th annual report of the work of the Union showing that a large amount of valuable work amongst the young had keen accomplished. The bon treasurer, Mr R Pierce Williams presented the financial statement which was considered satisfactory. The officers for the past year Pere thanked for their services. Mr H Wooley one of the assistaut secretaries wished to be relieved from all active work for a time. Mr Wooley's resignation was accepted %Otis deep regret, he haring held the position for a number of years during which time he has done his work most efficiently. Mr. C. S. Fellows, the other hon. resistant secretary, also asked to be relieved. he having been appointed secretary of the new organization recently started by the Union, The Warrington Temperance Gullet. Mrs. James Dodds was again unanimously re-elected president. In accept- ing this position Mrs. Dodds expressed her delight in the office, it being ouch a great pleasure to work amongst such an earnest band of workers. The follow- ing ladies and gentlemen were elected vice-presidents subject to their consent. Revs .1 Yonge, II Wall, W Cooper, W T Jaye, IV Reid, W A Beckett, E Morgan, II Williamson, 8.A., II Middlewood, J S Chedburn. Curtis and H. Ogden : Miss B Duttou, Mrs Simpson, Mrs E V Walker, Mrs W Walker, Mrs Thomas Owen, Mr W Clatworthy, Mr A Mounfield, Mr J J Firth, Mr Ben Few, Mr H Thornton, Mr W T Hesse, Mr J Griffiths and Mr Rowlands. Other officers elected were: Treasurer, Mr R Pierce Williams; Hon. Secretary, Mr F (land,: Hon Assistant Secretaries, Mr L H Elliott and h Fellows; Press Secretary, Mr Whitfisld ; Auditors, Messrs P Howard and J Thornily ; Stocktakers, Mrs Whitmore, and Messrs Howard and Thorniley ; Representatives to Lancashire end Cheshire Band of Hope Union, Messrs J Tborniley, C S Fellows and Watkins; Organist and Accompanist, Mr A W Clatworthy ; Conductor, Mr A Spur, a. Arrangements were made to hold a united after service meeting on Temperance Sundae ,Noverober 12th,and the workers were invited to enter heartily into the scheme to cbtain a million more new members throughout the United Kingdom this ;ear, this beitg the Coronation effort of the U.E.8.11 U.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>The monstre meet and opening run of the members of West Lancashire C.C.A. clubs takes plate this Filer- noon from the headquarters of the Sacred Heart Harriers, schoolrooms, Borough-road, St. Helens, start- -11)7. at 3 p rn sharp. The distance of the run will be about six miles in all, and concludes with races over the last mile for both seniors and novices with the definition—"A novice shall be deemed one who has never won a first, second or third prize for running at open sports, or who has never won a medal in the L. and D.C.C. championship', nor won a medal in the Northern, Midland, Southern or National championships, or in any Arcs of a similar kind. Prizes won in Club Events shall not debar a runner from competing in this race. Previous winmera of the first prizes in "Opening Huns" not eligible." First, second and third prizes will be given to the , successful runners in each "break" which will be run separately, the seniors first and the novices a few minutes later. Novices must wear distinctive tickets which will be supplied, on application, by the Hon. Secretary, before the start. The annual general meeting of the Northern will be held at the Hotel de Ville, King-street, Leeds, to-day,at 6.15 p m. ltylands W.R.C. Harriers are getting into their stride and finding anew trail. Promising reports are heard concerning their novices of whom J. Thornley and A. Hollington are at present most prominent. Warrington A.C. have booked the Assembly Rooms at the Conservative Club for their dance on November 10th and bold their opening run from the Orford Hotel, Gorsey-lane, on Saturday next, start at 3.30 p.m., all members are requested to attend, intending members and visitors are cordially invited. Now is the time for novices to start so that they can have the advice and help of experienced runners before the latter begin racing. Athletes who have ccmpeted at unregistered meet- ings should now apply for re-qualifloation. Mr. C. Berry, of 9,lledford-street, Stockton Heath, will supply the necessary information.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MASS OF RUNNING SORES.</head>
        <p>MASS OF RUNNING SORES.</p>
        <p>A Bad Leg That Doctors Couldn't Cure.</p>
        <p>ZAM-BEK ROOTS DISEASE OUT COMPLETELY.</p>
        <p>Mrs. E. E. Goodchild's leg was so bad that it was covered with thousands of little sores. Hospital and doctots' treatment couldn't move them,. but Zam-Buk c'eared the lot away and grew in their place healthy new skin. Mrs. Goodchild who is the farmer's wife at New House Farm, Wrotham, Kent, gave this start- ling story to a reporter : " About eight years ago, when living near Gravesend, a small pimple with a white head came on the lower pelt of my right leg. Rubbing the itchy pimple brought the bead off and caused a slight dis- charge, from which there quickly grew an accumula- tion of thousands if these little discharging pimples, cove, tag my leg from toes to knee. The discharge dried quickly, and afterwards ecaled, taking the skin off as well, and leaving the flesh raw and sore. " The skin that formed over the sore places threw out fresh layers of these itching pimples, patch after patch, all over the leg. The limb kept swelling up until it became nearly twice the usual size, and during the last two years I was unable to wear a boot and couldn't wain. " When the disease filet started I tried ointments, salves, and other remedies, but finding no benefit I went to the Gravesend Hospital. I was an out-patient there for two years. As the disease then still covered my leg I gave up going. When we came here I gave a private doctor's ointments a trial, but with no good effect. So I gave them up and attended the Maidstone Hospital for nine months. Here agaio, however, the treatment was unsuccessful. Naturally, after all these experieness, I lost heart. . - .</p>
        <p>"It was only half-heartedly that I started using Zam-Buk, although it had been strongly recommended But Zam-Buk made such wonderful smproeement in toy leg - soothing the pain and itching, and bringing grrat comfort to the whole limb - that my hopes revived.</p>
        <p>'1 persevered with this magic balm, which did more good than anything I had ever tried before. Zara-Buk cleared away the masses of diechargiog sores in an astonishing way, and rooted out all disease from my system. Instead of a leg covered with awful sores, I was left with as clear and healthy a skin as aryone could wish. Zans.Bith had done what hospitals and doctors had failed to accomplish. No words of mine mu: express my gratitude for this splendid cure." You have an excellent example not only in Mrs. Goodchild, but also in millions of others who in their faith to Zam-Buk and nothing Don't run risks by depending upon ccirnmon oint- ments or crude home-made salves, the coarseness of which is a positive danger. There is only one Zam.Buk, and that is sold in the familiar square green package sealed with the Government stamp. There is no getting away from the fact that Zara-Buk is necessary in every home. Scarcely a day passes without father, mother, child, or maid requiring its , healing aid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE. PILE-DRIVING CEREMONIES AT BRIDGE FOOT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE. PILE-DRIVING CEREMONIES AT BRIDGE FOOT.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING HI!</p>
        <p>Thursday will rank as a memorable day in local history, for it witnessed the first practical step in the erection of the new Warrington Bridge, which his been the subject of agitation and controversy for so many years past. In the presence of a large gather- ing, which was folly representative of tl-e varied interests of the town, the first two piles of the new structure were driven by the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) and Alderman Smethurst (chairman of the Bridges Committee). While Warringtonians will regret the disappearance of the present handsome bridge, which has served the town since 1837, it has long been recognised that the width of roadway pro- vided-28 feet—is totally inadequate for the immense volume of traffic which makes use of this important connecting link between Lancashire a,cl Cheshire. The new bridge, which is r stimated to cost about £20,000, will be 801 t wide—only two feet le-s than the famous Westminster Bridge—and will have two footpaths, each 10ft • wide. It will be the finest example in the kingdom of ferro-concrete work, the C.msid4re system of reinforcemennt hsving bees adopted. The new bridge has been designed ly Mr. J. J. Webster, of Westminster, an eminent engineer who has very close associations wilt Warringten, and the contractors are Messrs. Alfred Thorne and Sot s. Mr. J. Mansfield Cobb is the resident et gineer and Mr. Grenville Hodgson will inspect and test every load of material del;vered on the works. The pile-driving ceremony ps,sed off withoat the slightest untoward event. Some striking speeches were delivered and interesting refer eoce eves made to history of the bridges which have preceded the present structure. It is expected that about two years will elapse before the bridge is completed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CEREMONY.</head>
        <p>THE CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>At noon the members of the Town Council, officials and other prominent townsmen assembled at the Town Hall, where a procesion was f rmed, and traversed the main streets to the site of the new bridge. First came a detachment of police, and next the Perfection Soap Worka Band, fresh from their great national triumph which had so fittingly coincided with an historic local event. Preceded by the mace and sword bearers, walked the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph), who wore his robes and ebain of office. He had n his right hand Mr. Han Id Smith, M.P., and the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), and on his left Mr. A. H. Crosfield. The procession was °cm- pitted by members of the Council, offizials and other townsmen including the Deputy Mayor (Alderman Forshaw), Rev. E. C. E. Carleton (Mayor's Chap'ain), and Mr. Robert Pierpoint. A huge crowd had gathered on the Arpley side of the river where they had an excellent view of the ceremony which took place on the Wilderspool bank. The borough standard had been hoisted and there was a liberal display of bunting. A crowning touch was given to the scene by the bright srnshine which opportunely burst forth, in phasant contrast to the sombre conditions which have prevailed of late. The asiembly having grouped itself round the improvised platform,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>AN INTERESTING HISTORY.</head>
        <p>AN INTERESTING HISTORY.</p>
        <p>The MAVoR said that was a red letter day in the annals of Warrington. (Hear, hear.) Some might think they were making too much of that ceremony,but it was an important stage in a very vexed question. Thase who knew the almast ;interminable difficnlties they had encountered in various directions before they covld inaugurate that great undertaking would feel the truth of what be said when he stated that that was a memorable day in the bor iugh. Their esteemed friend Alderman Smethurst had taken an almost super-human interest in the scheme, and it might naturally have been supposed that he would drive the first pile. The bridge had teen the apple of his eye—or rather one of them, because the aldermen was so loyally devoted to the welfare of the town, that he had at heart all schemes for its advancement. (Applause). Oa that ground Alderman Smethurst had the premier claim but as be (the speaker), by the indulgence of the C amen and, he hoped, the goodwill of tte townspeople, had the Lotiour to occupy the position of chief citizen and to represent his fellow townsmen in their every day life—not in Imperial politics as his friend Mr. Smith, a much greater luminary did—he felt it to be his duty to accept the invitation to drive the Int pile. (Applause.) Alderman Forshaw had also taken a great interest in the bridge—(hear, hear)—and he was mainly instrumental in haaing it 80 feet wide. It would therefore have been appropriate if be alao could have driven a pile, but that was found incon- venient. However, the alderman knew that the Town Council desired to honour him in that respect and they hoped he would take that as sufficient:Vouching upan the ' history of the bridge his Worship said he was indebted to Mr. C. Madeley, librarian and curator, for some interesting &amp;tails. Warrington Bridge had always been noted as an important link between Lancashire and Cheshire—is fact it was the first bridge over the Mersey within thirty miles of the sea and it stood on the main western route from England to Scotland. The first bridge way built over 600 years ago, but they knew nothing about it except that it , lasted fifty years. The second bridge was built about the end of the fourteenth century and stood for 100 years. Both bridges were built as acts of charity by private people. At that time the right of charging toll was retained by people who had nothing to do with the building of the bridge, nor did they attempt to keep it in repair. On that account various means were devised to defray the expanses. Pert was raised by tolls in the Market-place; even the clergy gave indulgences,and some attempt was made by the country to mice rates in another manner. But all those expedients failed. The third bridge was built by the first Earl of Derby at the end of the fifteenth century. It lasted 300 years and would have sufficed for a longer period had it not been for the fact that it was only 18 feet wide and traffia began to increase very rapidly. Another bridge—a wooden one—was balk in the early part of the 19 h century, but it was so steep that a horse could hardly pull an ordinary loaded vehicle over it, and the structure was no badly designed theta pier had to be placed underneath the arch to pre- vent it from collapsing. That bridge lasted twenty years, after which they came to the present bridge, which they all regretted had to be removed. (Hear, hear). It was constructed at the beginning of the reign of Queen Victoria, after whom it was called (Applause.) The first two bridges were built by charity, one by Lord Derby, and the other two by the county inaugurating was and the bridge they were now was the first to be buikby the inhabitants of Warring- ton themselves. (Applause.) " This is our very own bridge" concluded the Mayor, "and, as the cause which led to Lord Derby's bridge becoming obsolete does not obtain, I hope it will long remain as a monument of engineering skill and of the enterprise of Warriugton in 1011." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>FINE BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>A FINE BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Mc. .1. J. W&amp;HSTER, the engineer, explained that the new bridge would have a span of 139 feet and would be 80 feet wide. There was only one bridge in London which exceeded or even approached that width, and that was the Westminster Bridge which wee 82 feet wide. Vsughill Bridge was 68 feet and the new London bridge, after its widening, wool 1 be only 115 feet wide. Therefore Warrington would be able to pride itself upon a very fine bridge, when the under- telling was completed. In the erection of the bridge 182 miles of rods, 4,000 tons of coucrete, 400 tone of steel and 410 piles (in addition to 200 timber piles pro- vided by the contractor for his own purposes) would be used.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>DRIVING THE PILES.</head>
        <p>DRIVING THE PILES.</p>
        <p>The ceremony of driving the nest pile then took place. By pulling a rope, the Mayor released a huge block of iron which descended on the head of the pile, amid the cheers of the crowd. Others who afterwards took turns at driving the pile more deeply into the river bed were the Mayoress, Alderman Forsbaw, Mrs Webster, Mrs Robert Pierpoint, Mr Harold Smith, M.P., Mr A H Crosfleld, Mr Robert Pierpoint, the Rev E C E Carleton (mayors chaplain) and the Rector (Rev Canon Willie.) Before beginning the driving of the second pile, Alderman SMETHURST said he was sure the people of Warrington would be delighted that they had made a beginning with the new bridge. It had been a laborious task, as they had had t, consider all kinds of schemes and combat opposition from various quarters. At last, however, they were able to launch the scheme and he sincerely bepul that a good beginning would means good ending. (Applause.) He regarded that day as one of the proudest of his life. They had been talking about widening the bridge for over thirty years and they had had many plans before them, but he was thanktul to say that the ferro-con- crete scheme which he suggested and which met with much opposition, had been adopted. They had it on the I highest authority that ferroconcrete was almost ever- lasting. (Applause). Alderman SMETHURST then took the lead in driving the second pile, and others who afterwards pulled the rope were Mrs Smethurst, Mr John Fairclough, Councillor H Roberto, Aldermen W Bolton, A Bennett, John Evans. James Evans, W Pierpoint, T Burton, Councillor Wilkinson, Rev IV Brace:amp, Councillors C Jackson Holmes, T Birtles, J Sankey. T S Steel, T H Ashcroft, J D.ckenson, J Halliwell, W H J Jenkins, J Skelland, and it Henshall, Mr W Peter Itylands, Mr F A Frost, and Mr J II Smethurst. The Rev. E.C. E.CARgirrotr then offered prayer for all who would be engaged in building the bridge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>iTORIC OCCASION.</head>
        <p>iTORIC OCCASION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wansrau then presented the Mayor and Alderman Smethurst with a handsome memento of the occasion, in the shape of a silver salver bearing an engraved reproduction of the new bridge and a suitable Inscription. He announced that a similar gift was in preparation for Alderman Forshaw. , The Mayor and Alderman Smethurst were also I presented with the roges with which they had performed the ceremony. The National Anthem having been sung, the pro- cession MB5 re-formed and returned to the Town</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>LUNCHEON.</head>
        <p>THE LUNCHEON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Plerpoint's Farewell to Warrington</p>
        <p>STRIKING SPEECH.</p>
        <p>After the ceremony • large company sat down to luncheon at the Town Hall. The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) presided and there were also present : Mr Harold Smith, M.P., Mr A Ii Crosfield, Robert Pierpoint, Revs Canon Willie. W Brececamp, E C E Carleton, Aldermen Forshaw, Pierpoint, Tinnion, James Evans, John Evens, Sennett, Smethurst ; Caunoillors J Wilkinson Hearth:lll, Dickenson, Barnwell, Bellian, Steel, Chnotler, Sankey, Birtlia Roberts, Meson J J Webster (engineer), A Thorne (oon is tar). P Thorne, T B Shore, G ft Hodgson,T Ridgway, Seger Owen, (i) Roberts, J k 1 Cobb, Grosvenor, 3 W Hayward, L V0i607, Llt Rowton, R Roberts, W Roberts, PII Bennett, Val Furnese, John Fairclough, Vera B Davis", H Hatton, J H Smethurst, A Nield, Dr Harold Joseph, J H Joseph, R A Joseph, J Richardson, It H Meldrum, J Lyon Whittle, A T Rallaway, T H Spinks, J Gray, T Longdin, A 111 Ker, C Stone, J Moore Murray, F V L Mathias, Dr Ribbed, J Taylor, Vi/ 11 Wcoocock. C Madeley. The entering was admirably carried out by the Winruarla igh Cafe Co., Ltd. During the luncheon a choice musical programme was played ny Mr. Taylor's orcheatra. The loyal toasts were honoured on the proposition of the M•YOR. " SUCCESS TO THE NEW BRIDGE." Alderman SAlitTriuitsT, in submitting this toast, said th tt no one would grieve more than he if any unfor- tunate accident occurred during the construction of the bridge. It was a difficult matter to establish the foul dations inn ricer bed when they had the tides to contend with, and altogether the construction of a road- way over the Mersey would boa trying engineering feat. However, they hoped and believed that the contractors' efforts would be attended with great success and in case of any emergency the officials of the Corporation would render every assistance in their power. With regard to the engineer, Mr. Webster was a fellow townsmen and they trusted him for his own sake and for the sake of the good old town. (Applause.) They all looked forward to the day when they would be able to walk over the new bridge end they hoped the piles which had been driven that day would have been no" well and truly "Sued that they would admirably fulfil their purpose. (Applause). hIr.J. J. Weesvgn, the engitmer of the new bridge, in responding said Alderman Smethurst seemed unduly :apprehensive. Tee new bridge works would be perfectly cafe ; there was absolutely no fear of any- thing giving way. That occasion was doubly interest- ing to him (the speaker) as it concided with his pro- fessional jubilee. (Applause.) Ile hoped the new bridge would have something to recommend it in addition to the fact that it was designed by an old townsman. He thought they might rent as-tured that it would be carried through in a proper, substantial manner of which they would be proud. (Applause.) Everything had been thoughly thres:ted out in his office. They had a good contractor in Mr. Thorne who bad work,d for him (N 1 r. Webster) for many yearn on various undertakings and had carried them out with the great-st success in every instance. (Applause.) He had no doubt that the same happy results would attend his work at Warrington. The contractor had two able lieutenants in Mr. J. Mansfield Cobb and Mr. Granville Hodgson, two old pupils of his (Mr. Webster's). of whom he was proud, lie believed that when the Warrington people knew more about them they would he equally proud of their service- Nothing would be wanting on his (the speaker's) part to ensure that the new bridge should be worthy of the town and its illustrious associations. (Applause.) Mr. ALFRED THORNE, the contractor, also responded. He said that in carrying out the undertaking is would he his great aim to see that everything in the structure ann of the test. (Applause.) Ile would do his utmost to overcome all difficulties and he trusted that success would crown the efforts of all concerned. (Applawse).</p>
        <p>TILE MAYOR."</p>
        <p>Alderman FORSHAW submitted the health of the Mayor. He said that notwithstanding his professional life, his Worship was performing the duties of his office —which was no sinecure—with industry, energy and zeal, and they Were all pleased he had consented to accept the Mayoralty for another year. (Applause). Having thanked the Mayor and Mr. Webster for their kindly acts towards him in connection with the pile. driving ceremony, Alderman Forshaw said be must admit that he was anxious to drive a pile because of the warm interest he bad taken in the scheme during Ms mayoralty. The Alderman concluded with a tribute to the Mayoress for the admirable and gracious viciv in which she had performed the duties of her ciliate. (Applause.) The MAYOR, who bad a most enthusiastic reception on rising to resy•ad, sail he had had some little anxiety, not with regard to the bridge itself, but relating to concomitant circumstances. When a kody of men were associated with an undertaking, although they might be friendly in their disposition towards it and vie with each other to further its success, they wculd always have little heart-burnings and, perhaps, jealousies. Ile could only give himself credit for having done his best to Boothe those natural humane feelings, 133 that they might come into line with everything that was happy, cheerful and comfortable. With regard to the bridge' he did not think they need have any anxiety. Surely if in the fifteenth century the bridge which Lord Derby built at his own expense (having bought the land and the tolls and put en end to the great abuses) lasted three hundred years and would probably have suffered for te ice that period if it had been large enough, they need have no concern about the new bridge, with all the resources of the best science and workmanship, which would be erected under the supervision of an eminent engineer and an equally efficient contractor. (Applause.) In returning thanks for the Mayoress, his Worship mid it had been an arduaus task for her, as well as for him, to fulfil the duties during the past year—much harder than he coui i deecribe. In addition to presiding over pleasant meet;ogs of the Council— (laughter)—there were many social functions demanding attention, as well as meetings good and otherwise— (laughter)—and one had to know everything about everything—in fact to be a living encyclopwlia. (Laughter). "THE CORPORATION OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD AND WARRINGTON.S COMMERCIAL PROSPIRITY.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, who was warmly received, said the toast he had the honour to propose bad special importance and was particularly appropriate on that occasion, because it was owing to the courageand entarprise of their' city fathers" in the Corporation that they were celebrating such a notable evert that day. (Applause). In the admirable speech which Alderman Forshaw delivered, he alluded to the increas- ing responsibility, the tax on the time and energy of the chief citizen of Warrington is thee days, and those remarks had been very naturally enforced in his worship's charming reply. No doubt the same might be said of•the Corporation as a whole. The undoubted tendency in these days of devolution was to throw larger responsibility and to demand increasing atten- tion and work from the members of our civic corpora. Coml. " Ices only feel "continued Mr. Croeleld " that it must be a real source of satisfaction to the members of the Warrington Corporation to look around and see the EROGRESS THAT IS BEING MADE IN THE TOWN —I had almost said the progress and the prcsperity. Whether I should be trenching on somewhat deLcate I ground if I went that far I am not sure. (Laughter.) But I am sure that when the members of the Council turn their eyes to Bridge-street and Market Gate and see the progress that is being made out Bank Quay way, and more surprising still, in the dirootion of Dalton Bank— (laughter)—if I might quote the language of the famous prisoner of Chalon, 'their eyes must be filled with a glad surprise ' Slat in these days when we are living under sock a foolish and, I had almost said, fatal flacal policv—(laughtar)--it is possible for a commercial ' community like this still to eke outs precarious exist- ewe. (Laughter.) With all my hart, I congratulate the Corporation on this great enterprise the inaugura- tion of which we have witnessed to-day. It strikes my mind as something like the keystone to the arch in the great constructive work of regeneration of the whole town which the members of this old Corpora- tion are undertaking. I must also congratulate them on having Councillor Dr. Joseph to preside over their labours for another year. (Applause). We have all known the Mayor es a man of parts, of many and varied accomplishments, and everything to-day bas been con- ducted by his Worship with all the adroitness and tact which we have cometo expect sea matter of course. [don't know whether, among his many attainments, the Mayor Is an expert with the billiard cue or not (laughter)—but I remember many years ago, when my uncle lived at Bank House, the billiard r on, was not infrequently occupied. My cousins were pretty expert 'rah the ivories, and anybody who made • good long winning hazard out of hall, • good straight drive into the top pocket, we generally celled it a pile delver. (Laughter.) We have bad a lot of pile-driving to-dry and we have all come to blows, (Laughter.) I have</p>
        <p>never seen any assembly of men, especially where some little diversity of opinion is included—llan;hter) hob-nobbing and coming to blows in WWI good natured fashion. It reminds me of an event which took pl toe in that billiard room. A second cousin of mine, culled Tom Blanc, who usud to come to %Varrington, once marched into the room and, Poling my uncle there, slid ',dorland, I'll write the epitsch." That was in the days when at Bank Q lay we manufactured dip candles. Toe manufactures lin the old works to-d sy are pretty comprehensive—l I should be afreid to say what we do not make—but lam certain dip candles are not included. (Laughter.) At that time we made them and we found the competition of that eminent firm, Messrs. Price and Co., rather formidable. 'Porn Slane gave Morland Crosfleld his epitaph, which ran as follows : Header, if read thou coast, then read and know The ashes of a soap and cradle maker lie below. In this his trade he beat all brother chips Salve Price and Co.,but they ne'er dealt in dips. Of impudence he had the moat abundant share His faults were many and his virture rare. Yet if thou'rt wise go, reader, take the hint So do likewise and thou wilt ne'er repent.</p>
        <p>The story is that he never repeater' that last couplet. On that occasioo they good-humouredly came to blows and he retired somewhat expeditiously from the billiard room. (Laughter.) In</p>
        <p>THIS PUGNACIOUS ACUR whether tone happens to be the happy possessor of a Quaker aocestry or not, we are all familiar with blows tram our earlieat childhood. There are times when we have heard same gloomy zealot A dinging o' the pu'pit, And taking good care to Hood the congregation weel o'er the pit. Then some of no have also hal experiences—familiar to my friends Mr. Robert Pierpoint and Mr. Harold Smith, whom we are so glad to see here to-day—(bear I hear)—familiar also to nip, for an all too brief period, am sorry to say—(laughter)—l allude to the blows with which that historic box in the Home of Commons is resonant when eminent statesman want to emphasise a weighty areument or pitch into an ening opponent." (Laughter.) Again speaking in a reminiscent vein, Mr. Crosfield said that on one occasion Richard Cobden, when staying at Walton Lea, remarked to his (the speaker's) noels George —"I like this place. You look north and Bee the signs of industry. You look south and you have the beauties of nature." Toe great work which the Warrington Corporatioa had so well Inaugurated that day was going to help everyone in ' Warrington to profit by the !advances of industry, and to fad more encouragement and convenience in getting across the water and enjoying the infinite charm and variety of dear, quaint old Cheshire. The improved communication would therefore be a great advantage to the town in every way and the Corporation were greatly to be congratulated on their enterprise and courage. (Applanae). Alderman PIERPOINT responded. He said that in Mr. Crosflelda' speech there wan not one word of abuse. (Laughter.) Corporations, and more especially the Corporation of Warrington ever since its inception but particularly of late years. bad been surrounded by very ciptioos critics and by those who had not hesitated to apply the most opprobious terms to it. Whether they were deserved or not was a matter for individual opinion. Personally he had always maintained that the members of the Council were engaged in a good work and one which was important to every inhabitant of Warrington. They looked after the people not only in the day but in the night. They attended to their education and health and used their best endeavours to keep them elem. Why, in the some of heaven, did they not get credit for all that? If a few of them at their monthly meet- ings took the opportunity of blackguarding each other, it did not matter to the outside public. It was a mutter between themselves. (Laughter.) It was a lintutifal trait is the characters of all the members that the harder the hitting, the more they loved the man who hit them. (Laughter.) Alderman Pierpoint concluded by mentioned the interesting facts that, a distinguished relative of his (Mr. Holton Pierpoint) was the son of the third Mayor of Wartington, and that Mr. Cros- field's father weds Mayor of the Borough in 1892—the pearls which he (the speaker) joined the Corpuration. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>'THE B3ROUGH MEMBER."</head>
        <p>'THE B3ROUGH MEMBER."</p>
        <p>Councillor H. ROBERTS, who proposed this toast in the absence of Mr. W. P. Bylands, said it had been his privilege to know the members of Parliament for War. rington personally, setae of thorn extremely intimately from the year 1858. Having referred to the multifarios character of the chitin devolving upon an M.P., coun- cillor Roberts raid that fa their present Member they had a gentleman who was most worthily (Whiling the honourable traditions established by previous holders of the office who had rendered many-sided services to their constituents, irrespective of political party. There were many traits in Mr. Smith's character which he admired—his pluck, perseverance and indefatigable energy. They got a bit of a shock at the works at Bank Quay the other day by being informed that Mr. Smith bad actually joioed the coop trade. (Laughter.) It was perfectly true that in the house of one of their directors a piece of soap was discovered not a fortnight RAO with the name of Harold Smith upon it. (Laughter.) One of his (Mr Roberts's) colleagues got a shack which it took him some considerable time to recover from. (Laughter.) He was noi,however, going to inform them [bream it would be giving away a trait !secret— f. the true explanation of the fact. (Linghter.) Councillor Roberts concluded by expressing the hope that Mr. Smith's professional career would prove what it promised to be—n most successful and brilliant one— and that he might find suitable opportuoies for his P.irliamemtary energies—some of them would prefer not in Warrington but in some other plAce—(laughter). Everyone from the most prominent Conservative to the most ultra-Radical wished him, in all sincerity and heartiness, a long, happy and prosperous life. (Applause). MR. SMITH'S CRITICISM OF PARLIAMENTIRY ji !PROCEDURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. HAROLD &amp;Mill, in responding, said, it was one of his great ambitions in life, that when the time came for him—perhaps rsluctantly, perhaps willingly—to give up the ploud position he hold to-lay, he would he able to leave behind him many friends in both political camps in Warrington who would say : "Whatever his politics, he did not neglect or forget the town which he was no proud to represent in the House of Commons." For his age, he had bad a very considerable political experience in three large counties —Cheshire, Lancashire and Yorkshire—and he could safely say, without exaggeration and with great sincerity, that he had never in any lown in England met such warm-hearted, kindly, friendly people as he met from day to day in IYarrington. (Applause). Though, at the last election, they fought one of the ' most strenuous battles Warrington had eves known, Mr. Crosfield and he grew closer friends week by week. Apart from their political opinions, they bad much in common and were perhaps better disposed to each other than any two opponents in any other constituency. (Applause.) Having congratulated Warrington on having inaugurated such a magnificent entrance from his native county into his constituency, Mr. Smith passed some strong criticism on the procedure of the house of Commons as applied to private Bills. To his mind it was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>MONSTROLTS THING</head>
        <p>A MONSTROLTS THING</p>
        <p>that a private Ball which had been prom .ted at enormous cost and had taken years in it. arduous passage in the town in which it was proposed, should be subject to no many viccisitudes and risks in the House of Commons. (Hear, hear.) Under the present conditions such Bills were voted upon by many hundreds of member. of the House of Commons who had not the remotest knowledge of what they were voting upon. Propowtla bad been made for altering that state of affairs. It ought to be altered—(Hear, hear)—and some arrangement made whereby it was possible for only those to vole furor against a private Bill who bad some rudimentary knowledge as to what the measure was about. (hear, hear.) Mr. Smith concluded by saying he should endeavour to follow the example of the distinguished townsmen who p•eceded him in the office he fICIW held and taken real and lively interest in the town which ha already love.i. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>"THE TOWN AND TRADE OF WARRINGTON AND MR. ROBERT RIERPOINTS FAREWELL. Mr. ROBERT PIERPOINT who had a most cordial reception, said he had been honoured with a request to propose the toast of "The town and trade of Warrington: If he bad intended to detain them at length, he could have given them many reminiscenoes of Warrington, because his mamory went, back further than most of those who were present. There were not m toy, for instance, who could say they had known every Mayor of Warrington since the incorporation of the Borough. He had teen the trade of Warrington increase to a remarkable extent. He remembered when his house was almost in Arpley fields and when the Town Hall was tenanted by John Wilson Patten, !afterwards Lord Winmarlelgh. (Applause ) He remembered the things which had gone against the trade of Warrington and, curiously enough, the two cases which came to his mind were cases in which Warrington was not in the least at fault. He recalled the cotton famine when the people had to be fed and he remembered the time when the works chimneys were all abut iown bec.use they could not get any coal to work with. Notwithstanding all those drawbacks, he was happy to thiok that Warrington was a very successful commercial town and he hoped to heaven that that prosperity would g on for centuries and centuries. (Applause). " I am going to be a little egotistical, if I may be allowed " continued Mr. Pierpoint." I an going to try to any what I want to gay. It has been a little panful to me to sit at this luncheon, because I apprehend, in all probability, it will be the last luncheon I shall ever attend in this hall. (" No. No.") Probably many of you, possibly all of you are aware that my wife and I are</p>
        <p>GOING TO LEAVE WAR RIV,ToN. I am sorry to mond this nste of sadness but I think it is my duty to say what I can. I won't say ' Goodbye ' —at all events only until Wel meet again. (Hear, hear.) It is • sad wrench for a man to leave the house where he was born. It is a sad thing to leave the town where he has lived so much, and it is a very sad thing to leave hundreds, nay thousands of old and young friends. But GIECCMSTAN4 E, HAVE ARI9IGN, wIIICH WERE NOT OF MY CRE &amp;TING</p>
        <p>and into whinh II do not intend to eater, *Dr have made my wife aod me think vre should be bet. placed</p>
        <p>in I.lndon than here. I ant very sorry that Ills should occur. I feel sure there are hundreds who will be sorry too. (Hear, hear.) But I have said I will not say ' good-bye.' It is not a phrase that I like. I would very much rather say ' Good-day till we meet again.' And should the physnalogical moment occur when one who has served his town in a more or less public capacity for over thirty year; and has served it in Parliament for nearly fourteen years should be wanted again for something or other—well the ways from Warrington to London are short and easy, and the waya are always open. (Load applause.) I give you the Town and trade of Warrington.' May Gad bless them. (Applause). Alderman Dowson., in responding, said the pleasure with which he did so was undoubtedly tinged with sad- ness. It was most appropriate that their old friend Mr. Robert Pierpoint should propose the toast because he was a Warringtoninn and had been most intimately connected with the town of his birth for many years. He ( Alderman Bennett) WAS sure he was speaking not only his own opinio 5, but that of every gentleman in that room, and outside it, when he said how sorry they were to hear of Mr. Pierpoint's decision. (hear, hear.) They would miss him from Warrington more than he couLI say. Ile represented the best traditions and life of the town be had served so faithfully and well. Whatever their political opinions, they had always respected Lim, and the only glimmer of hope be (the speaker) had wry that he would change" Good-bye " to "Au revoir," end that although he was going to leave them as a matter of restdence, he would often come amongst them again. (Applause.) He would always be very welcome and he (the speaker)was sure that wherever Mr. and Mrs. Pierpoint might reside their hearts would often turn to that dear old town and they would al ways be interested in its progress and prosperity. Dealing with the slbject of the toast, Alderman Bennett com- plained that thanks to the efforts of the half-penny Press, all the bad points of the Council were magnified and their Rood deeds hidden, although he admitted that occasionally some of his fellow memben played "fantastic tricks" which were calculated to make angels weep. (Laughter.) The alderman than passed in rapid review the pageant of historic and picturesque episodes connected with Warrington Bridge. The third bridge was opened by the Earl of Derby and be and his retinue, accompanied by King Henry VIE., were rho first to pass over that ancient and magnificent edit se. Ring James I. prated over Warrington Bridge, having spent the night at Bewsey, on hie way to Rock Savage. A little later one of the greatest battles of English history terminated at the Bridge and the foes of Cromwell surrendered to him and were sent marching back to Saotland, throueh Scot- land Road the following day. King Charles 11. rode over the bridge, having gained a brief victory before the crowning defeat came and all his hopes were dashed. The Earl of Derby who held the town at the time of the Civil War no nobly and no splendidly, after- wards traversed the bridge on his way to emanation. The new bridge would not he more handsome or durable than the present !structure which they were sorry w suld have to be swept away. It was fitting that a bridge built by a local engineer (Mr. Harrison) should be replaced by a bridge which had been designed by a son of the old town (Mr. Webster.) Having congratulated the Perfec.ion Soap Works Band on having added to Warrington's laurels by their great nationel triumnh, the Alderman referred to the efforts which the Town Council were making at the present time to repair the blunders of former gene-atione. Tney were trying to,</p>
        <p>_CLEANSE. THE HEART OF TFIE TOWN,</p>
        <p>to get rid of the slums and to make new beauty spots. Ile hoped that on the new bridge there would be bronze tablets recording the history of Warring.oo, so that future generations would know what great events had been enacted in the ancient town. (Applause.) Alderman TINNION also responded. He said that not only would the new bridge be a great benefit by afford- ing a better means of access to the town, hut one of its most important features was that it would improve the waterway and the navigation of the river. Warring- ton owed a great deal to its situation on the banks of such a apleniid river as the Mersey for its commercial , success, and he was pleased to say that the manufac- tuners of the town were doing all they could to improve the river and keep it in an effi3ient state, no that it would still help forward the trade of Warrington. It was admitted on all hands that the trade of Warrington to-day was in a very flourishing condition. It was a pleasant thought that during the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>'RECENT LABOUR TROUBLES</head>
        <p>'RECENT LABOUR TROUBLES</p>
        <p>..___... _....__.. there was no town in England of the size of Warrington where the manufacturers and their employees were on better terms. The conduct of the workmen and of those in authority, from the Mayor and Chief Constable downwards, IWPS very creditable. Warrington passed through all those troubles and trivia without any outward manifestation of or temper. (Applause.) The Corporrtien, ho contended, did not get as much credit as they deserved for the great amount of work they did. Warrington squill campus more than favJurably in any way with any manufacturing town in Lancashire or Yorkshire. Considering the many disadvantages that had to be faced, the sanitary conditions were a credit to the town. He hoped that by the time the new bridge was completed, the prosperity of Warrington would be such that it would be nedessary to have another bridge nearer to Livorpool for the Cheshire trade, and possibly I another bridge at Howley. (Applause.) I Before the company separated the MAYOR thanked the members of the Perfection Seep Works Band for their services in heeding the procession and warmly coogralulated them upon their brilliant richevement at I the Crystal Palace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Winwlck Rurl-Decanal Conference.</head>
        <p>Winwlck Rurl-Decanal Conference.</p>
        <p>The Winwick Ituri-Deconal Conference MIS held at the Church lionise, Bridge-street, on Wednesday after- noon, the Rev Canon Penrhyn presiding. There was a Coed attendance, most of the clergy in the deanery being present. The following resolution was proposed by the Revd H 0 HOLLING WORTH, secondei by Dr. rdocNsEv and carried :—" That no settlement of the Education ques- tion can be accepted by churchmen which Is not based upon the principles of (I) the right of the parent to secure the religious teaching he desires for his children in the schools to which he is obliged to send them and (2) the equal treatmont of all forms of faith in the schools."</p>
        <p>The REV. B. PERM' 11. proposed the following resolution : " That this meeting protests &amp;Aiwa the propovils now made to disestablish and disendow the National Church in the fond Welsh di icor, r. It undertakes to adopt all reasonable means of promoting a round knowledge of the faith, and to , co-operate strenuously with Welshmen in scarring the I maintenance of their opportunity of orodering to the people of tie land the distinctive service which is the duty and the privilege of the National Church." The rev. sentleman raid that disestablishmelt meant the , repudiation of the national recognition of the Christian religiou, and was a retrograde step in the national life. It intent the discherabership of the Chorch ; the cutting MY of four dioasses from the Church, es the Church of Eugland and Wale] were one. The cry of re!igious equality was a misnomer, and in these days should mislead no one. There was eunuch thins in this land -s religious inequality, for all men were free to worship 101 I hey liked. Ile contendel that if it were good for Welsh Nonconformista to be united with English Nonconformists sh it was equally fool for Vte'sh Churchmen to le united with English Churchmen. Mr A J JOLLEY seconded the resolution, which wail carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LATE MR. PETER EDGAR.</head>
        <p>LATE MR. PETER EDGAR.</p>
        <p>Memorial Unveiled.</p>
        <p>The goodwill and kindly feeling which exists one to another, to a marked degree,in the lives of the member, of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds was again manifest on Saturday afternoon, when e large attend- ance of the members of the district assembled at the Ca celery to witness the unveiling of a memorial which they have erected tothe memory of their departed district secretary, Mr Peter Edgar. The memorial which takes the form of a scroll arcuated in white marble is • splendid piece of workmanahip,2ft 9i05 long and 2ft Gins wide. A mono befitting inscription to the ardaous work of the deceased daring his 19 suns secretaryship of the district could not have been found than the one which it bears.—" Erected to the memory of Bro Peter Edgar, by the members of the Warrington District of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds, A.U., as a token of respect and esteem in which he was held doming the 19 years he war secretary of the district. The unveil- ing ceremou was performed by a Put Chief Shepherd (Bro AF G Potter), while among those present we rvltced : Chief Shepherd Bro Thomas Champion,Deputy Cale( Shepherd Joseph Pomfrey, Past Chief Shepuerd William Taylor, District Trusuer P.P.C.B. James Parr, Trustee P.P.O S. William Eckersley, P.P.C.S. Thomas Miller., P.P.C.S. Thomas Duckworth, P.P.O S. Jaum• Gillooly, P.P.C.S. Joseph Mather, L Weir, (Secretary of the !durum ial Committee). Mr. POTTER Sail that their meetiug there that after- noon was to tumour the memory of one who was • great and trusted friend of that Order—one who worked assiduously for its welfare. During the nine- teen years of his secretaryship he strived to do hie duty, in which he was a great aucuss, and died with the respect of every member, lie hoped that he (V r Potter) would oats die with the earn, respect. H asked Mr. P. L. E Isar, to accept that menorml fro r. the Shepherds as a token of their respect for la deceased tether. (Applause). Mr. P. L EDGAR 03 behalf of the family said that it Wa3 with mingled feelings that be received that memorial to their father. They deeply appreciated the goodwill that hind prompted the Shepherds to ereot that memorial and it was a source of great satisfaction gni comfort for the family to know that their p trent was held In inch high esteem by them all. He (Mr Edgar) could only say that they woold always think well of it, kneeing the depth of feeling which had prompted Its erection. P.P.C.S. TROIKAS MILLER said he could not let the opportunity pane without referring to the deceased who was one of the most highly prized members and one of the hardest workers in the district. The emotion of that memorial tended to show the high respect in which the decemen was held. PC. S. WILLIAM TAYLOR also referred to the late Mr. Edger awl said that his hard work had greatly helped forward the cause of the S osphenis. It was also befitting that Bro. Potter ehoold have been incited to perform that ceremony an he and the deceased were Intimate friends.</p>
        <p>A vote of thank] mu paoe3d to Brather PAter for ueveiling the memorial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL COUNCIL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Tha Padgate Sewerage Scheme.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington and District Rural Council was held on We Inesday a:el-noon at Bermes, Chamhers. Mr George Mather preside], mid the-e were present :—The Rev A M Mitchell, Meson P Longton BsII, A Brooks, W FUfOO5l, Dr Murray, W Houghto%, J C Sutton (clerk), B S Newcombe (surveyor), H H Barrage (assistant clerk), F Kerfoot (inspector), sod Dr &amp;piston (nedioal officer of health). A letter was read from Mr Faulkner, farmer, of Penketb, who claimed £2O for damage to his cattle which he alleged wan due to sewage overflowing on to his field.</p>
        <p>The Council aft r considerable discussion repudiated the claim, on the ground that the damage was not doe in the renage.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPIITON asked if scything hod bOOO dono with Psdgate drainage, which had teut dragging on year after year. It was In a frightful condition. The CHAIRMAN reastukel that the Surveyor had bee, inatruotel to prepwe plans to be submitte I at the test meeting.</p>
