      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>VIEW OF TRIPOLI.</head>
        <p>A VIEW OF TRIPOLI.</p>
        <p>Tripoli, the bone of contention between Italy and Turkey, was formerly a native dynasty, but passed under direct Turkish rule in 1835, and even then it took the Turks many years to obtain possession of the whole country. It was with the advent of the Young Turks' power that the relations between the Italian and Turkish authorities became strained. The European population numbers from 5,000 to 6,000, chiefly Italian and Maltese, but all the important officials of Tripoli are Turkish. Our picture shows the Buropean quarter of the town of Tripoli.</p>
        <p>THE SULTAN OF TURKEY.</p>
        <p>Mohammed V., the Sultan of Turkey, was proclaimed Sultan in _April, 1909, when Abdul Humid was dethroned by the Young Turks. He is a younger brother of the late Sultan, and is 67 years of age.</p>
