      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>KING OF ITALY.</head>
        <p>THE KING OF ITALY.</p>
        <p>Victor Emmanuel 111., King of Italy, is 42 years of age, and ascended to the throne in 1900 on the assassination of his father. He married Princess Helena of Montenegro, and has one son.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>HAY CROP AND THE DROUGHT.</head>
        <p>THE HAY CROP AND THE DROUGHT.</p>
        <p>Mr Ernest Parke ban issued his tenth annual report on the manurial trials carried out on gross land on his farm at K melon under the advice of Dr Dyer. Although the season's hay crop has been here, as elsewhere, a small one, well chosen fertilisers have yielded remunerative results. Portions of the fields representing the natural condition of the pasture (now about 20 years ;old), which receive no manuring except that incidental to autumn grazing, gave this your, in two separate fields, only 7 met. and 8 cwt, of bay per sore respectively. 1 hese yields were more than doubled by phosphatic manure, namely, 3 cwt, of caper-phosphate per acre in one field and 5 cwt. of basic slag in the other. Where l cwt. of nitrate of eoda per acre was applied in addition to the phosphates, the crop in both fields was about trebled. being N cwt., in one sod 24i cwt. in the other. The average results for the last 10 years show in both fields an increase of nearly l ton of hay per acre over rud above the natural unaided yield of the lend, through the use of phosphates and nitrogen together, es indicated, at a cost of somewhere about 255. to 303. an acre; so that each shillingsworth of the combined fertilisers adds more than a hundred- weight of hay to the average crop. Dr. Dyer reports that, while the uTmenured land remains full of weeds and wild gra.ses of an undesir- able kind, there is a rich turf on all the menaced plots. Clovers abound most on the plots which receive only " mineral " manures without nitrogen, while on the plots getting only nitrate the herbage is nudely gramineoue. The plots receiving both phosphates and nitrate, which yield f.O per ceut, more increase than when either of the fertilieets is used singly, ere rich in clovers as well as In true grasses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>L PILGRIMAGES</head>
        <p>L PILGRIMAGES</p>
        <p>THE CITY OF THE GIANTS</p>
        <p>A GREAT PREHISTORIC FORTRESS VISITED.</p>
        <p>It is inspiring to stand upon one of the hills that surround Pwllheli and trace the at mountains that stretch across Wales from east to west like rolling uneven billows. Petrified billows it is true, but in the chang- ing light, with mist and sun alternating, making a picture of kaleidescopic charm. 3nowdon, generally mist covered,and holding the clouds back as they travel past, makes a noble centre piece and last in the chain rising sheer above the Irish Sea is the twin peaked mountain called in Welsh Yr Eifi, in English The Rivals. There is something in Yr Eifi that holds us in perpetual challenge. It places its rock- ribbed sides to the Irish Sea inviting the storms to do their worst, and its fine head stands sharp and thin against the blue— a picture of aspiration and promise. Yet Yr Eifi, despite itslB49 feet is not a for- bidding mountain to the climber. The road from Nevin lifts gently to the upland and by the time we have reached the village that nestles at the foot, there is but a thousand fek t of actual mountaineering.</p>
        <p>A motor bus journey from Pwllheli to Nevin, with an intolerable smell of petrol, and an ascending journey on foot, have brought us to the foot of the ragged giant. fHE SIGNAL MOUNTAIN OF NORTH WALES.</p>
        <p>The English name "The Rivals" appears to be built upon a mistake or, it may be an imitation of the pronunciation of Yr Eifi. The true meaning of its name is Signa Fire' and</p>
        <p>exhibit from below a grand and extensive front The space on the top is an irregular area, part is steep, part flat. The whole is almost filled with cells. To be seen to advantage, the station should be taken from the summit, about which the cells arelvery distinct, and dispersed with much art. About the middle is a square place fenced with stones, a sort of pmetorium surrounded with two rows of cells. The cells are mostly perfect, of various forms, round, oval, oblong, square. Some of the round were fifteen feet in diameter, of the oblory, thirty feet in length, with long entrances regularly faced with stones. All of ti ern, when inhabited, were well protected from the weather by roofs of thatch or sod. Tbe upper wall was in many places fifteen feet high on the outside, and often sixteen broad. It consisted of two parallel and contiguous p rte one higher than the other, serving as a parapet to the lower, which seemed to have had its walk like that on the walls of Chester. There was in one place a cell the thickness of the wall, or perhaps a sally-port in part stopped by the fallitz of stones." This pretorium (if such it were) lies at our feet as we stand upon the citadel. Did the chieftain sit here like an oriental judge to dispense rough and ready justice, and was it here they feasted the travelling guest and ally? The wall remains in ruined silence, and all else is silence too.</p>
        <p>Ireland, doubtless, the refuge of the distressed.</p>
        <p>On the rocky slopes the wild goats browse in places where no creature but themselves dare venture. Hunters come by boats with hounds to catch them, but if the goats can but reach the precipices the boats go empty back again. The goats, too, are in the guide book, but we doubt that they are on the precipice now. What self respecting wild goat would live in the smell of gunpowder and miners' curses. There are other curiosities about the mountains. They say that birds aro never seen there. If the birds find as little to eat as we do there is no mystery about that. But we are getting critical and quarrelling even with the faithful guide book. Can it be that we are getting hungry ? Not even a sandwich has cheered the solitude ; just the fragments of a bar of chocolate divided among three. And those seven miles that separate us from Nevin and the teapot I However did the old Britons endure this thin cold air. A. M.</p>
        <p>i I GOODALLS."</p>
        <p>46 ALLGOOD " ART CARPET.</p>
        <p>BRITISH-M ADE. SEAMLESS. REVERSIBLE. DURABLE.</p>
        <p>GUARANTEED TO BE TUE Besr VALUE EVER OFFERED IN CARPETS.</p>
        <p>" GOODALLS " POSSESS THE SOLE RIGHT OF SELLING THESE CARPETS IN GREAT BRITAIN 21 yards x 2.i yards 9d. „ z 3 3 a z 3 „ .. 3 „ x 4 „ 30,04. OTHER SIZES IN PROPORTION.</p>
        <p>Samplee Loaned on Application. Please Write. Prompt Attention to Orders.</p>
        <p>POSTAL ORDER DEPARTMENT. TERMS CASH OR SATISFACTORY REFERENCE WITH ORDER 6 GOODALLS," KING-STREET, MANCHESTER. Entabl idled 1827.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>FRUITLESS SPECULATIONS.</head>
        <p>FRUITLESS SPECULATIONS.</p>
        <p>Who were the builders of the City of the Giants ;of what race and age? There is not one historic syllable to show. Once, no doubt, it was a place of renown, known far and wide as a city of impregnable bulwarks, the Gibraltar of some prehistoric age. Before the earliest British record was made these walls were old and deserted, and even tradi- tion did not supply a clue. The ruins are old enough to have forgotten their age, and neither spearhead nor fashioned tool has been found to help our speculations. If they had buried tools and ornaments with their dead these might have come to light (for men are known by their tools all down the ages), but no cemetery has been found and we are left to conjecture that they practised cremation.</p>
        <p>• . .; . 44. - f:I4.A .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>SITE OF THE SO-CALLED CITY OF THE GIANTS.</head>
        <p>SITE OF THE SO-CALLED CITY OF THE GIANTS.</p>
        <p>The peak to the left is known as Yr Eifi or The Rivals and rises 1,849 feet sheer above the Irish Sea. Upon the crest of the twin mountain is the City of the Giants. It covers the summit and the citadel stands on the topmost portion to the right.</p>
        <p>iinee the mountain is visible from every corner of the Peninsula it is most probable that the name dates from the very early times when fire signals were blazed from the summit.</p>
        <p>..... A cluster of three or four peaks make up the mountain and crowning the Eastern one are the wonderful ruins called Tre'r Ceiri, a name commonly taken to mean "the City of the Cewri" or giants, though another translation would make it "the City of Fortresses." Much as the loftiest peak allures us the prespect of seeing the giant's city attracts us more and we begin the ascent. There is a suggestion of a road all the way and after a mile or so of not very hard travelling the peak comes into view. Broken by time one would never suspect that this stony hill crest sheltered an ancient fortress and many 3. traveller must have passed beneath quite oblivious that a compact and wonderful little town stood above him.</p>
        <p>And the inhabitants! Did they forsake the fortress when the need for it was past, or were they driven westwards to Ireland like the Celtic tribes? Was this the last stronghold of a native tribe who made here a death struggle against the Roman soldier?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>CITY GATE.</head>
        <p>THE CITY GATE.</p>
        <p>We have clambered over scores of tons of rough stones (unspeakably ugly and uncomfortable) before we see anything to suggest human workmanship. And then we find we are between rugged walls and realize that we are entering through the main portal of the city of giants. Yes, the walls are 15 feet thick as the guide book says, but so uncouth in their decay, as to suggest nothing better than the wreckage of an earthquake. The ragged head of an ancient Briton peeping over might give life to the place, but every- thing is so shapeless and forlorn and we pre- pare to be thoroughly disappointed. Once inside the city gates things look different, for we now can see the length and proportions of the fort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>TOWN.</head>
        <p>THE TOWN.</p>
        <p>_ Picture the gently sloping summit of a mountain, say a quaiter of a mile in length and that quarter mile surrounded by the ruins of three distinct protecting walls. Completely covering the mountain top are dotted the walls of ancient huts varying from six to 15 feet in diameter mostly circular, but some square or oblong in shape. There is no ancient" High-street" to lead us to the summit, or "City Cross" to mark the centre, and we must perforce thread our way between wrecked and ruined huts until we scale the citadel. This occupies the crest of the mountain and seated upon the ruins of the Keep we grasp the fine plan upon which the little town is built.</p>
        <p>Those who have given special study to the fortress regard it as a masterpiece of skill and are ready to acclaim the builder a famous military engineer. We have entered at the North-West End up the sloping side of the mountain. At this, the easiest approach, three heavy walls have been built with an immensely strong gate- way and sally port. On the inner side along this side is a lowered portion along which a sentry could walk and keep watch. To appreciate the skill of the design we need to remember that in primitive warfare battles were won by sudden rushes and the surprise attack was most likely to bring suc- cess. Three such walls would effectually test the agility of an invading army and those who did man-tge to rush the narrow gateway could be easily destroyed in detail. Even in their present ruinous condition a handful of men could hold the walls against a regiment. The entrances, of which there are said to be three, are all " curtained " against a frontal attack, amid could only be ' entered by small nurabers. It is almost a pity that the ruins are open to the irreverent foot of the chance visitor, (though we are conscious that prohibition would have excluded casual pilgrims like ourselves), for the vandalism of the last generation has wrought more ruin than the storms of the last millenium. THOMAS PENNANT AT GIANT'S CITY.</p>
        <p>Earlier writers get a much clearer idea of things, as for instance, that fine traveller Pennant, who eulogised Trer Ceiri as the finest ancient post he ever beheld. This is , his description written in the 18th century: "It was defended by three walls—the loaeat is very imperfect, the next tolerably entire, and has in it the grand entrance. This wall in one fart points upwards towards the third wall, shirt runs round the edges o'; tbe top of the bill ; the second wall unites wit-i the first, which runs into a point, reverts, and joins the highest, in a place where the hill becomes ilaccessible. The facings on the two upper walls are very entire, especially that the uppermost.. Thee* ate lofty, end</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>BOYD DAWKINS AND THE RUINS.</head>
        <p>BOYD DAWKINS AND THE RUINS.</p>
        <p>The most confident opinion given by any expert in that of Professor Boyd Dawkins who declares the ruins to belong to the Pre-historic Iron age. From the beginning of that remote age, says the veteran archeologist, up to the Roman invasion a large group of such mountain villages was in constant occupation, but the effect of the coming of the Romans was to make the camps unnecessary and the inhabitants descended to the valleys. This may be so, but with so scanty evid- ence he is a bold antiquarian who dare be so dogmatic. Certainly there are traces of many such ruined villages in the North of Wales.</p>
        <p>A somewhat similar ruin crowns the summit of Penmaenmawr. Here too there are three walls on the landward side and within the fortification traces of a full hundred huts. But the modern hunger for granite is carrying away Braich-y-dinas, and the remains of the prehistoric town will pave the streets of Lancashire towns— perhaps our own. Conway Mountain shows traces of an ancient fort and those who have climbed the rough summits of Madryn or Bodvean have perhaps traced in the stony wilderness encircling walls and circular huts. Not any of the houses here of Tre'r Ceiri retain their roofing and we can only specu- late concerning the substance which kept out the weather.</p>
        <p>Boyd Dawkins comes to our help to say that all the older type of monastic buildings were, in the prehistoric iron age, formed in the same way, with circular stone walls roofed with stones in circles of gradually reduced diameter,thus lapping over the stone till the roof was complete. Such a hut may be seen intact, says the Professor, at Penzance.</p>
        <p>Traces of fire places may be seen and we speculate about the rough-haired heroes who sat here between two and three thousand years ago. That vast period has elapsed since the fires burned and the natives huddled here for warmth. It is a thought of uncanny flavour, and we try in vain to sit in comfort among the stones,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>AN UNCANNY RUIN.</head>
        <p>AN UNCANNY RUIN.</p>
        <p>Indeed the whole place is uncanny in its mystery. Most old places give some clue to their builders, but this grim old town repells us at every enquiry. Assuming we are never to know the builders, we cannot help asking how they are fed. These ugly mountain slopes would never yield crops, though the marshy land below might yield water. Was there a well to draw from when the besieger was at hand, and what reserve of food could be held up here? We may as well ask the Sphinx ; and we suppose that every traveller who has climbed hither will have but added to the list of questions which are unanswered and unanswerable. Of building materials there would be no scarcity for the mountains are covered with big rugged stones. Except where the door- ways demand it the stones are not cut to</p>
        <p>_.., shape and no mortar appears to have been used. Possibly a plastering of mud kept out the wind, and that walls constructed of such poor materials should last so long says some- thing for the skill of the builders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WEIRD CORNER Or THE WORLD.</head>
        <p>A WEIRD CORNER Or THE WORLD.</p>
        <p>From the ramparts we have a view scarcely rivalled in Britain. Anglesea lies at our feet like a map and the Snowdonian range spreads in billowy grandeur. Cader Idris and Plynlimmon stand out impressively against the sky and to the far south the southern shores of the Cardigan Bay are dimly seen. Close by, on the North side of the headland, Carreg Liam rises from tho sea—a sheer precipice. Here the raven finds 1 a Lome. The bird once found in every corner of the land has fled before an advancing civilization and now finds a last refuge on the ledges of the rock. But as we climb to the citadel there is a roar as of cannon and a rattling like that of artillery. They are blasting Carreg Llam to make pavements for the cities. It is only the guide book that says the last raven is there. Possibly by this time he has fled—to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M.P.</p>
        <p>THE MENDING OF THE INSURANCE</p>
        <p>BILL.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons will be re-assembling in a fortnight, and the con- sideration of the Insurance Bill will be resumed. In view of this it may be inter- esting to give a summary of the changes which have been made in the Bill so far as it has gone in Committee. There are 87 clauses in the Bill besides a number of very important schedules. At the adjournment of Parliament seventeen clauses had been passed, but a number of very important points which had arisen on these clauses had not been finally settled, but had been post- poned to a later stage of the Bill. The changes which have been made are fairly numerous, but none of them alters at all the general structure of the measure. They are all matters of small detail, and it is important to bear in mind that nothing has been done which adds to the total benefits under the Bill. Additions have been made here and there to the benefits, but as the total sum of money remains the same it all means that what has been given with one hand has been taken away with the other. There is a surplus in the Bill of about 10 per cent., or one million and threequarters a year. If the Bill had remained unamended in any way this surplus would have been available for additional benefits, but the amendments which have been passed which increase the cost will simply have the effect of reducing the surplus available for additional benefits. It is important to remember this in consider- ing the list of amendments passed which Lam going to give. The Bill as originally drafted included in medical treatment the provision of proper and sufficient medicines, but an amendment has been accepted adding," such medical and surgical appliances as may be prescribed by the Insurance Commissioners." If these</p>
        <p>Insurance Commissioners give a liberal inter- pretation to their powers this should be a useful alteration, as it will enable sick persons to get appliances free which would be very expensive to purchase. FRIENDLY SOCIETY GRIEVANCE.</p>
        <p>One of the chief grievances the Friendly Societies have against this Bill is that it does not conform to the Friendly Society practice of giving sick pay from the first day of illness. Very great efforts were made in Committee to get an amendment accepted which would have adapted this practice in the Bill. But the Chancellor of the Exchequer, who has the fear of themalingerer always before his eyes, would not yield. However, he accepted an amendment which was moved from the Labour benches substituting for" fourth day after the notice has been received" the words "fourth day after being rendered unfit." This means that no sick pay will be given for the first three days of the illness. The friendly societies will have to find this out of the voluntary part of their income. Another useful amendment was carried.</p>
        <p>The original clause provided that no sickness or disablement benefit should be paid to insured persons under 16 years of age, though they might have been paying full premiums for nearly two years. This was taken out of the Bill, and these juveniles will now qualify for benefits in the same way as older persons. Another injustice to young people was modified. It was provided that persons between the ages of 16 and 21 should, though they had paid the full contributions, receive a reduced benefit. This has been amended to the extent that such minor shall have the full benefits if he has any member of his family dependent upon his earnings. Though not fully satisfactory, this amenument does lessen the hardship somewhat. THE MATERNITY BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>The Maternity benefit has been improved. It should be borne in mind that the wife of</p>
        <p>an insured man, though she is not herself an insured person, is entitled to the maternity grant of 30s. But many married women will be insured persons. As the Bill stood when introduced these insured married women would get no more than the uninsured married women. It was felt that the insured women at least were entitled to something better, and it has now been passed that they will be entitled to sick benefit from the women's fund as well as the maternity benefit from the husband's fund. Unmarried women are only entitled to the maternity benefit.</p>
        <p>A good deal of discussion took place on a clause which proposed to allow the rates of sickness and disablement benefits to be reduced in any case where the Insurance Commissioners consented to such a course, and which required that the benefits should be reduced where the sick or disablement benefit exceeded two-thirds of the person's usual wages. That really meant that no person whose wages were less than 15s. a week would get the full sick pay for which he had insured. It was provided that equiva- lent benefit should be given, but it was not specified what particular form these equiva- lent benefits should take. This clause again was due to the fear of malingering. However, it was slightly amended; and in the new form the word " may " has been substituted for " shall " in regard to the low paid persons, and the right to interfere with the benefits of the better paid has been taken out altogether.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>OLDER MEN.</head>
        <p>THE OLDER MEN.</p>
        <p>There was one very extraordinary pro- posal in the original Bill. How it got there nobody ever ventured to explain. It was that a man who joined the scheme when say forty-one years of age and paid regularly for nearly ten years without ever coming upon the fund should have his benefit reduced when he did make a claim, but if he rrade • claim in 27 weeks he was to have the full benefit. The intention appears to have been to give smaller benefits to men who were over 50. This has been amended, so as not to put the good member in a worse position than the bad one; but still as it stands men who are over 50 en joining the scheme are to get reduced benefits until they have paid not less than 500 weekly payments. THE QUESTION OF ARREARS.</p>
        <p>The question of the payment of arrears has given rise to a good deal of discus- sion. The Bill excludes the payment of con- tributions when a person is in receipt of sick pay. When a person is out of work both his own and his employer's arrears are running up against him. He is entitled to be thirteen weeks in arrears for each year he has been a member without losing benefit, though he suffers a reduction of the amount if he be more than one month in arrears. If he is more than 13 weeks in arrears, but less than twenty six weeks on the average, he is still entitled to maternity, medical and sana- torium benefits. This latter provision was inserted as an amendment to the Bill.</p>
        <p>As the Bill originally stood the workman had not only to pay up his own and the employer's arrears, but he had to pay three per cent, interest upon them. That point has been struck out of the Bill, and an addition has been inserted which empowers the approved society of which the workman is a member to discharge the employer's portion of the arrears, but not the workman's. This concessiomis far more unreal than substantial. If the society does discharge the arrears it simply means that it is depleting its reserves, and there will be so much less money for the many "additional benefits" which are held out in the Bill. The only reasonable course to have taken was for the State to shoulder the responsibility for arrears which had accumulated in times of unemployment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MEDICAL AMENDMENTS.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL AMENDMENTS.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the most important amendments which have been made so far are those which relate to the relation of the doctors to the scheme. The control of doctors has been taken away from the approved societies and given to the Health Committees. This has greatly upset the friendly societies, but it is the only practical thing to do. Now that the insured person has been given the choice of doctors it would not have been possible to have left the doctors under the direction of the societies. An amendment of very great importance, and one which is a serious thing for many workmen who are earning fairly good wages, has been passed dealing with the income limit of insured persons. It will be remembered that the doctors pressed for an income limit of £2 a week, that is to say that workmen who earned more than £2 a week should not be entitled to free doctoring under the scheme. The Chancellor of the Exchequer would not listen to that, but he accepted an amend- ment which puts the onus of settling this difficulty with the doctors upon the local Health Committee. Under the Bill, as amended, the Health Committee in a district can fix an income limit—a pound a week if they think fit—and workmen above this limit will have to pay for their own doctor- ing at ordinary rates, although they have paid into the scheme for free medical treat- ment. Probably the Chancellor thinks the Health Committees will never attempt to put 1 this into operation. But if they don't what about the doctors? These are the main alterations which have been made in the Bill up to the present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <p>_ NEVER WHAT THEY DO. Holloway's Pills cleanse the system of BE all impurities, restoring energy and vig- our. They are a reliable remedy for In- digestion, Biliousness' Feverish Attacks, Headache, Dizziness and all complaints WITHOUT arising from a constipated condition. When feeling "out of sorts" take a few doses of Holloway's Pills, they will HOLLOWAY'S fortify you against disease and may avert a serious illness. —.................. pILLS Holloway's Ointment is especially use- ful, where there are large families, for Burns, Cuts, Scalds, Bruises, Chapped AND hands, Chilblains, &amp;c. It quickly cures Bad Legs, Wounds, Piles, Sores, Eczema and all skin affections. For Rheu- OINTMENT matism or stiffness in the joints or limbs it has no equal and affords speedy relief in Throat, Chest and Lung complaints.</p>
