      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>I ALT, TiVatTR RFRFAVF.D].</p>
        <p>"It cannot be done,* he replied; "there %re difficulties."</p>
        <p>He drew himself up and sat silent with a suspicious expression round his mouth. Mary hastily changed the conversation. After a time she said gently : "Quite independent of your business, Mr. IJuisver - "</p>
        <p>Yon know my name. and you pronounce it in a pretty manner of your own," remarked Julaver. . am considered to have • good accent," said Mary. "Pray continue your speech," said the Persian. _</p>
        <p>"I was going to remark," said Mary, in her pretty, innocent way, "that quite inde- pendent of your business—you must find it very dull here. Why don't you do something to amuse yourself--something pour passer le (em /a—you comprehend!" Nadir Julaver bowed. • •</p>
        <p>"I comprehend," he said. "May I ask how a pretty, fair, young English lady passes the Lime?—for you also are waiting for the good boat Hercules to bear you to Batum." "Oh—I?" said Mary. "I amuse myself in various ways. I go about the town; I see the sights. Then I have the privilege of an ac- quaintance here, M. Lavo, a most charming Frenchman, an old friend of my husband's. "Ah--you are married, madame?" "Yes; I hope to meet my dear husband at Batum." .</p>
        <p>Julaser made no response to this. You amuse yourself by tiring yourself," be then remarked. "The views of the Persian with regard to pleasure are different. I amuse myself—not by wearying my limbs, but by resting them. I enter when and where I choose through the portals of Elysium, and find myself on enchanted ground. That is my ainteiement." .</p>
        <p>"How how fascinating !" said Mary. What do you mean?" "I. am a devotee to the opium pipe. madame. If you never smoked it, you know as little what true bliss means as does the unborn babe." . .</p>
        <p>ig "Do tell me abottt it," said Mary, her eyes glistening. "Do, for instance, ladies eves smoke?" "It has occasionally been done," replied Nadir Julaver. "Of course, at the beginning the portion of opium must be very small, for the unaccustomed brain can only stand a minute amount. I would myself prepare a pipe for madame, if she wished to try the effect." 1 "I wish you would," said Mary, "and per- haps my friend, M. Lavo, would also like to smoke the opium." "He is a Frenchman; he knows about the habit already," said Julaver, with some con.</p>
        <p>tempt. Mary smiled. I will introduce him to you," she said. "Ali! and here he is!" At that moment Seleuens entered the coffee room. He came straight up to Mary, bowed to her, and also bowed to Julaver. Julaver looked up at him, noticed his cropped head, his French dress, observed a certain drawl in his accent, and was con- vinced on the spot that the man was a Per- sian. The thought of his servant, Abbas, and the warnings the man had given him with re- gard to Mary and Seleucus now returned to him. But because he suspected, he was all the more gracious and affable. He entered into a long talk with Seleucus, speaking in excellent French, a language he knew tho- roughly, and finally he invited Mary and Seleucus to share the opium pipe with him in his own sitting-room that afternoon. Having done this, and Mary and Seleucus having accepted the invitation, Nadir Jula- ver left the room. Seleucus gave a keen glance at his companion. He was a clever man, and highly educated for a servant, but he !fad neither the intellect of Julaver nor of</p>
        <p>..- the great Mina himself. "Madame," he said, 'will you come with me for a walk, I have something to say." Mary, who wore her outdoor things, imme- diately complied. "I will now, madame," said Seleuens, "prepare for you something to put into the Nadir's opium pipe. It will complete his re- pose, madame, and we will secure from his person the great diamond, the 'Fountain of Beauty,'" . . _</p>
        <p>" From his person?" said Mary, turning</p>
        <p>very while.</p>
        <p>"Yes; he wears it just above the hip— in the hollow just above the hip—in a woollen pad. I know it-1 have marked the spot.</p>
        <p>-. Madame, we are safe; our task is nearly over. Abbas and Kim hay.sailed to a dis-</p>
        <p>taut port, and by no possibilty can be back at Marseilles under a week. You and I win our great reward, and I shall to-day send a telegram to the Mirza Kara Yusuf, which will, I fancy, please him much. Madame, if I mistake not, it is the great Mires who will sail in the good ship Hercules to Batum, and it is Natiir Julaver who will remain behind." Mary looked full at Seleucite, an expres- sion of distress and alarm on her face. "I have said," she began, and her lips trembled. He bent towards her. "I am listening, adorable madame," he said. . .</p>
        <p>"I have said that I will go far, very far, to fulfil the object of this expedition. But I will not take," her voice dropped, "life. I draw the line at that—you understand." "Perfectly, madame. And," continued Seleucus, "I respect your scruples as my great master, the Mires Ears Yustat, has already respected them. He wrote to me about you, madame, and gave me full direc- tions with regard to the part you were ex- pected to play." "If you understand, it is all right,” said Mary, 'and I will do my beet." "It is, as I have assured you," said Seleu- cite. "a case of life or death for both of us. Failure spells Death. Success spells Life. Perhaps, madame, it would be safer for us to I part company here." Mary immediately look the cue. Site en- tered a large shop, where she made a few purchases, while the Persian, with bent head, went in the direction of the General _ . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Post-office. Ile entered and sent • telegram to his master. The telegram ran as hallows: "All is well. Expect further news to- morrow at latest."</p>
        <p>This was despatched to the Mimi at the Carlton Hotel, and Seleucus, having per- formed his duties, went into a certain chemist's shop, where he bought a few appa- rently innocent preparations. These were given to him without comment, for he pro- duced a doctor's prescription for the amount. He then went down to the quays, and amused himself watching the different vessels as they entered the port. He little guessed - what was happening during his absence, and that he had left a far greater mind than either his own or that of Mary Deepe behind him at the Hotel des Ambassadeurs. For Nadir Julaver, having retired to his own room, sat for a time in desp thought. It was his habit when he was thinking to sit motionless, with his eyes fixed on the ground. Be was puzzling out a pro- blem, and that problem had to do with the man who was introduced to him as "M. Lavo." After a time he had evidently come to the right solution, for he clapped his hands on his thighs with a sudden move- ment. He then raised his hand cautiously and pressed it against the hollow .just above his hip. A smile of satisfaction slowly cr‘no edghtisup m his Eastern face. i, deep chair, crowd the room, and went out. He walked down the narrow, dirty street, found himself in a broad street, and entered the General Post- offices— There he, too, despatched a telegram. "To the Mirza Kara l'usuf, from Firleuctm otherwise M. Lavo.—The ' Fountain of Beauty' in my possession. Come without a moment's delay to the Hotel Lies Ambassa deurs, Rue du Petit Pont, Marseilles."</p>
        <p>_ When Mary Deepe an hour later returned to•dejeuner at the hotel she found a little note, written in exeellent English, waiting for her by her plate. She opened it, and read an follows:</p>
        <p>alit Julmer requests madame, sslmer</p>
        <p>name he has not the pleasure of knowing, and also M. Lave, to sup with him to-morrow evening at nine of the clock, when he will produce for their benefit the pipe of peace. Ile regrets having to postpone to-night's entertainment, owing to • sudden indisposi- tion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>FOUNTAIN OF BEAUTY</head>
        <p>THE FOUNTAIN OF BEAUTY</p>
        <p>L. T. MEADE,</p>
        <p>Author oi "The Raelhon of Ll_ Carrington," "A</p>
        <p>Sweet Girl Graduate:. &amp;e.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIII,</p>
        <p>"FAILURE SPELLS DEATH. SUCCESS SPELLS LIFE."</p>
        <p>Nadir Julaver was induced without the slightest difficulty to put up at the Ilbtel des Ambassadeurs.'elicits was right when he said that a Persian would not notice the dirt. The Persian wished for lofty and large rooms. lie required food to his liking, coffee made after the Turkish manner, fruit, and an abundance of opium. Ile was much chafed and weary at the delay which sud- denly prevented his leaving Marseilles, but. being a true Oriental, he kept his feelings tc., himself.</p>
        <p>He made his acquaintance with Mrs. Deepe just in the fashion which Se!emus had planned, and when he stepped into the omni- bus which was to bear him to the Hotel de, Ambassadeurs, she followed him into the same vehicle. His servant, Abbas, came after with Julaver's luggage; a porter from one of the quays brought it to the hotel iv question. They passed several gorgeous and line-looking holds on the way, but Abbas was silent as the grave, and no more com- mented than did Julaver himself on the bad street and gloomy outlook where the Hotel des Ambassadeurs was situated. Abbas waited on his master assiduously. quietly, noiselessly. M. Lavo visited Mary Deepe once or twice. Julaver took not the slightest notice of this, although he saw the good-looking, smart young Englishwoman walking with the Frenchman. But Abbas made observations. Abbas was exceedingly clever, and he began to watch for certain characteristics in the features of AL Lace which might tell him that the man was not a genuine Frenchman. Seleuens, however, was also watching Abbas, and so was Ktim, who kept quite in the background. Julaver arrived at the hotel on Wednesday night. Thursday passed without anything special occurring. Julaver did not go out: his spacious rooms satisfied him. Whateter his thoughts, they were turned inwards. He was near the great triumph of his life. Be was picturing the moment when he would open the eyes of the Shah, and reveal to him the perfidy of his favourite servant. With the downfall of the Mirza Kara all the hopes, the dreams, the desires at Nadir Julaver would be realised. He had offered thirty thousand pounds for the pos- semion of the great diamond, but through his own wit and daring, and the cleverness of his spy, Abbas, he had secured it for next to nothing. He reflected with pleasure on his cunning in substituting the false gems, made from an old recipe known in Juin ver's family, for the real. -The false stones were made with such daring and cleverness that only the eye of an expert could detect the difference. Occasionally, as Nadir Julaver paced tip and down his great sitting-room, he laughed short laughs of contentment to himself. Everything was going well. The annoying part, however, was the delay. It was in- tolerable to have to stay idling at Marseilles for the greater Part of a week. Ile dared not charter a vessel for himself or he would have done so, for this would have imme- diately have caused remark; and, besides, he could not have got beyond Datum without further expense and further gossip. In short, the only thing before him at the pre- sent moment was to stay quiet. Thursday passed, therefore, in pence. That evening Julaver stretched himself on one of the uncomfortable sofas, desired his servant, Abbas, to prepare his opium pipe and then . •</p>
        <p>• . to leave him. Inlayer felt safe enough here. Surely no one would follow him to Marseilles, and, last of all, to this hotel in a back street. Abbas grumbled a little at the accommodation. There were grander hotels with finer views, more light, better food. Inlayer told the man to hold his tongue. Abbas stood still. looking down at is 4naster. Ile was no more faithful than any other person would have been in his position, but as lie knew that faithfulness meant life, and faithless- ness death, he was as canny as he could be, and as anxious for Julaver's personal safety. The lady downstairs," he said, the Eng- lish lady!" "What of her?" said Inlayer. "I like her not," said Abbas. Inlayer made no answer. Ile was already preparing to enjoy his opium and did not want to be disturbed. "And I neither like nor trust that French gentleman, M. Lain." "Leave me now, Abbas," said Inlayer, "and keep your likes and dislikes to your- self." . . . .</p>
        <p>Abbas withdrew softly. With velvet steps he went downstairs, and, a minute or two later, was in the street. There, unknown to him, Kum, the Persian, followed him at a re- spectful distance. Rum was dressed as an Englishman. He wore a disguising mous- tache and false beard, and a slouch hat. To Abbas, however, his movements alone would have betrayed him. Rum was aware of this, and kept in the background. Abbas plunged into a low part of the city, and by and ha- entered an opium den. If his great master. Nadir Inlayer, enjoyed the delights of the opium pipe, why should not he also have that pleasure? Why should not bis dreams be golden? Why should be not think of that time when, having performed his part worthily here, the great Prophet might take his faiihful servant into the Elysium which he had always been taught to believe in. Abbas was stretched upon a sofa enjoying his pipe in the company of many other vic- tims of the same vice, when Ktim appeared. Abbas had already almost closed his eyes in . . .</p>
        <p>sleep. Kum asked for a pipe, but did' not smoke; he was watching the other man. After a minute he rose, took Abbas by the arm and roamed him roughly. "Your master, Nadir Julater, is in danger. You are to meet him at the Quay Alexandra without a moment's delay." The man awoke, gazed with lack lust- c eyes at Kum, who tore of his false beard- and tionStache, and revealed at once the face of a true l'ersian.</p>
        <p>"Be quick I" said Kim. The men left the opium den. 'll.e head of Abbas was reeling from the effeets of the opium. It seemed to him as he followed Kinn that it was all part of a dream. He was per- fectly happy and contented. His brain was so dull with the narcotic that be had not the smallest sense of fear.</p>
        <p>They reached the quay, where a man was seated in a boat. Kum jump2d in, dragging Abbas after him. The man rowed out to a small sailing boat. Kul and Abbas got on board, and in a few minutes' time the vessel sailed assay, leaving Marseilles far behind. When Julaver awoke the next morning, he was somewhat surprised, but not specially annoyed, at the absence of Abbas. He could do without him, although it was tiresome of the fellow to be away. He dressed and went downstairs. He went into the coffee room. The English lady was there having breakfast. She bowed to him pleasantly; lie bowed to her in return, and sat down by her side. It was better to talk to her than to no one.</p>
        <p>" Are you, too, going to Battimt'• he asked. "Yes." replied Mary; "and the delay is exceedingly tiresome." "it annoys me more than I can say," re- plied Julaver. "I am on my way to Teheran. I have important business to transact with my master, the Shah, and every hour is of consequence. If I could charter a steamer, I would do so." " Why don't you!" askrd Mary, toroidag at hins with sparkling eyes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>CHAPTER XXIV. TH2 OPIUM DREAM.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIV. TH2 OPIUM DREAM.</p>
        <p>It was about six o'clock on that eventful winter's evening when Rebecca Henshall en- tered the presence of the Mims Kara Yusuf. She came in breathlessly, her eyes very bright, her cheeks pale. He received her with a low salaam. He was holding in his hand the yellow envelope of • lately received telegram. "Ah," he said, "it has been hard to get you here, and the lady, the gracious lady. the English lady of high rank, is tired of waiting. Will you go to her to the :.00m where 'She awaits your presence?" Rebeka, astonished and relieved at this apparently mild reception, crossed the great and stately apartment, being led by one of the Persian servants. A door was flung open, and she found herself in a bright-looking room furnished in the English style, where poor Lady Jane, her face swollen with cry- ing, her whole appearance dishevelled almost beyond recognition, was waiting for her. "I have come," said Rebecca. "Oh! what is the matter? What has happened?" "My dear," said Lady Jane, in a choked voice, "I know you will blame me; I am pre- pared for any amount of censure. Don't, my dear child, don't, at least for a little, my nerves are so over strung! Can we get out of this?" _ .</p>
        <p>..... . "Of course we can," said Rebecca. "What is to keep us here?" She walked boldly towards the door of the room. Lady Jane clasped and unclasped her hands. .</p>
        <p>"You can't get out that way." she saki. The door is locked." But Rebecca still avnt towards it.</p>
        <p>"It is not locked," she said. She opened the door. "Come, dear Lady Jane." She drew the old lady's hand through her arm. Saunders immediately came forward. " Will you see us to our carriage?" said Rebecca to the inan. _ . .</p>
        <p>He accompanied them downstairs. They entered the carriage, where Silver, with a gasp of ecstacy, almost threw her arms round her beloved mistress's neck.</p>
        <p>"Oh! dear—dear lady! I thought never Lo we your face again.' "Nonsense!" said Lady Jane, who, now • ''</p>
        <p>that she was out of he trap, felt in- clined to make as little of it as possible. "Don't make a fuss, Sliver, and for goodness sake don't be seen crying in the street. Re- becca. you were a brick to come to me. I will tell you afterwards what occurred." One minute first," said Rebecca. _</p>
        <p>She bent towards 'Saunders, and said a few words to him in a low tone. In consequence, .te took off his hat politely to both ladies and walked away. They drove back at once to Lady Jane's house in Portland-place. The door was opened by George, looking just as though nothing special had occurred. The peaceful sensation of being at home again had a tonic effect on Lady Jane's nerves. Nevertheless, she was much shaken, and drew Rebecca with her into the dining- room. . . .</p>
        <p>..„ ..... "Now, my dear, shut the door," she said. "I must relieve my mind. You were right, Rebecca, and I was wrong. The Him is a terrible, incomprehensible man. Of course he is fascinating, my love. I really felt myself in Persia while I listened to him and his wonderful stone'. But I could quite see that he was sinister. Oh, my dear Rebecca, I had thrills through my whole body as I ob- served the strange, unearthly sort of light in his dark eyes. And he was so cold and firm. Do you know, Rebecca, he absolutely gave me the most exquisite little head of jade, and jade is so costly, dear, and this was such a perfect specimen. I have it in my bag; I will show it to you presently. "Well, he made me write for you. I was afraid to refuse. Of course I had no right to go in at all. But there—l did go, and I confess it, dear, the first part of my visit was enjoyable. Then I began to get frightened. He forced me to write that letter, and F saw he meant it, and that nothing would make him change, so I just had to do it. Imme- diately afterwards, he himself conducted me into that cold little room where you found me. He said an English servant would wait on me; but nobody came—not that I wanted anyone. I, of course, went straight to the door to get out, but it was locked, and, oh! then I had such a fit of terrors. I was nearly wild —I was so terribly afraid that you would not come." "Did you think I would leave you?" said Rebecca. "Of course I came as quickly as ever I could." -</p>
        <p>"Well, my child, you did take some time about it; it 'ass past six o'clock before you arrived, and I began to despair of you. I tried to summon the hotel officials, but there was no electric bell in the room, only one of those horrible telephones, which I haven't the least idea how to use. So I was virtu- ally a prisoner, and I grew more and more alarmed each moment. Why were you so long, dear Rebecca?" "I was taking steps," said Rebecca. "Steps, my love? What do you mean?" "I thought the position very serious, dear Lady Jane, and I went to Scotland-yard and got a detective to come with me to the Carl- ton." "You did? A detective? Horrors! Oh, Rebecca, they are such alarming, fearful men." "He was a very nice young man," said Rebecca, "and had there been danger he would have been useful, but as things turned out, there did not seem to be any danger'. I cannot imagine what the Mina wanted. As soon as I arrived, he had nothing whatever to say to me, and simply bowed me into your presence, and then we found that the door of your room was not locked at all. What can it mean, Lady Jane?" "I don't know, I never shall know," said Lady Jane. "But at least I have got the little head of jade, and I'm exceedingly glad to be home again. Oblige me, my love, by passing me that bag, and I will show you the precious little curio." During the days that followed Lady Jane was exceedingly pleased to relate her adven- tures to her numerous friends and acquaint- ances. She regarded herself as quite a heroine, and could not dilate enough on the majesty of the Mirza's bearing, the great and fearful dangers she herself had under- gone, and Rebecca's courage in going to Scotland-yard in order to help her in ease of need. There WAS a special case made for the little jade head, which Lady Jane declared she would keep as long as she lived in memory of about the biggest adventure of her whole life. Rebecca stayed on at Lady Jane's house, nothing at all spebial occurring, no mys- terious letters arriving for her, and no news whatever with regard to Gerald Lonsdale. No news, in his case, was good, however, and she '"•4l'. the best of eirrionsia.i.as.</p>
        <p>(To be concluded.)</p>
