      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST,</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST,</p>
        <p>A POWERFUL SERMON.</p>
        <p>The recurrence of the annual harve-4 thanksgiving services, ju-t now in full " blast " in these parts, reminds me of a contemporary's extraordinary rep rt of an event of the kind at Tiverton about twelve months ago. After giving particulars of the festival, the paper went on " The Rev. S. C. Heard was the preacher, when one of the couplings broke. Fortunately no farther damage was done." In this instance, the unexpected happened—in the case of the unlucky journal, at least. I remember one in which anticipa- tions were not fulfilled. A clergyman, preaching in a ccuntry church for the first time, was delighted to find the congregation so attentive, and told the sexton so after the service. The sexton replied : "Why, bless you, sir, we was all looking for you to disapperte." " Why, shut do you mean? " said the clergyman. " Well, sir you see, the pu,pit is rotten, and basal ben preached in this ten year or more ! " CHURCH'S " HANDYMAN."</p>
        <p>..... _ The appointment of anew curate to the Warrington Parish Church recalls an article by Frederick ' Sherlock in the " Church Monthly," in which the young clergyman of the class in question is described as " the handyman of the church.' I think the writer fairly makes good his claim in this regard, but of course I cannot here reproduce the article in extenao. It opens with the remark that the arrival of a new curate in a parish is the opportunity of the year for , all and sundry to exercise their inborn gift of acrid criticism. There are none too young and few to, old to have their "say " about the new cittate. His faults are discovered and laid bare, with a keenness and zest which leave no manner of doubt that the self- constituted critics are having a thoroughly good time , in picking boles in the coat of the )(rung man who has come amongst them to earn his living with a sincere desire to prove their friend. After much more to the like effect, with details in support, the writer I insists in conclusion that the nation owes a deep debt of gratitade to the curates, adding as to these " handymen" —"If: a difficult job has to be under- taken in a parish, what more easy than to roll it along to their shoulders. They have to turn their: hands to anything at any time. They have to be the 'Whiteleys' and 'Harrods' of their parishes, providing everything for everybody upon the shortest possible notice. In it not the duty of the laity to help the new curates with a little generous sympathetic appreciation, rather than hinder them with chilling, carping criticism?" Surely, to borrow a stock Parliamentary phrase, the answer is in the affirmative. THE ECU.</p>
        <p>_ The" Examiner" account of the English Church Union meeting in Warringtm brought to mind times I have spent at gatherings addressed by Lord Halifax and other men of eminence of that ilk. I must not, however, suffer betrayal into generalisation, even by way of reminiscence, but must confine myself to this particular and latest local instance. As usually the wood " Catholic " was much in evidence, and indeed, if my experience goes for any thing, the word " Protestant " is anathema to, and, so to put it, stinks in the nostrils of this section of the Anglican Church. It is all very instructive to the interested outsider, affording a curious comment on the attitude and ill- advised criticisms of certain clergy in reference to Nonconformity. As " Bagshot "(hit, J. A. Spender) once expressed it in the " Westminster Gazette," the Ai tides of the Church of England are lees of a com- promise than an equivocation. They are so drafted an to enable one party to maintain that they are Catholic and another party to maintain that they are Protestant. They represent, therefore, not a com- promise on which the two parties can unite, but a formula which enables them to go their separate ways 'under an appearance of unity. I merely here allude to the incontrovertible fact to enforce the moral of the well-worn proverb as to the folly of stone-throwing on the part of those who themaelves lire in glass houses. At the stme time! cannot help suggesting that the latter part of last week's resolution of protest against the charitable and characteristic action of the enlightened and truly Christian Bishop of Hereford, with its pretence of solicitude for the cause of home re-union, might well have been omitted. In my view, the brutal frankness of the " Church Times" were preferable. I for one have not forgotten what that E.C.U. organ had to say in relation to the reason- able observation of the Bev. Charles Brown, president of the Free Church Council, when the preparations for the Coronation were in progress. The Editor com- mented that no doubt Mr. Brown's ideal of the funcVon would be a programme including a prayer by Dr. Clifford, a reading by the president of the Wesleyan Conference, and an address by the Moderator of the Presbyterian Kirk of Scotland. Well and what of it? one may naturally ask. Aye. but here's the rub. "We notice," continued the" Church Times," " that Mr. Brown looks forward to the day when be and his fellow-preachers will share with the clergy the use of St. Paul's Cathedral and Westminster Abbey and other churches. We would rather make them a present of the exclusive use of those buildings than take our turns on the rots with them. The ides is unthinkable." " Home re-union," indeed! Well may Canon Henson contend that the possibility of any titian between this Church of Eng- land and the Evangelical Free Churches must depend upon the repudiathm of sacerdotalism by the &amp;tab- lished Church and the acceptance of the " evident logic of its ,itnat ion as a Reformed Church." Behind the hysterics at the Warrington meeting was the fear and dread of the disestablißhment or rtther the die. endowment bogey, and I had hoped for o space in which to convict some of the speakers out of Ike months of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCIIFOR D.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven beat Altrincbam " A " last Satnr- day by? golds to 2. Up to half time the Altrlncham men were quite the beet team and led at the Interval by 2-1. After this, however, the home team were on top. Pearson equalized and also giined the lead with • fine rising shot from a penalty corner. Altrincbam failed to soon again, their forwards being overshadowed by Knight and Wilkinson, sod Dixon at left back 'leo made many clean clearances. Travis and Pearson among the forwards were very good, the former scoring four times an i the latter twice. _ . . .</p>
        <p>The Serend &amp;even hod a hard game at Chester v. Chester" A" and in the end We, narrowly betties by 2-1. Miner&amp; soon op.oei the scoring with e pod shot and the confined pre wore by Chester kept Latchford strictly on defe we. It waa not until well on in the sensed half that Chester atterree led in 'vitalizing. Toe score re va1.:m.114 untilcluse on tine, when the horn". feint opn thraugh aril from a plushy °enter. Cb..1.1.r we the hot leen eel but f/f some sound full-heck pley by Pickle, and Parkinson the would bare leen larger. Bradshaw at centre half also gave s good display. The Pura Eleven • Clayton it afanchsster this afternoon is :—T Diann, goal ; P Allanson, F Tole, becks ; A Starkey, J II Wilkieson, A Knight, bsives ; Dale, J Pearson, 0 Travis, II Parker, E Banks, for- wards. Referee, Mr If Warburton. Train from Catral 1.55 p.m. The Second Eleven v Bowdon "II " at hatch( srd is :—I4 Wright, goal • W II Pickles, It Parkiminn, backs; A Kll:adshisw, F W Ishersond, It Walleh, helves; H Pletcher, II Harding, W II Mieards, H ' Colton, T Bank., forwards. Referee, Mr W Eccles. Dolly-off 2.43 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ELECTIVE AUDITORS ELECTION.</head>
        <p>ELECTIVE AUDITORS ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Polling took place In Widnes on Monday for the position of elective auditor. The retiring auditors were Mr A Moores and Mr 11 Ackerley. There wore floe candidates, and conserpiestly a heavier poll than is usually the case. The tn successful otodidatee were H Ackerley, 261; and F Manley, 267. Mr A Moores polled 163,J Feltner 76, sod T Harper Q.</p>
        <p>'MP Daresbury. HARVEST FESTIVAL. The harvest festival services in connection with Daresbury Pariah Church commenced on 'Thursday evening. September 21tb, the preacher beiag the Rev G IV Dickinson, vicar of eadgete. The offertories were for the Warrington Infirmary. On Sunday morn- ing the Hon and Rev C Cross, AA., Vicar of Stretton, preached to a large congregation. The preacher in the evening was the Rev T 'l' Lee Jones, vicar of Daresbury. Suitable hymns and anthems were sung at all the services is which the congregations joined heartily. Hatton. HARVEST FESTIVAL The harvest ft sties] was held in the Primitive Ilethrdist Chat el, Hatton, on Sunday. The building was tastefully decorated with fruit, vegetables, flowers and corn. The preacher was Mr. Midge of Runcorn who occupied the pulpit afternoon and evening. On Monday evening a sale of the fruit and vegetables was conducted in the schoolroom by lies. {V. Cooper 1 (superintendent of the Warrington Circuit). The proceede going to the chapel fund. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>HOW TO SPEAK.</head>
        <p>HOW TO SPEAK.</p>
        <p>In last week's issne of this paper a few " Dont's " for public speakess issued by Mr. Grenville Kleisher were reprinted. I thought of that authority's appear- ance at a meeting at Zion College, Victoria Embankment, London, on the occasi tn of the inauguration of the Public Speaking Club of Great rsritain under the auspices of the Y.M.C.A. Telling of the growth of the movement in New York, he remarked that they there trained their speakers to be concise, and to have the cyurage to sit down when they had finished. I was reminded of the advice which an old Parliamentary hand once gave in my hearing to an aspirant for municipal honours. It was simply this: "Never line to address the Council unless you really have something to say." It was in the vele chamber then in question that I heard the Father of the Council, an old Quaker,thus rebuke a forward and lognocious new corner : " Sit down, young man ; that haat only been here twenty minutes." It was about the same time that another budding orator, having delivered an address, turned proudly to his fricod and asked " Don't you think that ma; a finished speech ?" Yes," replied the friend slowly, " but at various paints I thonght it never wolild be !" But I must not forget my headline and obligations to which lam thereby committed I I have by me a back number of " Pearsents 1 Magazine" containing soon very good hints o the would-be orator, who, as the writer urges, should commence at a small meeting say, in the c,untry, where he is eure of a kindly disposed ann broad-minded audience. He is bound to he a little nervous at first, but each succeeding meeting will bring him more success. The greatest difficulty which he will have to contend with is the necessity of thinking while he is on his legs—thinkieg calmly and collectedly while facing an andience, which is vastly different from thinking while sitting in an arm- chair by the fireside. The points of the speech should be prepared neforehand, and marshalled in goad order. Illustrations or comparisons should never be wild or exaggerated ; appeals should be earnest, but not too dramatic. The orator should be as sincere and as straightforward as he poteibly can to his audience. Let him be honest —the surest way to the heart of the average Britisher. Hecklers must never be allowed to take the speaker away from his subject. They ehonld be ignored, or, if they are to 3 persistent, told that questions sill be allowed at the end of the speech. It never pays a speaker to lose bin temper with his hecklers. Sometimes smart repartee will turn the whole meeting into ridicule against the heckler, who finds that he his only succeedyi in making himself supremely foolish. "THE CRINOLINE SCARE."</p>
        <p>The contributor of "Women's Chat" t these poges had a useful paragriph under this heading on Satur- day last, and as I read it I thought (es the Walrus and the Carpen•er in "Alice" talked) of many things I happened just to have been dipping into hfr&amp; L B. Walford's " Recollections of a Scottish Novelist," and to have stumbled across s mis amusing reminiscences of the fashion and of what it involved. I venture to copy the following : -" What a thraldom that crinoline was! . . . The strange thing was that, no far as my recollection serves me, neither old our young resented the incubus which had been thrust upon us. We were wedged toge.her in carriages, with hoops billowing up to the roof; we scuttled crab-like through turnstiles; we were unable to pass in gangways; we endured every im iginable firm of inconvenience, and heard ourselves derided for it by [others, husbands, and brothers, and we boldly faced them. vowing that come what might, we would never, never give up our crinoline!" I have to confess (tell it not in gash) that I am old enough to remember this quite well. More than that, I have actually numbered among my acquaintances a lady who did not cease to weal the abomination until released by death scarcely a dozen years ago. The explanation was that her deceased husband was so enanoured of the monstrosity that hr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Orford.</head>
        <p>Orford.</p>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVAL. Harvest thanksgiving services were held in Orford Caurch on Sunday. The Rev. B. E. Taylor, curate.in- charge of St. Peter's Mivsion, Newtott.le-Willows, conducted the morning service and preached the sermon. He took for his text. Rev. siv.,ls, " Thrust in thy sickle and reap." Ile took this as a message first of all to apply to the clergy. A clergyman's calling was one of hard work and activity, lie had to/ go shout among the people,eowing the seed of God's word. The field was very often white unto harvest, and they were com- manded to thrust in the sickle and romp. Hs would oprivey the message next to the Sunday school teachers, who had a lot of young children nder their care, and to parents who had children growing up in the world, their responsibility was very great. The message wee also intended for all Church workers. They each had a certain amount of influence and a corner in the harvest field and the command for all was " thrust in thy sickle and reap." A children's service was held in the after- noon, when the Vicar. The Rev G Vale Owen,addreseed a good attendance of scholars and friends. The ohtirch was full for the evening service which was conducted by the Vicar, the Rev. B. Perc.val, of St. Peter's Warrington, preached the sermon, taking for his text, Psalm evil. 2, " Let the redeemed of the Lord say so." He said that if is man had a narrow escape from death, be would never tire of telling people about it. My then should he not go about telling of God's Rood works? A man would pray ti God to save him from some threatening calamity,snd be was miraculously saved or he might pray for something to turn up to save him from flnancial ruin,and something turned up just in time. Why then should be not go about and tell his fellow men how it bad Peen done ? "Let the redeemed of the Lord any so " The choir fovea good rendering of the anthem " Slimed be the name of the Lord." The collections throughout the day were given to various charities. The services will be continued on Sunday, when the preachers will be the Rev. Croon Morley Stevenson, in the morning, and the Rev. W. A. Gibson, of Birkenhead, in the evening.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall. PARISH COUNCIL MEETING. The monthly meeting of the Grappenhall Parish Council was held cn Wednesday evening last. There were present :—Mr F Mitring (in the chair) end Messrs A Lister-Kaye, J Carter, A Radial), J Bradford, F Ellison, F Fox, Dr Hutt, and Charles H Hoult (clerk).</p>
        <p>THE SN.VINO BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter front the County Surveyor stating that he was in communication with the Ship Canal Authorities re the Latcsford Swing Bridge. OVERSEERS' HALF-YEARLY ACCOUNTS. The overseers' half -yearly accounts were read sad parsed. Balance in hand March 31st, £BBB Hs Id ; income half-year, £822 8s 81 ; total, £1,711 4s 9d ; expenditore for the half-year, £1,289 2s 3d ; balance in hand, .C 122 2s Gd. A D ‘NOEROUS CORNER. Mr. Radlirth moved that a new lamp be placed at the corner of Chester-road facing the Dog and Dart. It was a very dangerous corner. Mr. have seconded, stating that only recently a local lady had roet with an accident which might have ended more seriously. The motion was carried. LATE CHAIR hl AN RESIGNS. Mr. Hadflrth stated that owing to continued ill-health be had to offer his resignation to the Council. lie was sorry that he could not continue to serve them. Under the circumstances he was forced to resign. Ha might not be in the district another month. The Ch drman said they were very sorry indeed to lose the services of no useful a member es Mr. Radii rth. Especially under such circumstances and in accepting Mr. Radflrth's resignation, he hoped that ere long he would be permanently restored to health. Mr. Rays supported the Cartirmack's remarks and hoped that Mr. Itadflrth would soon again he enjoying good health. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Stockton Heath. CHRYSANTHEMUM SHOW. FINE DISPLAY.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath. CHRYSANTHEMUM SHOW. FINE DISPLAY.</p>
        <p>The annual chrysanthemum show promoted by the Stockton Heath Garden Society, took place last Satur- day. The Church Imola, Victoria-square, presented a pretty appearance. but the handicap experienced by the bad season was reflected in there not behig as many exhibits as in acme previous years. The judging was in the capable hands of Mr Poulton, and Mr Wilkinson. There were some fine groups sent by gentlemen for non- competition. Mr H it Armitage seat some delicious fruit ; Mr Joseph Blinston, roses and cut blooms ; Mr Joseph Ryan, ruses ; Mr .1 Whitley, ornamental guard ; eon Sir Gilbert Oreenall, Bart, C.V.0., a Leuutiful display of plants. A fair number of people assembled at the opening ceremony. Sir Gilbert Greensll who opened the show, presided end supporting him were Mr. W. 11. Woodcock (chairman of ;he committee) Mr. E. 0. White, hon. secretary and members of the committee. Sir Gilbert said if they looked round they could not fail to be very pleased at the high quality a f the 'Whitt. The society bad had some very uphill work and had coutended with several difficulties, but the metnEers had faced them ins most determined manner and it was hoped hefore the end of the year to wipe out the deficieucy in the I finances. (Applause.) It vers not an easy matter when a society was in trouble to pull it up to th 3 top. When matters went well there were supporters in plenty, but when they went the other way stares number of people were always ready to say what should have bean done. Ile did not think, however, that people had regarded the society in that light but had not themselves the task of puttine it in order and having as good a show as possible, without launching into any extravagance. (Applause). He hoped there would be good atten- dances during the day and that the concert would be the means of raising a little money. (Applause). They could not run a society of this character without money and if the reople of Stockton Heath and Warrington would support it he was sure they would be able in time to make the show the best any society could have. (Applause) He had great pleasure in declaring the allow ores. (Applause). Mr. F. A. Jenkins proposed and Mr. H. B. Houghton seconded a vote of thanks to the judges. In responding Mr. Wilkicson said (be freshness of the blooms reflected very great credit on the exhibitors. he season had been a most trying one even for pro- fessional gardeners, although he did not know a hether professional men took quite as much interest in the work as amateurs did. (Laughter.) When men were as euthusiastic as the exhibitors In that show had teen they were worthy of encouragement. (Applause.) veto of tbatl;s was accorded to Sir Gilbert on the peopitiitiee ef Mr. Woodcock seconded by Mr. J. )dcDermid. The following Is a list of the prLla tOtttell I Six varieties in spray: 1, A J slottram ; 2, E Whitehouse; 3,P Cliffe. Three varieties: 1, Lockett; 2, Mrs E Mott ram; 3, J Owen. One Emmy or stem, not containing MOTS than nine blooms, any variety : 1, A Ifettrani ; 2, E W Whitehouse; 3, H Lyons. Six varieties, single blooms 1, E Pollard ; 2, J Colley ; 3,A J Mottram. Two plants in blcom, two varieties: 1, p EIJI% ; 2, GI Williams ; 3, E Vernon. One plant in bloom : 1,0 Willielce; 2, Cliffs; P 3, 5 Vettion. Bowl cm yam cf cite rariety of citryarniben unto arranged for a fleet : 1, Mrs Hargreaves; 2, P Cliffs; 3,,Loclett. Bowler vase, arranged for effect, contain- ing two or mote varieties at own option 1, E W Whitehouse ; 2, A .1 Mottram ; 3, Mrs E Idettram. Epergne, arranged for effect, containing any number of varieties stows option : I, Mrs E Mottram ; 2, Mrs Marge ; 3, A J Mottram. Single chryeanthea.um. three varieties in sprays, two of each : 1, Mrs E Mottram ; 2, E W Whitehouse. M ISCELLAIMOI7B.-Six dahlias fe,actcts : 1, F Eippax ; 2, D S Birrell ; 3, A J Mottmm. Sax dahlias (porn-pone) : I, F Klemm ; 2,11 Lyons ; 5, E Taylor. Eighteen single dahlia in three vases : I. A J Mottram; 3, F. Taylor; 3, F Kippur. Collection of out-door blootur, not to include more than three vases of any variety, space not to exceed three feet : I, P Chile ; 2, F Eippox ; 3.5 W Whitehouse. There vases of cactus dahlias 1, F Kippers. ;2,A .1 Mottram ; 3, D S Birrell. Three vases of roses (twelve) 1, A .1 Mottram. Three vases of pom-pone dahlias (twenty. four) : 1, E Taylor ; 2, H Lyons; 3, F Ripples. Collection of si c varieties of cut flowers in six versa: 1, Mrs Hargreaves : 2, A J Mottlam ; 3, If W White- house : special merit, PCliffe. JUNIOR SECTION.-BOS/Let of autumn berries and foliage: 1, Helga Acton ;2, Sibyl Crossley ; May White ; specials, Bertha Langley and Lily Melling, One spray of chrysanthemums : 1, Stanley Birrell ; 2 Daniel Birrell. As usual the concert in the evening drew a good audience. The VSTIOOS items were capitally rendered and a very enjoyable time was experienced. The society's orchestra, under the baton of Mr. W. E. Ray</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>T MEWS</head>
        <p>T MEWS</p>
        <p>played some charming music and the audience was not slow to appreciate the s trne. The items were as follow: —March, " Coder Freedom's 11 kg," caprice, " Bal Tranmerci," the society orchestra ; humorous sketch, " Hew Bill Adams won the Battle of Waterloo," Mr E A Sanders; song, " Roses," Mr A Greenough : humorous selection, Mr Harvey 0 Adams ; song, "Will- o'-th'•Wisp," Mr McArthur ; overture, " L'Espoir de L'Alsace," the society orchestra ; song, " Within your heart," Miss M H G Indy ; humorous sketob, " Pro- posals," Mr E A Sanders ; conjuring feats, Mr A White; eons,' Flight of ages," Miss E Barnes ; clarinet solo, Mr H Ravenport ; duet, Messrs A Greenough and McArthur ; humorous song, Mr Harvey 0 Adams ; song, "Moss," Mr A Greenough ; selection, "Tom Jones," the society orchestra ; song," There's a land." bliss M M Gandy ; recital, Mr Harvey 0 Adams ; "Sailor's grave," Mr McArthur; sing," Annie Laurie,'' Mina E Barnes. The accompaniments on the piano were played by Mr F H Banner and Mrs Berry.</p>
        <p>. . _ Culcheth. NEWCHURCII PARISH CHURCH. The harvest festival services in connection with thin place were bold on Send ly meet, the preacher for the morning service being the Rev. B. T. Bowker, M.A., vicar of St. Nathaniel's, Platt Bridge. The anthem "Fear not, 0 Land," WAS very ably sung by the choir at this service. In the afternoon, a young people's service was held conducted by the vicar, the Rev E W W Kaye. The Rev C A Mercer, M.A., of St. Philips Hulme, was the preacher at the evening service, when the church was filled to overflowing. The anthem " Greet is the Lord," wee sung hy the choir, the quartette being ably rendered by Miss Marsh, Miss B tirunily, Mr J Leatherbarrow and Mr J Foster. The church had been very beautifully dec3r_ited for the occasion by members of the congregation, and the collections which were takes at each service were in aid of the choir funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>SION Cut Cli ERRY--L ANS.—The !harvest thanks- giving ;services were held' on Sunday last, when the services were conducted by Mr. James Evans, of Warrington, who preached two excellent sermons to well-attended congregations. Mr. H. T. Mercer presided at the organ. On Monday evening Alderman Evans gave a lecture on "The story of my life," which proved very interesting to all. Afierwards the fruit and vege- tables were sold in the schoolroom. The collections were devoted to the Caurch Fund from all services. LIBERAL ASSOCIATION AND WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSoi: ATION.—A social was held on Wednesday even- ing in the Primitive 'Methodist schoolroom. The attendance wis not so large owing to other entertain- ments in the district, but this seemed to ha forgotten in the enjoyment of the songs by several artistes. and other items of interest which helped to make the evening thoroughly enjoyable. THE SECTOR AND Ly 3l DEV ELOPM Writing in the Parish Magazine, the Rector says have just returned from Messrs. Lever Bros.' sale at the Drill Hall. It is difficult to foresee what permanent effect these great changes will have on the future of Lymm. I am, and hope to remain, ea incurable , optimist, though the anxiety of making wise decisions is weighing very heavily on many. May Ood guide all concerned with an abundance of His Holy Spirit.l.et us set aside all hindrances to godly union and cancord and seek in prayer the blessings which ere never wanting to those who unreservedly pet their whole trust in Him CHILDRESS' UNION or laiso's MEssesomts.—The annual sale and extUbition was held In the Drill Hall, on Wednesday, when the goods consisting of wood- work. baskets, brassworlc, needlework, toys, etc., made by several of the children at the weakly meetinas in the winter months were exhibited for lisle. Refresh- ments were provided and there was also a flower and fruit stall. Shooting and other competions found favour with several of the young folks. Messrs Tonks quadrille band NVs in attendance during the exhibition and concluded its programme by playing to the dancing which took place in the evening, quite • good number being present. The sale was in every way a success and great praiee is due to those ladies who looked after the various stalls. The proceeds are to be divided between the Waifs and Strays Society and the S.P.O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>A NE, NT:RES.—The Padget° lliitrict Nurse Associa- tion bee engaged Nurse Street to take up the duties of the District irom the 2nd October. Nurse Street Mlles with excellent testimonials, and the Committee will be pleased to receive a continuation of the sympathy and aupport hitherto accorded to the Association.</p>
        <p>REV. IL G. ROBERTS ON "TUIS NOISY WORLD."</p>
        <p>In connection with the opening of the New Wesleyan Sunday school at l'adgate special services were held on Sunday. The Rev. H. G. Roberts, M.A. (formerly of the Warrington circuit, now of Sheffield; was the preacher, both morning mil evening. Ths anthem sung at the former service was "I am the Alpha and Omega." A special service was also held in the afternoon presided over by Alderman Bennett, who in the course of an address, said that whatever joy, comfort and happiness he had had in his life he attributed to ths training he received in the Sunday school. The Rev. Colley Hutchinson spoke upon the need of the scholars having God as a guide through life. Four children, trained by Mr. Levi Richardson, gave a tuneful render- ing of' In our hearts celern tat voices" and they also contributed a hymn by Mrs. Gladding, which appears in the new Sunday school hymn book. The church was crowded long before the evening service began and the sermon by the Rev. H. G. Rohmth was listened to with marked attention. In setting out he asked the question: Does ambition Sod its plane among the Christi. 'virtues? Its mid that ambition la acme form or other W 29 an innate craving of man and they all acknowledged that it had played a very important part in the education of the world and in the evolution or man in his mental and spiritual develop- ment. He knew that ambition had been spoken of as the mat infirmity of a noble mind, but he thought history bore witness that it hsd been the inspiration of many noble minds. True ambition was a condition of all true greatness. What would the world be to-day without emulation, without honounble rivalries? There were many things worth striving for and FORMS OF POWER TO BR ESREESTLY COVETED</p>
        <p>_ _.......—.. in the service of Jesus Christ and His Church. He wanted to invite the young folks to be ambitious in the service of our Lord; he wanted to inspire them with inward dissatisfaction and discontent, and he wanted them to have a craving for higher things. St. Paul was one of the most ambitions of men, for it was he who 'said "Study (be ambitious) to be quiet and to do your own business." The first object of a Christian's ambition was concentration of thought, purpose and effort. That was a marked feature in the life of our Blessed Lord and Master. He set aside all the work that He mune not to do that He might concentrate upon the work lie came to do. There was no fruitless expenditure of force in the life of Jesus, all was done no thoroughly with all due dignity, gravity and beauty. And we discerned in Jesus Christ our example as well as Saviour of that restless and th.t feverish baste which so cfteu marred our service to-day and robbed it of no much of its beauty, dignity and power. St. Paul's axiom "Be ambitious to be quiet" was a message to the world to-day. To shout was a very great gift to the people of to-day. We might warble most divinely, we might sing like a nightingale, we might echo and re-echo dulcet notes but</p>
        <p>IF W E COULD NOT SHOUT WE WERE NO GOOD</p>
        <p>a a aor.suoc. a a a aaa in this prattling age—an age of fever and excitement. A novel tinnily received attention 'unless it is written at the top of the voice, • speaker had very little chance cf attention unless he screamed and shouted in superlatives. The leading articles of some of our newspapers had to be written at the top of the voice in order to compel the attention of some people—not thoughtful people, for, thank God, there was always a grand minority who gave due value to the majesty of silence. Con' 1144, the rev, gentleman said that although be looked upon the Methodist Church as an p2rt of the Holy Catholic Church there had teen</p>
        <p>- A LOT OF SWAGGER, e lot of tall talk ; they did not always work quietly, but liked to get into the limelight now and again ; if a prominent member of the Church rneezed it was , reported in the Methodist Fapers. There must he a I tohch of the dramatic in sverythicg they ppt the:. hands to do. Many of their moot conscientious end thoughtful people were wondering what to do, for there were so many forces In their Church life which tended to vulgarism—there were some so-called successes which were worse than failures. They declared that they were making history when they were cnly shout- ing platitudes on a platform—they knew what he meant, politically revolutionising society when they were only talking six et it. Yes, the Methodiat Church Ler ded to learn to have confidence in the quiet spirit. Let others sing their praises. They must believe MOTO and work more, for to pray was to work. A little more medita- tion would obviate the tendency to rush. With Clod as the centre of their thought they would progress spiritually. They shou'd live momect by moment with the vivid sense of their accountability to God. During the service Mr. Levi Richardson ably "sang "Cell to remembrance " and the choir contributed The Magnill at," Misses Sileock and Loudon and Messrs. Richardson and Dutton taking the solo parts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Dr. Bareardo's Homes.</head>
        <p>Dr. Bareardo's Homes.</p>
        <p>We are asked to state that as a result of the recent t collection in Warrington on behalf of Dr. Barnardo's Homes, the sum of £l9 has been remitted to the headquarters of the Institutions, and has been buitab ly acknowledged on behalf of the Honorary Director ar d Council of the Homes, who desire us to Ewes-. their gratitude to the collectors for their self-denyirg service, and to the public for their generous response to the appeal. Any-outstanding contributions may be sent to Mr. F. E. Rainer, organising secretary of the Bernardo Satuiday Fund, 18 to 20, Stepney Causeway, Lon'.on, E.</p>
      </div>
