      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WEEK. IRISH AFFAIRS_ MINIMUrWACE FOR CORPORATION NOTES FOR IRISH READERS. EMPLOYEES. Councillor Halliwelis- Motion Carried.</head>
        <p>WEEK. IRISH AFFAIRS_ MINIMUrWACE FOR CORPORATION NOTES FOR IRISH READERS. EMPLOYEES. Councillor Halliwelis- Motion Carried.</p>
        <p>THE PARNELL MONUMENT.</p>
        <p>The outstanding event in contemporary Irish affairs is the unveiling of the Parnell monument. Nothing within the memory of living Irishmen can quite equal in significance the great outpouring of a deeply-felt national sentiment which was witnessed in Dublin on Sunday Jest, when Mr. John Redmond, the successor of the great dead leader, unveiled the impressive monument to Charles Stewart ParnelL Memorable as was the occasion, attracting as it did an interest confined to no one continent but stretching round the whole world, it gained additional significance from theturrounding circumstances of the time, which linking together the past and the present, the nearand the remote, emphasised in an indescribable way the striking interest and importance of this great national event. From whatever view point the political survey may be made, it is highly encourag- ing and eminently favourable to the cause to which Parnell consecrated his life and genius. At no time has the position of the constitu- tional and democratic movement of the Irish people been so strong as it is to-day. To such a degree is that truth manifest that even the slanderers and calumniators of the Irish have publicly declared that beyond raising dust in the eyes of the uninformed they are powerless to stop the onward march of a nation to its goal of equity and liberty, the well-earned fruits of self- government. The final campaign for and against Home Rule coincides in its opening with the unveiling of the statue of an Irish Irishman whose unerring statesmanship has already gained for him a permanent place in the world's roll of national leaders. To-day, twenty years after his strenuous life ended, it is possible to estimate his work for his country and to discern rightly his true place in that country's history. Not unlike many other constructive thinkers and fearless workers his short life ended in the midst of warring noise that might well disturb the minds and deflect the opinions of even the most rational men. But now in a cooler moment and with a truer perspective the minds of men not only in this Kingdom but far beyond are perceiving the figure of this much misunderstood reformer to grow in magnitude, and the importance of his work to be more accurately estimated as the years go by. And, as any man who takes the pains to read the later portion of Morley's life of Gladstone will tell you, if to-day we are on the threshold of Home Rule, no man is more accountable for that leading. fact of modern politics than the man whose life ended with the heavy sadness of Greek tragedy, just twenty years ago under an English roof in Brighton. And so, in a time of acute indus- trial war, when Ireland was temporarily stricken with the terrible consequences of a railway strike, a time of social distress which expresses itself in bread riots, the name and the fame, the memory and the honour of Parnell, were sufficient to draw togsther a generation after his death one of the most impressive and one of the most gigantic demonstrations ever witnessed on Irish soil. No eleme.t was necessary that could have lent interest and could give emphasis to the intensely national character of the demonstration. Every county of Ireland was represented and this despite the fact that railway travelling was denied to the whole province of Munster. Ulster's huge contingent could not fail to suggest to such Englishmen as witnessed the demonstration how solid and how real was the amification of the Irish forces.</p>
        <p>The much vexed question of a minimum wage for corporation employees came op for consideration at the conclusion of the ordinary business of the Warriegton Town Council on Tuesday evening, when Councillor James Barnwell moved: 'That no able- bodied man employed under the Corporation and having reached twenty-one years of age he paid less than at the rate of twenty-four shillings per week, and that this resolution come into force as soon as practicable after the let January next." Councillor HALLiwELL arid: I have now very great pleasure in putting this motion before you for your serious consideration. When we investigate this matter, we Rod that there is a general feeling that the claims of the able-bodied man should be considered, and that he should be better paid for his labour. THE RECENT aTlitites and the still prasent feeling of unrest, all point in this direction. I am informed that several large firms in the to*n, have already made increases to their work- men, without application being made from the men themselves, and it is general knowledge that other firms contemplate following their ezample. I am also gives to understand by a gentleman that the firm with which he is connected have decided to inures.° their labourers wages to a minimum of 21s. a week. I understand it D now six years since a motion was carried in this Council, that the minimum wage of the Borough workmen should he 21s, per week. During those six years, the tendency has been that everything, rent, food, clothing, &amp;c, have become dearer—and now at the present time, a lEW with a house to support, is greatly handicapped to make ends meet on his weekly earnioga. I look upon this v.a an opportune time for giving an increase of wages to our able-bodied workmen—and also for setting an example to all employers of labour to do the same. For,gentle- men, when we consider the conditions of life, what may have been sufficient payment in the past, is insufficient to-day. And as time rolls on there is every appearance that there will not be any reduction in the cost of living—but on upward tendency. I am also infor.red that in towns, which have adopted a standard minimum wage, that minimum is fixed at 255. or more per week. So that, gentlemen, my motion does not carry us to be on a level footing with those towns. We shout I still be under their standard rate,if this increase is granted. My feelings are, gentlemen, that I should be only too pleased to Bee the minimum wage of EVERY ABLE-BODIED MAN 151 OCR TOWN to be at the rate of 2ls. per week, and I look upon this rate of wages as being only a fair and reasosable increase, all things being considered. I also think, that there may he amongst as, probably, a few who think my motion forgo lucre ise of 3s. per week, per man, to be a great advance and a heavy pull on the rates. It probably meace an increase in the rates of Id. in the £. Whilst desirous of keeping down expenditure I cannot but call your attention to advances of salary, which have been granted to officials and others, during the past few years. From the year book I and that the increases have averaged something like £5lO or £l5lO per year. We who have a little, do not have these little home worries, and for that reason alone we owe a duty to our lees fortunate brethren, to see to it that homes shall be made blighter, by a little sacrifice of individual opinion. Councillor LASHFORD and Councillor DiceeNso&amp; rose torther to second the motion and after bargaining for a minute or more Councillor Dickenson gave way, Councillor LASHFORD explained that lb months ago he moved a similar resolution with regard to the scavengers. He believed that the workingmen were really in need of the extra rise which was proposed, for it could not be denied that the increased cost in food made it very hard for a man with a family. Councillor HA WTHo RN said the question was a very important matter and he regretted that the Council was no depleted when they were considering advance in wages of 3a. per week. A great point in favour of the motion was the undoubted INCREASED tOnT OF Everything had gone up except workmen's wages, end the least they could do was to prant a concession. They were setting a man an impossible task when they asked him to bring up a family on a guinea a week. He could not do it; they continually . hund such men in "the County Court running up debts to £3O and .£4O. It also meant that when he vas ill he bad to go to the Infirmary, and the wife received out-relief, and in that Imanner the burden which ought to be on the employer ras on the general community. The supply for labour being greater than the demand the labour market was overcrowded, and so compelled .eorkingmen to accept miserable wages. He did not know that he would be prepared at entre to give all the men an advance of 3s. a week on an all round scale. A differential scale might be adopted so that the alf.e.bodied men would be able to get 243. Alderman BEYNETT proposed that the consideration of the matter be adjourned as they had buds sitting of nearly three boom. He drew attention to the small attendeeso and added that in the heated atmosphere of an election he did not think it should be settled. Alderman liolfroN seconded Alderman Bennett's motion. Its was in sympathy with Councillor resolution,but lie would not think of adopting a general scale. The matter, he said, should be discussed by the General Pu poses Committee, where they would all be able to speak their minds. Then a sub-committee might be appointed to see the VariOUS departments. There were some meson the Gas Committee who were well worth the proposed 215., but he could not agree to it indiscriminately. Councillor PEMBERTON did not think the matter should be adjourned. In his opinion those who had left the meeting would not be there the next time it was brought up. fly eight votes to seven it eat decided to continue the discussion and Cauncillor Pemberton said that more and more the laws of this municipality and country called upon the work iogclasses to live in more healthy conditions and this they could not do unless they had better wages. He objected to Alderman Bennett leaving the Council Chamber when there was an opportunity for him to vote for the men to get higher wages. The alderman was always speaking of an ideal state ant telling the people what they ought to do, that it was a shame that they should live in hovels and that it was a disgrace to our civilization and education that people should live in such places. The MAYOR: Undid not say that to-night. Councillor PEMBERTON : 3.0, but he has said it many a time. I have heard him reel it off by the yard —he could go on for years. And yet Alderman Beenett would not stop to give poor men higher wages so that they might do it. Councillor DICKENSON in supporting the motion remarked that be di I not believes gu in.-a a week was au. ins wage. He quiteacknowledged that six years ago they granted to the scavengers and a number of their employees a guises pe: we,'&lt;, but he main- tained that those men were 35 PER ceNT. woRSE OFF TO-DAY</p>
        <p>thsn they were then, for everything, wearing apparel, and rents had gone higher. Taking it that a man with a wife and f unity of live children lived ia s house at 2s. 9d. a week, n guinea a week only left 2s. 4d. a week each on which they had to be fed and clothed. Councillor HALUIVELL'S motion was then pnt to the meeting and was mulled unanimously. The following voted for the motion :—Alderman Janies Evans, Councillors Halli cell, Locker, Ashcroft, Hawthorn, Dickenson, Cunnington, Pemberton. Lankford, Skelland, Cheaper, Wilkinson, Toole and Dr. McKee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>MR. REDMOND'S ORATION.</head>
        <p>MR. REDMOND'S ORATION.</p>
        <p>Nor did the oration delivered by the Leader of the Irish to-day leave anything to be desired. In grave yet moving tones, Mr. Redmond briefly told that portion of Parnell's life which makes him an outstanding figure in the history of democracy's advance, and that part of his work which indeed gives Parnell a right and title to universal fame. " -He saw the Irish peasant of his day a serf and in misery—cheated, hunted down, terrorised. He was moved to the depth of his nature by what he saw, and he resolved to become a champion of this serf against his wrongs and to make him a free man in a free nation." That summarises in simple language Parnell's aim and object, and the student of history will see that the present triumph of his far-seeing mind places Parnell in the catalogue of world leaders, such as the American Lincoln, the Polish Kosciusko, and the English Gladstone. The "forlorn hope " as it was termed, of Parnell in the early eighties, is the accomplished fact of to-day so far as the serf or the land is concerned, and whilst it is true that the Irish nation has still to enter on the era of its Self-Government the union of the democracies of England and Ireland to-day makes that proposal an assured certainty. Against the serried ranks of a mighty empire, against the forces of landlordism and monopoly and the powers of a coercion government behind them, the pale faces and unknown Parnell commenced to lead a race who were said to be "crushed and broken- spirited," and, if there be one suggestion in last Sunday's gathering, alike to English and Irish students of social and democratic advance, more opportune than another it is surely the suggestion of the power of unity in an army fighting for political or social emancipation. At any rate with the Irish- man there can be no doubt that his mind is re-inspired with the true appreciation of what unity and courage mean by the , memory of the man whose statue was unveiled on Sunday last.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
