      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>RESCUE REWARDS.</head>
        <p>RESCUE REWARDS.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court, on Monday, Mr. J. B Aitken, chairman, was asked by Superintendent Strick- land to make a pfesentat ion to William James Tickle, I, Peech-terrace, cud Michael Fallon, 51, Oxford-street, Widnes. The presentation consisted of an illuminated address and 20s. in moray, which had been awarded to both of them by the Liverpool Shipwreck and Humane Society. Tickle, on July 31, while working on toe Transporter Bridge, rescued a boy from drowning in • strong ebb tide fr.im the river Mersey. Fallon, a boy, was on the Sankey and St. Helens Canal side on August 29, when he saw a boy on the c pposite side in difficulties. He dived in, and, with the assistance of two workmen, the toy was rescued.</p>
