      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>-- TRACELESS TRAMS UNDER CONSIDERATION ' The St. Helene Corporation bane sppointed a committee to consider and report on the trackkee trolley system of running tramcars. PRAISE FOR CIVIC OFFICIALS. The St. Helena Parliamentary Committee, at their last meeting, received a report on the recently paved Coronation Bill, which received the Royal assent on August 18th, and passed a reeolution placing on record their appreciation of the ability displayed by the Town Clerk, Mr. W. 11. Andrew, in the drafting of the St. Helens Corporation Bill, and of the manner in which the briefs and other particelare in connection with the bill were presented to counsel, and to earners their thanks for the valuable services AO rendered by him to the Corporation. The committee have also thenked t'e other officials who were called in to assist and give evidencs. NEW INSTITUTE FOR ST. HELENS Y.M.C.A. Colonel W. W. Pilkington, president of the St. Ilelens Y.M.C.A., on Tuesday night opened a new institit'e, whith is to be devoted to the junior motion f the association. The old manse in College-street, which the askociation occupied before the new ttilding was erected in North-road, laas bees altered and improved, and two large rooms have been formed which will he eminently useful for the boys, for whom a speciM recre- tary has, been appointed. Colonel Pilkington was accompanied by Mrs. Richard Pilkington, Mr. A. C. Pilkington, and Councillor Norman Pilkington, and they were met at the new institute by the I Fleet Y.M.C.A. troop of Scouts, with their new hug. , Colonel Pilkington, after opening the door, f addressed the memtere and friends assembled, I and sedd it was possible to do more with the F boys than with the grown-ups, and in the hope that a good work would be done they had opened that institute. A reception w s afterwards held in the Y.M.C.A. Gymnasium, when Colonel Pilkington pre- sided and there was a large gathering. Addresses were delivered, and Sir Matthew Dorlawortb, Bart., presented to the members of the Y.M.C.A. the junior team the r.edals won as TlMllera spin the championship of the Liverpool and District Gymnasia League, and also to George Wareing, son of Councillor It. Wareing, of Parr, a silver medal and framed certificate from the Lieerpool nod District Hamane Society for saving the life of a hop who was drowning inc pool at Derbythife-hill, (Parr, on July 15th last.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <p>Sir,—Your columns of Saturday contain a few refer encee to the late strikes, and a few platitudes from the lige of an M.P. m}.4o real'y ought to have nwre sense. At the annual meeting of POPIIIIOII and Knowles, Ltd., Mr. Hammel Banner, M.P., in moving the adoption of the port, said "that it was necessary for workers to takeihese facts to their Mutest_ conscience at d test . •</p>
        <p>them.' I wish they would. It will tot be each a glorious day for figure head directors when that happens. Mr. Harrnood Benner deliberately suggests that the investor is the true philanthropist, and if that does not imply that the worker is livimg on the .inatestor I fail to see the drift of his argument. What 1r ere the true frets of the case? The figure head director is a paean who lives on the wealth that is created by the workers, to he exact, on that portion which remains after some (not all) of the needs of the wcrkers fare been satisfied. With regard tb his assertion that the new mills were primarily intended for the production of steel, is not that untrue? The eipw mills are primarily intended for the production of profits and the production of steel is p e"oondary con- eideration. In fact, if his firm could make more profits by prokluciog margarine, standard bread, or golf halls fo-r instance, than they can out of the production of steel, then the world reared have to go without steel for all the directcus of P. crud K. cared. Mr. Bleakly, in seconding, said "that sympathetic strikes were a serious menace to icdustrial peace and the whole social organization." Mr. James Ball at the Debating Society said 'That is general strike would moult in revolution." These two assertions, coming as they do from two such diverrnt soirees, constitute One of the best arguments in favour of this general strike that could he advanced. acd right to Oct as an inducement to those millions oho were said by the late Prime .Minister to be co itinually on the verge r 1 destitution, to strike hard and often. Barely people are beginning tO see that in the roldst of plenty thererg should be no star.: 1,7 _me, and ia only requires a revqation in the distribution of the wealth annually prc...uced, in order to provide for all Cue necessaries of life. A revolutLn would bs a quick way of altering the system of distribution that is practiced to-day. This system says that the more you produce, the sooner will the market be glutted; when the market is glutted and the warehouses are bursting with merchandise, then you (the worker) will be unemployed, because you have produced too mach, bi cause ycry` bane worked too hard. Take Mr. Barmood Banner s advice, take the facts to your innuat conscience and test them, and having done so you will know what value to pia' e on the platitudes of figure bead director*, and maybe you tql realise that a revolution in the manner of the distribation of the annual income is just what you require, and if, as Mr. Bleckly I (financier), and Mr. James Ball (one time Labour Councillor) assures you a general strike iroul,, mean a revolution, then I suggrst that the thanks of all workers are due to the above named gentlemen for no clearlypointing out the rrobable results of striking.— Yours A. W., PEAUSON. 4, Ash Grove, Latchford.</p>
        <p>‘lt`c 41 ) tr i . cti ‘Ztrosthe EDITOR;</p>
        <p>Mr. Harmood Banner. M.P ani the Workers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>SHIP CANAL TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>CHIEF ENGINEER'S SAD END AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>A sensation was crested at Latchford at five o'clock last evening, when the body of Mr. Hubert Congreve, of Summerville, GrappenhalL chief engineer of the Manchester Ship Canal Cc., was recovered from the Ship Canal near Cantilever Midge. It appeals that for a few weeks Mr. Congreve had been suffering, from a nervous breakdown and his medical attendant' advised him 19 go away fir a charge. On Tuesday be 0., wrote a letter to the Chairman of the Company asking for a few weeks holiday, but he did not post it until Thursday evening. flefore going out about 7 o'cl,ck he left a nets at home which gave rise to fears that he contemplated doing away with himself. As he did not return, grappling operations were commenced in the Bridgewater Canal tinder the direction of Captain Waring, and later the Rupeorn Grappling Corps dragged the Ship Canal with the result that the body was found near the Cantilever Bridge, at five echck yesterday aftei no n. Daring the day a letter arrived from the Ship Canal Co., granting Mr. Congreve three weeks' leave of absence. The news of Mr. 0 ongreve's laggic end will come as a painful shock to lA' any friends and great sympatiy is felt with the widow in her bereavenient. Mr. emigre's, was of an urbane disposition and a nun ef many and varied attainnkents. He had gained distinction in his profession ant, twelve months ago succeeded to the important position of chief engineer to the Ship Canal Company in place of the late Mr. Hunter. For over twenty years Mr. Congreve bad been in the service of the company, having been connected with the great l undertaking practically from its inception. Prigr toehat , be had been engaged in extensive railway week* abroad. Mr. Congreve who was fialtwee years of age, van a member of the hint to of Civil Engineers. was only in tLeapring of this year that he wena to 4 -filluppenhall, basil g removed from MOM wbir thel resided for somelyears. Tlite NC Longreve was a atauech Liberal and had often taken part in meetings under the auspices , of the Kiautaford Division Liberal Association. He , was a keen student of architecture, especially of an ecclesiastical character, and be had explored all parts of England in his quest for interesting and historic . churches. He had also travelled extensively abroad and, as he was an excellent photegrapher, he possessed a fine collection of pictorial mementoes of his wander- ings. Much of his camera work was transferred to lantern slides and Mr. Congreve never tired of deliver- I ing illustrated lectures before various societies. Last winter he addressed the Warrington Literary and Philosophical Society on "The Evolution cf the s Village Church." During his i esidence at Moore Mr. Congreve took an i ctive part in village affairs and as - , had occupied the chairmanship of the parish council. He was greatly respected by the inhabitants, was popular with all classes and creeds, and his death will be deep'y regretted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" Claudian."</p>
        <p>There is a peculiar fascination about the late Wilson Barratt's famous play " Claudian " which never fails to attract large and appreciative audiences. The ancient Roman setting is one of the chief factors in the success of the yroductinn, the paths and tragedy of the transactions in the slave market, the barbaric splendour of Court life, and the dash cf arms making a succesaion of stage pictut es of extraordinary vividness and realism. The " effects" are much above the average, the e aithquake Leing produced with remarkably thrilling and awe-insr.iring cempleteness A capable company presents the play with earnestness and skill. Mr. Maclaren achieves a triumph in the title role, his handsome presence, flexible voice, and powerful acting combining to make a perfect pot trope]. Mrs. Lily Pandmann invests the difficult part of Almida with great tenderness and charm, aid another striking emotional study is given by Miss Amy Hardc,.tle as Serena. Mr. F. Cyril ' Leighton impersonates the cruel Tetratch of Charydos in a highly realistic manner, and Mr. Basil Mitchell gives a humorous touch to the play as the bibulous Bolos. In the palace scer.e Mddle. Stella Bolin is responsible for a cymbal dance which is very grace- fully executed.</p>
        <p>" TANTALISING TOMMY." Tantalising Tommy" should be a huge attraction at the Royal Court Theatre, next week. This play comes to Wanington with a big reputation behind it. Running as it did at the Playhouse, London for over 300 nights it may with justice be desmibed as the funniest farcical comedy of modern times—adapted from the French by Michael Morton and is brimful of amusing situations. The comedy iccludes Mi 39 Mary Forrester a charming and dainty American actress. and Mr. Vivien Cilbeit. the net-known ccwidian.</p>
      </div>
