      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>LYMNI NURSES INJURY.</head>
        <p>LYMNI NURSES INJURY.</p>
        <p>Judge to Have Medical Help in Peculiar</p>
        <p>Case.</p>
        <p>Remarkable statements were made in a compensatiofl! matter which arcee at the Manchester County Crsirt last Friday, before bis Honour Judge Mellor, K.C. The applicant was Miss Emma Monroe (251, nurse of Lymm, and the reepondents were the South Manchester Guardians of the Poor. Mr. Leigh, barrister, appeared for the applicant, and Mr. Percy Jordan for the reepondents. Mr. Leigh said his client whilst employed at the Withington Workhouee Hospital scalded her right band from the effects of which ate we, still suffering. The injury was caused by the spilling of soup. The band was treated with acid, hot baths, and was twice lanced. She was afterwards taken home by her mother and sent to the Liverpool Infirmary. The hand was not better, and she received a month's notice frcm the Ousrdians. She left on May 28th. and compensation was chimed from that date. The bond was swollen, and while theru was movement of the thumb there was no movement of the fingers. Mr Jordan mid it was possible the applicant bad done something which had reaulted in the state of the hand to-day while aoffering from hysteria. Ile was not there to suggest that the applicant had done anything for the purpose of getting money. They did not want to adopt that attitude. Ills Honour said it was evidently a very peculiar case, and he thought he should have the attendance of a medical referee. _ . . . .</p>
        <p>lbe case was adjourned until October 13tb, when the Judge will he Accompanied by a medical messes.</p>
      </div>
