      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>INSURANCE BILL AND INFIRMARIES. Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Receives a Deputation.</head>
        <p>INSURANCE BILL AND INFIRMARIES. Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Receives a Deputation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ifarold Smith, yesterday received a deputa- Gen from the Board of Management of the Warrington Infirmary, which placed before him "the grave appre- hension felt by the Board as to the probable effect of the proposed Insurance Bill upon thdfinancial position." The meeting took place at the kfirmary under the chairmanship of Mel W. Peter Ryland. There were also present ;—lifir George Gelds (hon. treisurer), Captain Crostield, Alderman Burton (cha'rman of the Workingmen's Committee), Messrs John Fairclough, J H Smethurst, and J H J Hampson (secretary). Mr. Itylands aaid the poaition of Infirmaries under the new Bill was calculated to become intolerable. The income of that institution was approximately £l,OO a year. Of that, from £1,'200 to £1,500 vas contributed by the workingmen ; £1,250 was raised in annual sub- scriptions ; £l,OOO was derived from income on invest- ments ; and the balance came from special efforts. The Board were afraid that the contributions from workmen and employers would be seriously diminished, if not extinguished, if they bad to meet compulsory levies under toe Bill. After reviewing ether ways in ahich the Bill might harmfully effect voluntary hospitals, Mr. Rylands said the Medical Board had passed a resolution that they would probably be unable to gone gratuitous attend- ance to insured persons in the event of the Bill becoming Gw. . . .</p>
        <p>Alderman 13CRT033 reported that the Workingmen's Committee had passed a resolution" that in the evlnt of the National Insurstce Bill, as at present con. stituted, becoming law, it is the opinion of this meeting that the contributions from the .orkpeople o! the town and neighbourhood will be consideratly reduced." Mr. J. 11. SMETHURsT who, along with Csptain Croslield,has attended conferences on the Bill, reported that the e was a feeling among Lancashire hospital authorities Hitt infirm tries should be treated on the same footing as sanatoris and receive State assistance. Mr. RYLAND!, who confessed that he took a pessimistic view of the situation, suggested es the only remedy that insured petients must be paid for, like ordinary private ',attests would have to pay. If the State undertook that charge then the Warrington Infirmary would require £l,foo a year and the cost thee ughont the kingdom would probably approach E1,000,C00. Mr. HAROLD SMITH, M.P., said that not only did he 'veilse the danger with whiob the infirmaries were threatened and the seriousness of their position, but he did not see a solution of it. Ile bad listened to the debates on the Bill but bad never beard a setisfictory answer to the arguments raised on behalf of the infirmaries. He was satisfied that the e Mont upon voluttary hospitals would be so serious that they would not recover from the bl,w. The 5.11 had in it the makings of a greet measure. It was, however. too big and complex ; it trod on too many corns and for that reason it was unworkable In its present state. On first perusing the Bill, he concluded that it tended in a large measure to regenerate British people in the years to come. Having listened to the 08803 for the different interest.. which eould be injured, be could not help thinking tbst it would be wise to withdraw the measure end re-introduce it in two portions, allowing a longer time for its discussion. Mr. Lloyd-George was</p>
      </div>
