      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>GLOBE</head>
        <p>GLOBE</p>
        <p>Furnishing Co.</p>
        <p>(1 12 GRA, PEMBROKE I junrnool PLAOE,</p>
        <p>TRAP FATALITY.</p>
        <p>Warrington Man Killed. A fatal trap accident occurred at Go'borne on Sunday last, when William Cork (24), of Town Hill, Warring- ton, end Robert Pope, of Earlestown, were killed, and John Woods, of Winavick-road,Warrington, was seriously injured. Woods was conveyed to the Wigan Infirmary. and is still in a critical condition. On Wednesday the Wigan Coroner, Mr. 11. Milligan, held an inquiry into the death of Cork. It appeved that the deceased along with Robert Pope, a shoesmith. of Princess-street, Earlestown, rod John Woody, a wire worker of Winwick-street, Warrington, went for a drive in Pope's tra:‘. The horse b cone restive and ultimately collided with a lamp-post in Edge Green-lane. The occupants of the trap were thrown out, the Oat% were broken and the horse bolted. Pope died within a few m'nutes,: his injuries being about the head. Cork Wes conveyed to the Wigan Infirmary. Alezander Jenkinson stated that he saw the trap going past his house Its terrible rate, and he went out thinking on accident would happen an there was dangerous curve just at that point. He found the three men lathe roadway, lying inns unconsoicors state. The Coroner said the only men who could throw soy light upon the accident was Woods. Police-Constable- Whittle had seen him at the Infirmary, and he had said that the borne stumbled on some storm setts and. bolted. A verdict of" Accidental death " wan tett:row-I.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>PERFECTION BAND'S VICTORY.</head>
        <p>PERFECTION BAND'S VICTORY.</p>
        <p>(Continued from Pitge 4). CONGRATULATIONS TO THE VICTORS.</p>
        <p>Since their great victory the members of the Perfection Soap Wcrks band have been overwhelmed with congratulations from all parts of the country. Mr. A. H. Crcsfield, who has tikea the keenest personal interest in the bard from its inception, wired to the secretary (Mr. W. Taylor) Immensely pleased to bear of band's great triumph. Thousand congratulations to yourself and every member on this magnificent victory." Mr. F. J. Lord, of Christ Chutch, Bournemouth (formerly of Worrington) wrote : "1 (Her you heartiest congratulations, one and all, on the glorious result. The pinnacle at last, and in a comparatively few years. It is fine ! What a home-coming you. must have had. I should have enjoyed being there. I think everyone will now feel repaid for the asperse and hard work of the past years.' .</p>
        <p>r Amongst others sending mei, algae were Mrs. John Crosfield (Colwyn Bay) and Alderman C. Darien (Chester).</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Nuttall, the secretary of the Iratell Springs (Bacup) band who have twies won the championship, writea as follows "Dear Mr. Taylor, —All rtv me on behalf of our band and myself to expand to you our congratulations on your success on SaturdAy last at the Crystal Palace contest. Your tiands performance was an excellent one and tidily &amp;served the victorT, anal! mart admit who had the pleasure of hearing the performance</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Warrington !hotograpble.Sociaty:</head>
        <p>Warrington !hotograpble.Sociaty:</p>
        <p>An e xcelleai send oft was accorded to the new Session on Tuesday evening, October 3rd, at Atkinson's GeV. Sixty-three members and friend-n eat down to whist, the dummy hand being saved by the genial howess herself. The prizes, worthily so-named, were framed photographs by some of thor best pictorial workers among our own members and lecturers, the one chrsen as the first by Mrs. Rowlands, who headed the ladies scores, bring "Russet Apples," by Mr. E. Seymour of London, one of the first of our present-day still life artists. The M.C.'s duties were taken by the hon. treasurer. Supper was excellently served in the Caf4, and dancing followed till one o'clock. The po,ition of M.C. for this part of the evening was admirably filled by one of the Society's Hon. Curator&amp; Such a successful start V) the winter's programme augurs a good series of meetings at the Academy which the Committee and officers not only hope for, but have striven very bard to ensure. • A Motor Engineer and His Wife. . _ .</p>
        <p>At the Bcrough Podce Court yestcrday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, Ann Broady, at present residing at 104 i, Macchester--road, summoned her husband. George Brcarly, a motor engiiirer employei by a firm of motor engineers at Altrinchani, f r per- statent cruelty and asked for a separation crder. Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for the complainant and stated that the defendant only came home at the week-en Is. Ever since their marnage in 1901 the defendant had been is the habit of setting drank and ill-treating Ws wife and quoted various alleged acts of cruelty.—Mr. Highion, of Abrincham, alto appeared for the defen- dant, gave a general denial of the charges. After a lengthy hearing the Bench granted a s-pnratioa order with Pit. a week and nests, the complainant to have the custody of the two children.</p>
      </div>
